var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"#EuclideanGeometry.jl","page":"Home","text":"Documentation for EuclideanGeometry.jl, a Julia toolkit for planar Euclidean geometry — points, segments, lines, rays, circles and triangles, plus the constructions you build with them (midpoints, intersections, projections, reflections, rotations, triangle centers, tangency, conics...).\n\nEvery exported type is its own struct, prefixed EG (EGPoint, EGCircle2, EGTriangle, ...) and organized into a real abstract type hierarchy — no external geometry dependency, and genuine \"is-a\" relationships instead of loose, unrelated structs:\n\nEGObject{Dim,T}\n├── EGLocus{Dim,T}\n│   ├── EGCurve{Dim,T}\n│   │   ├── EGLine{Dim,T}\n│   │   ├── EGRay{Dim,T}\n│   │   ├── EGSegment{Dim,T}\n│   │   ├── EGConic2{T}                       -- circle, ellipse, parabola, hyperbola\n│   │   │   ├── EGCircle2{T}\n│   │   │   ├── EGEllipse2{T}\n│   │   │   ├── EGParabola2{T}\n│   │   │   └── EGHyperbola2{T}\n│   │   └── EGConicArc2{T}                    -- a bounded piece of one of the conics above\n│   │       ├── EGCircularArc2{T}\n│   │       ├── EGEllipticArc2{T}\n│   │       ├── EGParabolicArc2{T}\n│   │       └── EGHyperbolicArc2{T}\n│   └── EGSet{Dim,T}\n│       ├── EGHalfPlane2{T}\n│       ├── EGAngle2{T}\n│       ├── EGStrip2{T}\n│       └── EGRegion{Dim,T}\n│           └── EGPolygon{Dim,T}\n│               ├── EGTriangle{Dim,T}\n│               ├── EGQuadrilateral{Dim,T}\n│               ├── EGStraightNgon{Dim,T}\n│               ├── EGCircularSector2{T}\n│               ├── EGCircularSegment2{T}\n│               ├── EGAnnularSector2{T}\n│               ├── EGInterstice2{T}\n│               ├── EGCurvilinearTriangle2{T}\n│               ├── EGCurvilinearQuadrilateral2{T}\n│               └── EGCurvilinearNgon2{T}\n├── EGPoint{Dim,T}\n├── EGVector{Dim,T}\n└── EGBoundingBox{Dim,T}\n\nEGTransform{T}\n└── EGAffineMap{T}\n\nWhy a custom type hierarchy at all, rather than building on an existing geometry package: two concrete problems, both hit in practice while this package still built on GeometryBasics.jl.\n\nNaming collisions. GeometryBasics.jl exports Point, Circle, Triangle, Polygon, BoundingBox... — names that also collide with Luxor.jl's own Point, Circle, BoundingBox, etc. Since drawing (the main reason to load both packages together) needs exactly that combination, every call needed explicit qualification to say which package's type was meant. Every type here is prefixed EG, so it never collides with anything.\nNo real \"is-a\" relationships. With loose, unrelated structs, a Triangle couldn't be treated as a Polygon even though it obviously is one — so shared logic (area, perimeter, centroid, ...) had to be reimplemented separately per shape, and a function that legitimately wants \"any closed region\" had no single type to accept. With a genuine abstract hierarchy — every closed shape really <: EGPolygon — that logic is written once, generically, via sides() and a shared Green's-theorem line integral; a brand new shape (EGCurvilinearNgon2, EGAnnularSector2, ...) gets area/perimeter/centroid/is_convex/ point_in_polygon for free just by implementing sides().\n\nThe practical upshot: EGTriangle, EGQuadrilateral, EGCircularSector2 and every other closed shape genuinely is an EGPolygon, so area/perimeter/centroid/is_convex/point_in_polygon are defined once, generically — not reimplemented per type. See each abstract type's own docstring (EGObject, EGLocus, EGCurve, EGSet, EGRegion, EGPolygon, EGTransform) for what belongs where and why.\n\nSee the README for the full feature overview, and the API Reference for every exported function and type. For a guided tour with explanations and worked examples, start with:\n\nPoints, Lines & Rays\nCircles\nTriangles & Triangle Centers\nTangency & Apollonius Problems\nPolygons & Bounding Boxes\nConics: Ellipse, Parabola & Hyperbola\nAffine Maps","title":"EuclideanGeometry.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Quick-example","page":"Home","text":"using EuclideanGeometry\n\na, b, c = EGPoint(0.0, 0.0), EGPoint(4.0, 0.0), EGPoint(0.0, 3.0)\nt = EGTriangle(a, b, c)\n\ncentroid(t)        # center of mass\ncircumcircle(t)    # circle through a, b, c\nincenter(t)        # center of the inscribed circle\n\nl = EGLine(a, b)\nperpendicular_through(l, c)   # altitude from c\nintersection(l, circumcircle(t))","title":"Quick example"},{"category":"section","location":"#Drawing-with-Luxor.jl-(experimental)","page":"Home","text":"This package doesn't depend on Luxor.jl, but if you load both, a path function becomes available for every type here, via a package extension — this is new and not yet a stable, finished part of the API. See Drawing with Luxor.jl at the end of this site for the full rundown.","title":"Drawing with Luxor.jl (experimental)"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#API-Reference","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"API Reference"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.EGAffineMap","page":"API Reference","text":"EGAffineMap(a11, a12, a21, a22, tx, ty)\n\nThe affine map p -> [a11 a12; a21 a22] * p + (tx, ty). Instances are callable: m(p) applies the map to a point, and pointwise to every EG-typed curve/polygon defined so far.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.EGAffineMap"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.EGAffineMap-Tuple{EGCircle2}","page":"API Reference","text":"(m::EGAffineMap)(c::EGCircle2)\n\nThe image of c under m: in general an EGEllipse2 (a circle is only mapped to another circle by the conformal affine maps — a rotation, translation, or uniform scaling — and this covers all affine maps, so it always returns an EGEllipse2, never an EGCircle2, even when m happens to be conformal).\n\nThe linear part of m maps the unit circle to an ellipse whose semi-axes are its singular values and whose axes are its left singular vectors; c's own radius scales those semi-axes, and its center maps pointwise.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.EGAffineMap"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.EGAffineMap-Tuple{EGCircularArc2}","page":"API Reference","text":"(m::EGAffineMap)(arc::EGCircularArc2)\n\nThe image of arc under m — an EGEllipticArc2, not another EGCircularArc2 (matching (m::EGAffineMap)(c::EGCircle2)'s convention: a general affine map turns a circle into an ellipse).\n\nLike reflection(::EGCircularArc2, ::EGLine), this needs to know whether m preserves or reverses orientation: for a closed conic, \"the arc from p1 to p2\" only picks out one of the two complementary arcs because it's understood to sweep counterclockwise, and an orientation-reversing map (det(m) < 0, like a reflection) turns that sweep clockwise — so p1/p2 are swapped in that case to reconstruct the correct (not the complementary) arc.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.EGAffineMap"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.EGAffineMap-Tuple{EGCircularSector2}","page":"API Reference","text":"(m::EGAffineMap)(s::EGCircularSector2)\n(m::EGAffineMap)(s::EGCircularSegment2)\n(m::EGAffineMap)(s::EGAnnularSector2)\n(m::EGAffineMap)(g::EGInterstice2)\n\nThe image under m of a region built from circular arcs. None of these four types are closed under a general affine map — their circular arcs generically become elliptic ones (see (m::EGAffineMap)(arc::EGCircularArc2) above), which no longer fits an EGCircularSector2/EGCircularSegment2/EGAnnularSector2/ EGInterstice2 (each holds a concrete EGCircularArc2 field, not any conic arc) — so the result is the more general EGCurvilinearTriangle2/EGCurvilinearQuadrilateral2/ EGCurvilinearNgon2 with the same sides, each mapped through m (reusing sides rather than each type's own fields, so this automatically stays correct if those ever change).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.EGAffineMap"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.EGAffineMap-Tuple{EGCurvilinearTriangle2}","page":"API Reference","text":"(m::EGAffineMap)(t::EGCurvilinearTriangle2)\n(m::EGAffineMap)(q::EGCurvilinearQuadrilateral2)\n(m::EGAffineMap)(pg::EGCurvilinearNgon2)\n\nThe image under m of a general mixed-side region — the same type, each side mapped through m (every side type — EGSegment and all four conic arcs — already knows how to transform itself under m).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.EGAffineMap"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.EGAffineMap-Tuple{EGEllipse2}","page":"API Reference","text":"(m::EGAffineMap)(e::EGEllipse2)\n\nThe image of e under m — always another EGEllipse2 (never a circle, even if e happens to be one and m conformal — same convention as (m::EGAffineMap)(c::EGCircle2) above).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.EGAffineMap"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.EGAffineMap-Tuple{EGEllipticArc2}","page":"API Reference","text":"(m::EGAffineMap)(arc::EGEllipticArc2)\n\nThe image of arc under m — another EGEllipticArc2, on the image of its ellipse (an affine invariant, see (m::EGAffineMap)(e::EGEllipse2) above). Swaps p1/p2 when m is orientation-reversing, for the same reason as (m::EGAffineMap)(arc::EGCircularArc2) above.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.EGAffineMap"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.EGAffineMap-Tuple{EGHalfPlane2}","page":"API Reference","text":"(m::EGAffineMap)(hp::EGHalfPlane2)\n\nThe image of hp under m. Unlike rotate/homothety (always orientation-preserving in 2D, so side never needs to change), a general affine map can reverse orientation (det(m) < 0, like a reflection), which would flip which side of the transformed boundary is \"inside\" — so side is recomputed fresh from a point already known to be on the correct side, rather than carried over unchanged.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.EGAffineMap"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.EGAffineMap-Tuple{EGHyperbola2}","page":"API Reference","text":"(m::EGAffineMap)(h::EGHyperbola2)\n\nThe image of h under m — always another EGHyperbola2.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.EGAffineMap"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.EGAffineMap-Tuple{EGHyperbolicArc2}","page":"API Reference","text":"(m::EGAffineMap)(arc::EGHyperbolicArc2)\n(m::EGAffineMap)(arc::EGParabolicArc2)\n\nThe image of arc under m — the same arc type, on the image of its underlying conic. Unlike the closed-conic arcs above, a single hyperbola branch/a parabola is open, so there's no complementary-arc ambiguity and no swap is needed even when m reverses orientation (matching reflection(::EGHyperbolicArc2, _)/reflection(::EGParabolicArc2, _)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.EGAffineMap"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.EGAffineMap-Tuple{EGParabola2}","page":"API Reference","text":"(m::EGAffineMap)(par::EGParabola2)\n\nThe image of par under m — always another EGParabola2. Unlike (m::EGAffineMap)(e::EGEllipse2)/(m::EGAffineMap)(h::EGHyperbola2) above, focus/directrix are metric (not affine-invariant) constructs, so they can't just be mapped pointwise like a segment's endpoints — m can shear or scale non-uniformly, which moves the true focus/directrix off of m(par.focus)/m(par.directrix). Instead: par's own parametrization point_on_parabola(par, y) = vertex + (y²/2pf)u + y·w becomes, under m, q(y) = m(vertex) + y·m(w) + y²·(m(u)/2pf) — a quadratic vector function of y whose own vertex/axis/focal parameter are recovered by rewriting it in the orthonormal frame aligned with its (generally skewed) y² coefficient direction, then completing the square.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.EGAffineMap"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.EGAffineMap-Tuple{EGStrip2}","page":"API Reference","text":"(m::EGAffineMap)(s::EGStrip2)\n\nThe image of s under m. Affine maps preserve parallelism, so the two transformed boundary lines are still parallel.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.EGAffineMap"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.EGAffineMap-Tuple{EGVector{2}}","page":"API Reference","text":"(m::EGAffineMap)(v::EGVector)\n\nThe image of the free vector v under m's linear part only — a vector has no position, so the translation (tx, ty) is not applied.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.EGAffineMap"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.EGAngle2","page":"API Reference","text":"EGAngle2(vertex::EGPoint, a::EGPoint, b::EGPoint)\n\nThe angle at vertex between the rays vertex -> a and vertex -> b, plus (since it's an EGSet) the infinite wedge they bound — the region swept counterclockwise from ray vertex -> a to ray vertex -> b by normalized_measure radians. Unlike angle_at/ angle_between, which just return a number, this keeps the three defining points around.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.EGAngle2"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.EGAnnularSector2","page":"API Reference","text":"EGAnnularSector2(outer::EGCircularArc2, r_inner::Real)\n\nThe region between two concentric arcs: outer (on the outer circle, outer.circle.r > r_inner) and the corresponding arc of the concentric circle of radius r_inner, closed by the two radial segments between them — Luxor's own sector(center, innerradius, outerradius, ...) shape, represented here as an EGPolygon.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.EGAnnularSector2"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.EGBoundingBox","page":"API Reference","text":"EGBoundingBox(min::EGPoint, max::EGPoint)\n\nAn axis-aligned bounding box. Deliberately not part of the EGRegion hierarchy and has no rotate/reflection methods: an arbitrary rotation or reflection wouldn't generally produce another axis-aligned box. Translation (+/-) and uniform scaling about the origin (*) are the only transforms that always keep it axis-aligned, so those are what's supported.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.EGBoundingBox"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.EGBoundingBox-Tuple{EGCircularArc2}","page":"API Reference","text":"EGBoundingBox(arc::EGCircularArc2)\n\nThe axis-aligned bounding box of arc itself (not the full circle): exact, from arc.p1/arc.p2 plus whichever of the circle's own axis-extreme points (angle 0, π/2, π, 3π/2) fall within arc's own angular sweep.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.EGBoundingBox"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.EGBoundingBox-Tuple{EGEllipse2}","page":"API Reference","text":"EGBoundingBox(e::EGEllipse2)\n\nThe axis-aligned bounding box of e: exact, via the standard closed form for a rotated ellipse's extent, half-width = √(a²cos²φ + b²sin²φ), half-height = √(a²sin²φ + b²cos²φ).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.EGBoundingBox"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.EGBoundingBox-Tuple{EGEllipticArc2}","page":"API Reference","text":"EGBoundingBox(arc::EGEllipticArc2)\n\nThe axis-aligned bounding box of arc itself (not the full ellipse): exact, from arc.p1/arc.p2 plus whichever of the ellipse's own axis-extreme points fall within arc's own angular sweep (the same critical angles used implicitly by EGBoundingBox(::EGEllipse2), found here from x(θ) = cx + A cosθ + B sinθ/y(θ) = cy + C cosθ + D sinθ, each an unrotated sinusoid in θ whose own extrema are at θ = atan(B,A)/ atan(D,C) and their +π counterparts).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.EGBoundingBox"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.EGBoundingBox-Tuple{EGHyperbolicArc2}","page":"API Reference","text":"EGBoundingBox(arc::EGHyperbolicArc2)\n\nThe axis-aligned bounding box of arc: exact, from arc.p1/arc.p2 plus the critical points of x(t) = cx + P·cosh(t) + Q·sinh(t)/ y(t) = cy + R·cosh(t) + S·sinh(t) (each solved via _hyperbola_axis_critical_t) that fall within arc's own parameter range.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.EGBoundingBox"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.EGBoundingBox-Tuple{EGParabolicArc2}","page":"API Reference","text":"EGBoundingBox(arc::EGParabolicArc2)\n\nThe axis-aligned bounding box of arc: exact, from arc.p1/arc.p2 plus the critical points of x(s) = Vx + (s²/2pf)u₁ + s·w₁/ y(s) = Vy + (s²/2pf)u₂ + s·w₂ (each a plain parabola in the scalar parameter s, critical at s = -pf·w/u for its own u/w component) that fall within arc's own parameter range.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.EGBoundingBox"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.EGBoundingBox-Tuple{Union{EGAnnularSector2, EGCircularSector2, EGCircularSegment2, EGCurvilinearNgon2, EGCurvilinearQuadrilateral2, EGCurvilinearTriangle2, EGInterstice2}}","page":"API Reference","text":"EGBoundingBox(pg::EGPolygon)\n\nThe axis-aligned bounding box of pg. The generic EGBoundingBox(::EGPolygon) in eg_polygon.jl (via vertices) doesn't apply to any curved-sided region — EGCircularSector2, EGCircularSegment2, EGAnnularSector2, EGInterstice2, EGCurvilinearTriangle2, EGCurvilinearQuadrilateral2, EGCurvilinearNgon2 don't implement vertices — so this instead unions the bounding box of each of pg's own sides (every side type, straight or any conic arc, already knows its own bounding box).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.EGBoundingBox"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.EGCircle2","page":"API Reference","text":"EGCircle2(center, r)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.EGCircle2"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.EGCircularArc2","page":"API Reference","text":"EGCircularArc2(circle::EGCircle2, p1::EGPoint, p2::EGPoint)\n\nThe arc of circle traversed counterclockwise from p1 to p2.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.EGCircularArc2"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.EGCircularSector2","page":"API Reference","text":"EGCircularSector2(arc::EGCircularArc2)\nEGCircularSector2(circle::EGCircle2, p1::EGPoint, p2::EGPoint)\n\nThe \"pie slice\" swept by arc: bounded by the two radii circle.center -> p1, circle.center -> p2, and the arc itself.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.EGCircularSector2"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.EGCircularSegment2","page":"API Reference","text":"EGCircularSegment2(arc::EGCircularArc2)\nEGCircularSegment2(circle::EGCircle2, p1::EGPoint, p2::EGPoint)\n\nThe \"cap\" bounded by arc and the straight chord [p1, p2].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.EGCircularSegment2"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.EGConic2","page":"API Reference","text":"EGConic2{T} <: EGCurve{2,T}\n\nThe parent of the four 2D conic types: EGCircle2, EGEllipse2, EGParabola2, EGHyperbola2. EGCircle2 is a sibling here, not a subtype of EGEllipse2 — despite being the degenerate a == b case mathematically, it's a genuinely different (simpler, one-parameter) struct rather than an EGEllipse2 with equal axes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.EGConic2"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.EGConicArc2","page":"API Reference","text":"EGConicArc2{T} <: EGCurve{2,T}\n\nThe parent of the four conic-arc types, each a bounded piece of the matching EGConic2: EGCircularArc2, EGEllipticArc2, EGParabolicArc2, EGHyperbolicArc2. For the two closed conics (circle, ellipse) two points leave a \"which way around\" ambiguity, so reflection about an EGLine swaps the arc's endpoints to stay a true mirror image rather than jumping to the complementary arc; the two open curves (parabola, a single hyperbola branch) have no such ambiguity, so their reflection never swaps.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.EGConicArc2"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.EGCurve","page":"API Reference","text":"EGCurve{Dim,T} <: EGLocus{Dim,T}\n\nA one-dimensional locus: EGLine, EGRay, EGSegment, the conics (EGCircle2, EGEllipse2, EGParabola2, EGHyperbola2, grouped under EGConic2), and the conic arcs (EGCircularArc2 and friends, grouped under EGConicArc2). Has no interior — it bounds a region rather than being one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.EGCurve"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.EGCurvilinearNgon2","page":"API Reference","text":"EGCurvilinearNgon2(sides::AbstractVector)\n\nA closed region bounded by any number of sides, each an EGSegment or an EGCircularArc2 — the fully general case of the curved EGPolygon family.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.EGCurvilinearNgon2"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.EGCurvilinearQuadrilateral2","page":"API Reference","text":"EGCurvilinearQuadrilateral2(side1, side2, side3, side4)\n\nThe 4-sided analogue of EGCurvilinearTriangle2.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.EGCurvilinearQuadrilateral2"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.EGCurvilinearTriangle2","page":"API Reference","text":"EGCurvilinearTriangle2(side1, side2, side3)\n\nA closed region bounded by 3 sides, each independently an EGSegment or a conic arc (EGCircularArc2, EGEllipticArc2, EGParabolicArc2, EGHyperbolicArc2) — the general 3-sided analogue of EGTriangle (all straight) and EGInterstice2 (all circular arcs). The wider arc coverage (beyond just circular) exists mainly so that EGAffineMap-transformed circular-arc regions — whose arcs generically become elliptic under a non-conformal map — still fit.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.EGCurvilinearTriangle2"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.EGEllipse2","page":"API Reference","text":"EGEllipse2(center, a, b, angle=0.0)\nEGEllipse2(f1::EGPoint, f2::EGPoint, a::Real)\nEGEllipse2(f1::EGPoint, f2::EGPoint, p::EGPoint)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.EGEllipse2"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.EGEllipticArc2","page":"API Reference","text":"EGEllipticArc2(ellipse::EGEllipse2, p1::EGPoint, p2::EGPoint)\n\nThe arc of ellipse traversed counterclockwise (in the ellipse's own parametrization) from p1 to p2 — the ellipse analogue of EGCircularArc2, same swept/complementary-arc convention.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.EGEllipticArc2"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.EGHalfPlane2","page":"API Reference","text":"EGHalfPlane2(boundary::EGLine, side::Int)\nEGHalfPlane2(boundary::EGLine, interior_point::EGPoint)\n\nThe closed half-plane bounded by boundary: side = +1 means the side left of boundary (oriented p1 -> p2), side = -1 means the right side — see side_of_line's convention, which this mirrors. The second form is often easier to reason about: pass any point known to lie inside instead of working out left/right by hand.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.EGHalfPlane2"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.EGHyperbola2","page":"API Reference","text":"EGHyperbola2(center, a, b, angle=0.0)\nEGHyperbola2(f1::EGPoint, f2::EGPoint, a::Real)\nEGHyperbola2(f1::EGPoint, f2::EGPoint, p::EGPoint)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.EGHyperbola2"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.EGHyperbolicArc2","page":"API Reference","text":"EGHyperbolicArc2(hyperbola::EGHyperbola2, p1::EGPoint, p2::EGPoint)\n\nThe arc of hyperbola between p1 and p2, assumed to lie on the same branch. Like EGParabolicArc2 (and unlike the closed conics), a single branch of a hyperbola is open, so reflection never needs to swap p1/p2.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.EGHyperbolicArc2"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.EGInterstice2","page":"API Reference","text":"EGInterstice2(arc1::EGCircularArc2, arc2::EGCircularArc2, arc3::EGCircularArc2)\n\nA curvilinear triangle bounded by three circular arcs, each from a different circle, meeting end to end. Build with interstices (deferred to Phase 7 — needs tangent_circles, not yet ported) rather than directly, in the common case of 3 mutually tangent circles.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.EGInterstice2"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.EGLine","page":"API Reference","text":"EGLine(p1::EGPoint, p2::EGPoint)\n\nThe infinite straight line passing through p1 and p2.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.EGLine"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.EGLocus","page":"API Reference","text":"EGLocus{Dim,T} <: EGObject{Dim,T}\n\nAnything definable as the set of points satisfying some geometric condition — the parent of both EGCurve (1-dimensional loci: lines, conics, arcs) and EGSet (regions with interior area: half-planes, angles, polygons). EGPoint/EGVector and EGBoundingBox sit outside this branch — a single point isn't usefully a \"locus\", and a bounding box is an axis-aligned convenience object rather than a rotation/reflection-covariant one (see its own docstring for why).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.EGLocus"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.EGObject","page":"API Reference","text":"EGObject{Dim,T}\n\nThe root of every type this package defines: anything that lives in Dim-dimensional space with coordinates of element type T. Every concrete EG-prefixed type — points, vectors, curves, regions, bounding boxes — is an EGObject. EGTransform (affine maps and friends) is deliberately not part of this tree: a transform isn't itself a geometric object, it's a function between them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.EGObject"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.EGParabola2","page":"API Reference","text":"EGParabola2(focus::EGPoint, directrix::EGLine)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.EGParabola2"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.EGParabolicArc2","page":"API Reference","text":"EGParabolicArc2(parabola::EGParabola2, p1::EGPoint, p2::EGPoint)\n\nThe arc of parabola between p1 and p2. Unlike a circle/ellipse (a closed curve, where two points leave the \"which way around\" arc ambiguous), a parabola is open — two points on it always determine a single, unambiguous arc, with no complementary alternative — so, unlike EGCircularArc2/EGEllipticArc2, reflection never needs to swap p1/p2.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.EGParabolicArc2"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.EGPoint","page":"API Reference","text":"EGPoint(x, y)\nEGPoint(x, y, z)\nEGPoint(coords::NTuple)\n\nA point in Dim-dimensional space (Dim inferred from how many coordinates you give it). Supports +, -, unary -, scalar *//, indexing (p[i]), destructuring iteration (x, y = p), ==, isapprox, and the standard linear-algebra functions dot/norm/normalize.\n\nArithmetic between two EGPoints is deliberately permissive — p1 + p2, p1 - p2 and k * p all return another EGPoint, not an EGVector: this mirrors how points have always been used throughout this package (as both locations and free vectors at once), so existing formulas keep working unchanged. EGVector exists only for when you explicitly want a type that reads as \"this is a direction, not a location\" — convert either way with EGVector(p)/EGPoint(v).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.EGPoint"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.EGPolygon","page":"API Reference","text":"EGPolygon{Dim,T} <: EGRegion{Dim,T}\n\nA closed region bounded by an ordered sequence of sides — implements either vertices (straight-sided: EGTriangle, EGQuadrilateral, EGStraightNgon) or sides directly (mixing straight EGSegments with EGCircularArc2s: EGCircularSector2, EGCircularSegment2, EGAnnularSector2, EGInterstice2, and the EGCurvilinear*2 family). Either way, area/perimeter/centroid/is_convex/ point_in_polygon are defined once, generically, on EGPolygon itself via a shared Green's-theorem walk over sides(p) — a straight side's contribution reduces exactly to the familiar shoelace term, so this is one formula covering the entire polygon family, curved or not.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.EGPolygon"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.EGQuadrilateral","page":"API Reference","text":"EGQuadrilateral(a, b, c, d)\n\nConsecutive vertices a, b, c, d — not assumed convex, cyclic, or of any special kind.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.EGQuadrilateral"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.EGRay","page":"API Reference","text":"EGRay(origin::EGPoint, through::EGPoint)\n\nThe half-line starting at origin and passing through through.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.EGRay"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.EGRegion","page":"API Reference","text":"EGRegion{Dim,T} <: EGSet{Dim,T}\n\nAn EGSet with finite extent — as opposed to EGHalfPlane2/EGAngle2/EGStrip2, which stretch to infinity. In practice, every concrete EGRegion is also an EGPolygon (a closed boundary made of straight or curved sides).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.EGRegion"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.EGSegment","page":"API Reference","text":"EGSegment(p1::EGPoint, p2::EGPoint)\n\nThe finite segment [p1, p2]. s[1]/s[2] access its two endpoints.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.EGSegment"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.EGSet","page":"API Reference","text":"EGSet{Dim,T} <: EGLocus{Dim,T}\n\nA locus with a genuine interior/exterior — p in s is meaningful. Covers both the unbounded members (EGHalfPlane2, EGAngle2, EGStrip2 — regions with infinite extent) and every bounded EGRegion/EGPolygon. Grouping unbounded and bounded regions under one parent reflects that both answer the same basic question (is this point inside?) even though only the bounded ones have a finite area/perimeter.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.EGSet"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.EGStraightNgon","page":"API Reference","text":"EGStraightNgon(vertices::AbstractVector{<:EGPoint})\n\nA simple polygon with an arbitrary number of straight sides (assumed simple — edges don't cross themselves), given as an ordered list of vertices (no repeated closing point). The straight-sided analogue of EGCurvilinearNgon2.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.EGStraightNgon"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.EGStrip2","page":"API Reference","text":"EGStrip2(line1::EGLine, line2::EGLine; atol=1e-9)\n\nThe closed band between two parallel lines line1/line2 (either boundary counts as inside). Throws ArgumentError if the lines aren't parallel.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.EGStrip2"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.EGTransform","page":"API Reference","text":"EGTransform{T}\n\nThe parent of EGAffineMap and any future transform type — a function between geometric objects, rather than one itself, which is why EGTransform sits outside the EGObject tree entirely.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.EGTransform"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.EGTriangle","page":"API Reference","text":"EGTriangle(a, b, c)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.EGTriangle"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.EGVector","page":"API Reference","text":"EGVector(x, y)\nEGVector(x, y, z)\nEGVector(coords::NTuple)\nEGVector(p::EGPoint)\n\nA direction/displacement in Dim-dimensional space — the same underlying representation as EGPoint, kept as a distinct type purely so a signature can say \"this is a direction\" (and so norm/dot/normalize read naturally). Supports the same operations as EGPoint; EGPoint(v) converts back the other way.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.EGVector"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Base.abs-Tuple{EGAngle2}","page":"API Reference","text":"abs(ang::EGAngle2)\n\nThe unsigned measure of ang (radians, in [0, π]) — see angle_at.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.abs"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Base.in-Tuple{EGPoint, EGAngle2}","page":"API Reference","text":"p in ang::EGAngle2\n\nWhether p lies in the infinite wedge swept counterclockwise from ray vertex -> a to ray vertex -> b (see EGAngle2).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.in"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Base.in-Tuple{EGPoint, EGHalfPlane2}","page":"API Reference","text":"p in hp::EGHalfPlane2\n\nWhether p lies in the closed half-plane hp (on hp.boundary counts as inside).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.in"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Base.in-Tuple{EGPoint, EGStrip2}","page":"API Reference","text":"p in s::EGStrip2\n\nWhether p lies in the closed band between s.line1 and s.line2.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.in"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Base.in-Union{Tuple{Dim}, Tuple{EGPoint{Dim}, EGBoundingBox{Dim}}} where Dim","page":"API Reference","text":"p in bb::EGBoundingBox\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.in"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry._rotate_toward-Tuple{EGPoint, EGPoint, Real, EGPoint}","page":"API Reference","text":"_rotate_toward(p, vertex, angle, ref)\n_rotate_away(p, vertex, angle, ref)\n\nRotate p around vertex by angle (radians), choosing the sign (angle or -angle) that lands on the same side of line (vertex, p) as ref (_rotate_toward) or on the opposite side (_rotate_away).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry._rotate_toward"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry._solve_quadratic-Tuple{Real, Real, Real}","page":"API Reference","text":"_solve_quadratic(a, b, c; atol=1e-9)\n\nReal roots of a*t^2 + b*t + c = 0, as a Vector{Float64} with 0, 1 or 2 elements (falls back to the linear/constant cases when a ≈ 0).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry._solve_quadratic"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.affine_map-Tuple{Tuple{var\"#s230\", var\"#s230\", var\"#s230\"} where var\"#s230\"<:(EGPoint{2}), Tuple{var\"#s231\", var\"#s231\", var\"#s231\"} where var\"#s231\"<:(EGPoint{2})}","page":"API Reference","text":"affine_map(src::NTuple{3,EGPoint}, dst::NTuple{3,EGPoint})\n\nThe unique affine map sending src[i] to dst[i] for i = 1, 2, 3. src must be non-collinear.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.affine_map"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.altitude-Tuple{EGTriangle, Integer}","page":"API Reference","text":"altitude(t::EGTriangle, i)\n\nThe altitude line from vertex t[i], perpendicular to the opposite side.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.altitude"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.angle_at-Tuple{EGPoint, EGPoint, EGPoint}","page":"API Reference","text":"angle_at(vertex, p1, p2)\n\nUnsigned interior angle (in radians, in [0, π]) at vertex between the rays vertex -> p1 and vertex -> p2. See also angle_between for the signed version, and EGAngle2 for a reusable object carrying the three points around instead of just the number.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.angle_at"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.angle_between-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"angle_between(u, v)\n\nSigned angle (in radians, in (-π, π]) to rotate vector u onto v (counterclockwise positive).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.angle_between"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.angle_bisectors-Tuple{EGLine, EGLine}","page":"API Reference","text":"angle_bisectors(l1::EGLine, l2::EGLine; atol=1e-9)\n\nThe angle bisector(s) of l1 and l2: two mutually perpendicular lines through their intersection point (the internal and external bisectors) if they intersect, or a single midline if they're parallel.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.angle_bisectors"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.angle_trisectors-Tuple{EGPoint, EGPoint, EGPoint}","page":"API Reference","text":"angle_trisectors(vertex::EGPoint, p1::EGPoint, p2::EGPoint)\n\nThe two rays from vertex that trisect the angle ∠(p1, vertex, p2) into three equal parts (the oriented angle from vertex -> p1 to vertex -> p2, i.e. going counterclockwise when that angle is positive).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.angle_trisectors"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.anticomplement-Tuple{EGTriangle, EGPoint}","page":"API Reference","text":"anticomplement(t::EGTriangle, p::EGPoint)\n\nThe anticomplement of p with respect to t: p homothetically expanded by -2 about the centroid — the inverse of complement. Sends each vertex to the corresponding vertex of the anticomplementary_triangle.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.anticomplement"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.anticomplementary_triangle-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"anticomplementary_triangle(t::EGTriangle)\n\nThe anticomplementary (antimedial) triangle of t: vertices B+C-A, C+A-B, A+B-C — the inverse of medial_triangle (t is the medial triangle of this one). Each vertex is the anticomplement of the opposite one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.anticomplementary_triangle"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.apollonius_circle-Tuple{EGPoint, EGPoint, Real}","page":"API Reference","text":"apollonius_circle(a::EGPoint, b::EGPoint, k::Real; atol=1e-9)\n\nThe Apollonius circle of a, b and ratio k: the locus of points p with distance(p, a) / distance(p, b) == k. Its diameter endpoints are the points dividing [a, b] internally and externally in ratio k. Throws an ArgumentError if k ≈ 1 (the locus is then the perpendicular bisector of [a, b], a line rather than a circle).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.apollonius_circle"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.arc_length-Tuple{EGCircularArc2}","page":"API Reference","text":"arc_length(arc::EGCircularArc2)\n\nThe length of arc: circle.r * measure(arc).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.arc_length"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.arc_length-Tuple{EGEllipticArc2}","page":"API Reference","text":"arc_length(arc::EGEllipticArc2)\n\nThe length of arc. Unlike arc_length(::EGCircularArc2), there's no elementary closed form for this (an elliptic arc length is, true to the name, an elliptic integral) — found instead by numerically integrating the parametrization speed |Δθ|·√(a²sin²θ + b²cos²θ) over arc's own angular range.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.arc_length"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.arc_length-Tuple{EGHyperbolicArc2}","page":"API Reference","text":"arc_length(arc::EGHyperbolicArc2)\n\nThe length of arc, found by numerically integrating the parametrization speed |t2-t1|·√(a²sinh²t + b²cosh²t) over arc's own parameter range (no elementary closed form exists for this either).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.arc_length"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.arc_length-Tuple{EGParabolicArc2}","page":"API Reference","text":"arc_length(arc::EGParabolicArc2)\n\nThe length of arc, found by numerically integrating the parametrization speed |s2-s1|·√((s/pf)² + 1) over arc's own parameter range (no elementary closed form exists for this either).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.arc_length"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.area-Tuple{EGPolygon}","page":"API Reference","text":"area(p::EGPolygon)\n\nUnsigned area of p, via the Green's-theorem line integral `(1/2)|∮(x dy\n\ny dx)|around [sides`](@ref) — correct whether every side is straight\n\nor some are EGCircularArc2s.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.area"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.asymptotes-Tuple{EGHyperbola2}","page":"API Reference","text":"asymptotes(h::EGHyperbola2)\n\nThe two asymptote lines of h, as a 2-tuple.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.asymptotes"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.barycenter-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:EGPoint}, AbstractVector{<:Real}}","page":"API Reference","text":"barycenter(points::AbstractVector{<:EGPoint}, weights::AbstractVector{<:Real})\n\nThe weighted barycenter (center of mass) of points with the given weights.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.barycenter"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.barycentric_coordinates-Tuple{EGTriangle, EGPoint}","page":"API Reference","text":"barycentric_coordinates(t::EGTriangle, p::EGPoint)\n\nThe normalized barycentric coordinates (α, β, γ) (summing to 1) of p relative to t, computed from signed sub-triangle areas.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.barycentric_coordinates"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.barycentric_point-Tuple{EGTriangle, Real, Real, Real}","page":"API Reference","text":"barycentric_point(t::EGTriangle, wA, wB, wC)\n\nThe point with barycentric coordinates (wA, wB, wC) relative to t (the weights need not be normalized).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.barycentric_point"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.bbox_aspect_ratio-Tuple{EGBoundingBox}","page":"API Reference","text":"bbox_aspect_ratio(bb::EGBoundingBox)\n\nbbox_width(bb) / bbox_height(bb).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.bbox_aspect_ratio"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.bbox_center-Tuple{EGBoundingBox}","page":"API Reference","text":"bbox_center(bb::EGBoundingBox)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.bbox_center"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.bbox_diagonal-Tuple{EGBoundingBox}","page":"API Reference","text":"bbox_diagonal(bb::EGBoundingBox)\n\nThe distance between bb.min and bb.max.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.bbox_diagonal"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.bbox_height-Tuple{EGBoundingBox}","page":"API Reference","text":"bbox_height(bb::EGBoundingBox)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.bbox_height"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.bbox_intersection-Tuple{EGBoundingBox{2}, EGBoundingBox{2}}","page":"API Reference","text":"bbox_intersection(a::EGBoundingBox, b::EGBoundingBox)\n\nThe overlapping box of a and b, or nothing if they don't intersect.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.bbox_intersection"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.bbox_union-Tuple{EGBoundingBox{2}, EGBoundingBox{2}}","page":"API Reference","text":"bbox_union(a::EGBoundingBox, b::EGBoundingBox)\n\nThe smallest box containing both a and b. Unlike bbox_intersection, this always exists — a/b don't need to overlap.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.bbox_union"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.bbox_width-Tuple{EGBoundingBox}","page":"API Reference","text":"bbox_width(bb::EGBoundingBox)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.bbox_width"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.bboxes_intersect-Tuple{EGBoundingBox{2}, EGBoundingBox{2}}","page":"API Reference","text":"bboxes_intersect(a::EGBoundingBox, b::EGBoundingBox)\n\nWhether a and b overlap (touching counts as intersecting).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.bboxes_intersect"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.bevan_point-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"bevan_point(t::EGTriangle)\n\nThe Bevan point of t: the circumcenter of its excentral triangle (the triangle formed by the three excenters).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.bevan_point"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.bisector-Tuple{EGTriangle, Integer}","page":"API Reference","text":"bisector(t::EGTriangle, i)\n\nThe internal angle bisector line from vertex t[i] (passes through the incenter).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.bisector"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.bisector_ext-Tuple{EGTriangle, Integer}","page":"API Reference","text":"bisector_ext(t::EGTriangle, i)\n\nThe external angle bisector line from vertex t[i], perpendicular to the internal one (bisector) at that same vertex.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.bisector_ext"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.brocard_angle-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"brocard_angle(t::EGTriangle)\n\nThe Brocard angle of t: the common value of ∠ΩAB = ∠ΩBC = ∠ΩCA at the first Brocard point.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.brocard_angle"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.brocard_axis-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"brocard_axis(t::EGTriangle)\n\nThe Brocard axis of t: the line through the circumcenter and the symmedian (Lemoine) point.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.brocard_axis"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.brocard_circle-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"brocard_circle(t::EGTriangle)\n\nThe Brocard circle of t: centered at the midpoint of the circumcenter and the symmedian point, passing through both Brocard points (among others).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.brocard_circle"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.brocard_inellipse-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"brocard_inellipse(t::EGTriangle)\n\nThe Brocard inellipse of t: the bifocal ellipse with foci at the second and first first_brocard_point/second_brocard_point (in that order), passing through the t[1]-side vertex of the cevian triangle of the symmedian_point.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.brocard_inellipse"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.brocard_midpoint-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"brocard_midpoint(t::EGTriangle)\n\nThe Brocard midpoint of t (Kimberling center X(39)): the midpoint of the two first_brocard_point and second_brocard_point.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.brocard_midpoint"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.centroid-Tuple{EGPolygon}","page":"API Reference","text":"centroid(p::EGPolygon)\n\nArea-weighted centroid of p. Falls back to the plain vertex average for (near-)zero-area (degenerate) polygons. For a triangle this coincides exactly with the plain vertex average (they're mathematically the same thing at n = 3); EGQuadrilateral overrides this with the plain average deliberately, since that's its own well-known convention.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.centroid"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.centroid-Tuple{EGQuadrilateral}","page":"API Reference","text":"centroid(q::EGQuadrilateral)\n\nThe plain (equal-weight) average of the four vertices — not area-weighted (that's centroid(::EGPolygon), which EGStraightNgon/EGTriangle use instead).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.centroid"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.cevian_triangle-Tuple{EGTriangle, EGPoint}","page":"API Reference","text":"cevian_triangle(t::EGTriangle, p::EGPoint)\n\nThe cevian triangle of p with respect to t: where each cevian vertex -> p meets the opposite side. Generalizes medial_triangle (cevian triangle of the centroid).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.cevian_triangle"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.circles_position-Tuple{EGCircle2, EGCircle2}","page":"API Reference","text":"circles_position(c1::EGCircle2, c2::EGCircle2; atol=1e-9)\n\nHow c1 and c2 relate, as one of: :identical (same center and radius), :concentric (same center, different radius), :disjoint_ext (too far apart to meet), :tangent_ext (externally tangent), :secant (cross at two points), :tangent_int (internally tangent), or :disjoint_int (one strictly inside the other, not touching).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.circles_position"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.circumcenter-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"circumcenter(t::EGTriangle)\n\nCenter of the circle passing through the three vertices of t.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.circumcenter"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.circumcevian_triangle-Tuple{EGTriangle, EGPoint}","page":"API Reference","text":"circumcevian_triangle(t::EGTriangle, p::EGPoint)\n\nThe circumcevian triangle of p with respect to t: where each cevian vertex -> p, extended, meets the circumcircle of t again.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.circumcevian_triangle"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.circumcircle-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"circumcircle(t::EGTriangle)\n\nThe circumscribed circle of t.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.circumcircle"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.circumradius-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"circumradius(t::EGTriangle)\n\nRadius of the circumscribed circle of t.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.circumradius"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.complement-Tuple{EGTriangle, EGPoint}","page":"API Reference","text":"complement(t::EGTriangle, p::EGPoint)\n\nThe complement of p with respect to t: p homothetically shrunk by -1/2 about the centroid. Sends each vertex to the midpoint of the opposite side (i.e. to the corresponding vertex of the medial_triangle).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.complement"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.conic_through_points-NTuple{5, EGPoint}","page":"API Reference","text":"conic_through_points(p1::EGPoint, p2::EGPoint, p3::EGPoint, p4::EGPoint, p5::EGPoint; atol=1e-9)\n\nThe ellipse or hyperbola (as an EGEllipse2 or EGHyperbola2) passing through the 5 given points, assumed to be in general position. Throws an ArgumentError if the points don't determine a unique conic (a degenerate configuration), or if that conic is a parabola (discriminant ≈ 0), which isn't representable by this function.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.conic_through_points"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.contact_triangle-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"contact_triangle(t::EGTriangle)\n\nThe contact (intouch) triangle of t: its vertices are the points where the incircle touches the sides of t.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.contact_triangle"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.convex_hull-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:EGPoint}}","page":"API Reference","text":"convex_hull(points::AbstractVector{<:EGPoint})\n\nThe convex hull of points, as an EGStraightNgon (Andrew's monotone chain algorithm, O(n log n)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.convex_hull"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.conway_circle-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"conway_circle(t::EGTriangle)\n\nThe Conway circle of t: centered at the incenter, passing through the 6 conway_points. Its radius is sqrt(inradius(t)^2 + s^2) where s is the semiperimeter.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.conway_circle"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.conway_points-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"conway_points(t::EGTriangle)\n\nThe 6 Conway points of t, as a 6-tuple: at each vertex, extend both sides meeting there beyond that vertex by the length of the side opposite it. All 6 lie on the conway_circle.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.conway_points"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.cross2-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"cross2(a, b)\n\nThe 2D (scalar) cross product a[1]*b[2] - a[2]*b[1].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.cross2"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.de_longchamps_point-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"de_longchamps_point(t::EGTriangle)\n\nThe de Longchamps point of t: the reflection of the orthocenter across the circumcenter (it lies on the Euler line).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.de_longchamps_point"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.diagonal_intersection-Tuple{EGQuadrilateral}","page":"API Reference","text":"diagonal_intersection(q::EGQuadrilateral; atol=1e-9)\n\nWhere the two diagonals [a,c] and [b,d] cross, or nothing if they're parallel (a degenerate quadrilateral).\n\nDeferred from Phase 3 (needed intersection(::EGLine, ::EGLine), not yet ported then) — now available since eg_intersections.jl (Phase 7) is included after this file.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.diagonal_intersection"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.diagonals-Tuple{EGQuadrilateral}","page":"API Reference","text":"diagonals(q::EGQuadrilateral)\n\nThe two diagonal segments [a,c] and [b,d].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.diagonals"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.direction-Tuple{EGLine}","page":"API Reference","text":"direction(obj)\n\nThe direction of a EGLine, EGRay or EGSegment, as an EGVector.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.direction"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.distance-Tuple{EGCircle2, EGCircle2}","page":"API Reference","text":"distance(c1::EGCircle2, c2::EGCircle2)\n\nThe distance between the two curves: 0 whenever they touch or cross (tangent, secant, or one properly inside the other without touching is the only case this is nonzero for and nested — see below), otherwise the gap — either the external gap (d - r1 - r2, when disjoint) or the gap between a smaller circle and the inside of a bigger one that encloses it (|r1-r2| - d, when nested without touching).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.distance"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.distance-Tuple{EGCircle2, EGLine}","page":"API Reference","text":"distance(c::EGCircle2, l::EGLine)\n\n0 if l crosses or is tangent to c; otherwise the gap between them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.distance"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.distance-Tuple{EGLine, EGLine}","page":"API Reference","text":"distance(l1::EGLine, l2::EGLine; atol=1e-9)\n\n0 if l1 and l2 cross; otherwise (they're parallel) the constant perpendicular gap between them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.distance"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.distance-Tuple{EGLine, EGRay}","page":"API Reference","text":"distance(l::EGLine, r::EGRay; atol=1e-9)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.distance"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.distance-Tuple{EGLine, EGSegment}","page":"API Reference","text":"distance(l::EGLine, s::EGSegment; atol=1e-9)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.distance"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.distance-Tuple{EGPoint, EGAngle2}","page":"API Reference","text":"distance(p::EGPoint, ang::EGAngle2; mode::Symbol=:region)\n\nDistance from p to the wedge ang (see EGAngle2). The wedge's boundary is exactly the union of the two rays vertex -> a and vertex -> b, so mode = :boundary is min of the distances to each ray — this holds for any measure, reflex wedges included. With mode = :region (the default), 0.0 whenever p lies inside the wedge, else the same boundary distance (for a closed region, the nearest point to an exterior point always lies on the boundary).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.distance"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.distance-Tuple{EGPoint, EGCircle2}","page":"API Reference","text":"distance(p::EGPoint, c::EGCircle2)\n\nDistance from p to the curve of c (like every other EGCurve — not to the filled disk Base.in tests membership of).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.distance"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.distance-Tuple{EGPoint, EGCircularArc2}","page":"API Reference","text":"distance(p::EGPoint, arc::EGCircularArc2)\n\nDistance from p to arc itself — not the full circle: if p's angular projection falls within the arc's own sweep, this is the same as distance(::EGPoint, ::EGCircle2); otherwise it's the closer of the two endpoints.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.distance"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.distance-Tuple{EGPoint, EGEllipse2}","page":"API Reference","text":"distance(p::EGPoint, e::EGEllipse2)\n\nDistance from p to the curve of e. Unlike every other conic distance in this package, there's no closed form for this — it's found by Newton's method on the ellipse's own parametrization (essentially exact in practice, converging to machine precision in a handful of iterations for any non-degenerate ellipse).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.distance"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.distance-Tuple{EGPoint, EGEllipticArc2}","page":"API Reference","text":"distance(p::EGPoint, arc::EGEllipticArc2)\n\nDistance from p to arc itself: if the closest point on the full ellipse falls within the arc's own parameter range, that's the answer (via the same Newton solve as distance(::EGPoint, ::EGEllipse2)); otherwise it's the closer of the two endpoints.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.distance"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.distance-Tuple{EGPoint, EGHalfPlane2}","page":"API Reference","text":"distance(p::EGPoint, hp::EGHalfPlane2; mode::Symbol=:region)\n\nDistance from p to the half-plane hp. With mode = :region (the default), 0.0 whenever p in hp, else the distance to hp.boundary. With mode = :boundary, always the distance to hp.boundary, even from inside.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.distance"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.distance-Tuple{EGPoint, EGHyperbola2}","page":"API Reference","text":"distance(p::EGPoint, h::EGHyperbola2)\n\nDistance from p to the curve of h (either branch — whichever is closer). Like distance(::EGPoint, ::EGEllipse2), found via Newton's method rather than a closed form.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.distance"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.distance-Tuple{EGPoint, EGHyperbolicArc2}","page":"API Reference","text":"distance(p::EGPoint, arc::EGHyperbolicArc2)\n\nDistance from p to arc itself: if the closest point on arc's own branch of the full hyperbola falls within the arc's own parameter range, that's the answer (via the same Newton solve as distance(::EGPoint, ::EGHyperbola2)); otherwise it's the closer of the two endpoints.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.distance"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.distance-Tuple{EGPoint, EGParabola2}","page":"API Reference","text":"distance(p::EGPoint, par::EGParabola2)\n\nDistance from p to the curve of par. Like the ellipse/hyperbola cases, found via Newton's method rather than a closed form.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.distance"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.distance-Tuple{EGPoint, EGParabolicArc2}","page":"API Reference","text":"distance(p::EGPoint, arc::EGParabolicArc2)\n\nDistance from p to arc itself: if the closest point on the full parabola falls within the arc's own parameter range, that's the answer (via the same Newton solve as distance(::EGPoint, ::EGParabola2)); otherwise it's the closer of the two endpoints.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.distance"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.distance-Tuple{EGPoint, EGPoint}","page":"API Reference","text":"distance(p1::EGPoint, p2::EGPoint)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.distance"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.distance-Tuple{EGPoint, EGPolygon}","page":"API Reference","text":"distance(p::EGPoint, pg::EGPolygon; mode::Symbol=:region)\n\nDistance from p to pg, defined once for the whole EGPolygon family via sides — straight or curved alike. With mode = :region (the default), 0.0 whenever p lies inside or on the boundary of pg, otherwise the distance to the nearest point on sides(pg). With mode = :boundary, always the distance to the boundary itself, even from inside pg.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.distance"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.distance-Tuple{EGPoint, EGRay}","page":"API Reference","text":"distance(p::EGPoint, r::EGRay)\n\nDistance from p to the closest point of the half-line r (clamped at r.origin, unbounded past r.through).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.distance"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.distance-Tuple{EGPoint, EGSegment}","page":"API Reference","text":"distance(p::EGPoint, s::EGSegment)\n\nDistance from p to the closest point of the finite segment s (unlike distance(::EGPoint, ::EGLine), which measures to the infinite line through s's two points).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.distance"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.distance-Tuple{EGPoint, EGStrip2}","page":"API Reference","text":"distance(p::EGPoint, s::EGStrip2; mode::Symbol=:region)\n\nDistance from p to the strip s. With mode = :region (the default), 0.0 whenever p in s, else min of the distances to s.line1/ s.line2. With mode = :boundary, always that min, even from inside.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.distance"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.distance-Tuple{EGPoint{2}, EGBoundingBox{2}}","page":"API Reference","text":"distance(p::EGPoint, bb::EGBoundingBox; mode::Symbol=:region)\n\nmode=:region (default): 0 when p is inside or on bb, otherwise the usual point-to-axis-aligned-box distance. mode=:boundary: always the distance to the nearest edge, even from inside.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.distance"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.distance-Tuple{EGRay, EGRay}","page":"API Reference","text":"distance(r1::EGRay, r2::EGRay; atol=1e-9)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.distance"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.distance-Tuple{EGRay, EGSegment}","page":"API Reference","text":"distance(r::EGRay, s::EGSegment; atol=1e-9)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.distance"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.distance-Tuple{EGSegment, EGSegment}","page":"API Reference","text":"distance(s1::EGSegment, s2::EGSegment; atol=1e-9)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.distance"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.egyptian_triangle_on_segment-Tuple{EGPoint, EGPoint}","page":"API Reference","text":"egyptian_triangle_on_segment(a::EGPoint, b::EGPoint; ccw::Bool=true)\n\nThe 3-4-5 right triangle with [a, b] as its \"4\" side: the right angle is at b, and the \"3\" side [b, c] is perpendicular to [a, b] with length 0.75 * distance(a, b) — giving hypotenuse [a, c] the remaining \"5\" side automatically. Built counterclockwise from a to b (ccw=false builds it on the other side).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.egyptian_triangle_on_segment"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.equilateral_triangle_on_segment-Tuple{EGPoint, EGPoint}","page":"API Reference","text":"equilateral_triangle_on_segment(a::EGPoint, b::EGPoint; ccw::Bool=true)\n\nThe equilateral triangle with side [a, b], built counterclockwise from a to b (ccw=false builds it on the other side).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.equilateral_triangle_on_segment"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.euler_line-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"euler_line(t::EGTriangle)\n\nThe Euler line of t, through its centroid, circumcenter and orthocenter.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.euler_line"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.euler_points-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"euler_points(t::EGTriangle)\n\nThe three Euler points of t, as a 3-tuple: the midpoints of the segments joining the orthocenter to each vertex. Together with the three edge midpoints and the three altitude feet, these are the nine defining points of the nine_point_circle.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.euler_points"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.excenters-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"excenters(t::EGTriangle)\n\nThe three excenters of t, as a (A=..., B=..., C=...) named tuple where A is the excenter opposite vertex t[1], etc.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.excenters"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.excentral_triangle-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"excentral_triangle(t::EGTriangle)\n\nThe excentral triangle of t: its vertices are the three excenters of t.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.excentral_triangle"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.excircles-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"excircles(t::EGTriangle)\n\nThe three excircles of t, as a (A=..., B=..., C=...) named tuple (see excenters and exradii).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.excircles"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.exradii-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"exradii(t::EGTriangle)\n\nThe three exradii of t, as a (A=..., B=..., C=...) named tuple where A is the radius of the excircle opposite vertex t[1], etc.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.exradii"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.external_similitude_center-Tuple{EGCircle2, EGCircle2}","page":"API Reference","text":"external_similitude_center(c1::EGCircle2, c2::EGCircle2; atol=1e-9)\n\nThe external center of similitude of c1 and c2: the point dividing the segment of centers externally in ratio c1.r : c2.r (the common intersection of the external tangent lines, and of any line through both centers' \"same-direction\" homothety images). Throws an ArgumentError if the radii are equal (the external tangents are then parallel, with no finite center).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.external_similitude_center"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.external_tangent_lines-Tuple{EGCircle2, EGCircle2}","page":"API Reference","text":"external_tangent_lines(c1::EGCircle2, c2::EGCircle2; atol=1e-9)\n\nThe common external tangent lines of c1 and c2 (the ones that don't cross the segment between the centers). Returns 0, 1 or 2 lines.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.external_tangent_lines"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.extouch_triangle-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"extouch_triangle(t::EGTriangle)\n\nThe extouch triangle of t: its vertices are the points where each excircle touches the corresponding side of t.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.extouch_triangle"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.fermat_axis-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"fermat_axis(t::EGTriangle)\n\nThe line through the two fermat_point/second_fermat_point.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.fermat_axis"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.fermat_point-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"fermat_point(t::EGTriangle)\n\nThe (first) Fermat point of t: the point minimizing the sum of distances to the three vertices when every angle of t is below 120°. Constructed as the common intersection of the segments joining each vertex to the apex of the equilateral triangle erected outward on the opposite side.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.fermat_point"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.feuerbach_point-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"feuerbach_point(t::EGTriangle)\n\nThe Feuerbach point of t: the point where the incircle and the nine-point circle are (internally) tangent.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.feuerbach_point"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.feuerbach_points-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"feuerbach_points(t::EGTriangle)\n\nThe three points where the nine-point circle of t is (externally) tangent to each of the three excircles, as an (A=..., B=..., C=...) named tuple matching excircles — the excircle analogue of feuerbach_point (which is the nine-point circle's tangency with the incircle, an internal tangency instead).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.feuerbach_points"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.first_brocard_point-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"first_brocard_point(t::EGTriangle)\n\nThe first Brocard point of t: the point Ω such that ∠ΩAB = ∠ΩBC = ∠ΩCA (the Brocard angle). Constructed as the second intersection of the circles (A,B) tangent to BC at B and (B,C) tangent to CA at C (both pass through B).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.first_brocard_point"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.first_lemoine_circle-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"first_lemoine_circle(t::EGTriangle)\n\nThe first Lemoine circle of t: centered at the midpoint of the circumcenter and the symmedian point, passing through the 6 first_lemoine_points.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.first_lemoine_circle"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.first_lemoine_points-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"first_lemoine_points(t::EGTriangle)\n\n6 points, as a 6-tuple: through the symmedian point, draw the line parallel to each side, and take its intersections with the other two sides. All 6 lie on the first_lemoine_circle.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.first_lemoine_points"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.focal_parameter-Tuple{EGParabola2}","page":"API Reference","text":"focal_parameter(par::EGParabola2)\n\nThe distance between the focus and the directrix of par (often denoted p).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.focal_parameter"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.foci-Tuple{EGEllipse2}","page":"API Reference","text":"foci(e::EGEllipse2)\n\nThe two foci of e, as a 2-tuple.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.foci"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.foci-Tuple{EGHyperbola2}","page":"API Reference","text":"foci(h::EGHyperbola2)\n\nThe two foci of h, as a 2-tuple.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.foci"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.gergonne_point-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"gergonne_point(t::EGTriangle)\n\nThe Gergonne point of t (barycentric coordinates 1/(s-a) : 1/(s-b) : 1/(s-c)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.gergonne_point"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.golden_gnomon_on_segment-Tuple{EGPoint, EGPoint}","page":"API Reference","text":"golden_gnomon_on_segment(a::EGPoint, b::EGPoint; ccw::Bool=true)\n\nThe golden gnomon with base [a, b]: isosceles with base angles 36° and apex angle 108°, built counterclockwise from a to b (ccw=false builds it on the other side).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.golden_gnomon_on_segment"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.golden_ratio_point-Tuple{EGPoint, EGPoint}","page":"API Reference","text":"golden_ratio_point(a::EGPoint, b::EGPoint)\ngolden_ratio_point(l::EGLine)\n\nThe point on segment [a, b] (or [l.p1, l.p2]) dividing it in the golden ratio from a, i.e. a + (b - a) / φ.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.golden_ratio_point"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.golden_triangle_on_segment-Tuple{EGPoint, EGPoint}","page":"API Reference","text":"golden_triangle_on_segment(a::EGPoint, b::EGPoint; ccw::Bool=true)\n\nThe golden triangle with base [a, b]: isosceles with base angles 72° and apex angle 36°, built counterclockwise from a to b (ccw=false builds it on the other side).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.golden_triangle_on_segment"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.harmonic_conjugate-Tuple{EGPoint, EGPoint, EGPoint}","page":"API Reference","text":"harmonic_conjugate(a::EGPoint, b::EGPoint, p::EGPoint; atol=1e-9)\nharmonic_conjugate(l::EGLine, p::EGPoint; atol=1e-9)\n\nThe harmonic conjugate of p with respect to a and b: the point q (collinear with a, b, p) such that the cross-ratio (a, b; p, q) equals -1. Throws an error if p is the midpoint of [a, b] (its harmonic conjugate is the point at infinity).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.harmonic_conjugate"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.homothety","page":"API Reference","text":"homothety(p::EGPoint, k, center=EGPoint(0.0, 0.0))\n\nScale p by ratio k about center.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.homothety"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.homothety_map-Tuple{Real, EGPoint{2}}","page":"API Reference","text":"homothety_map(k::Real, center::EGPoint)\n\nThe affine map scaling by ratio k about center. Equivalent to p -> homothety(p, k, center), but composable. See rotation_map for why center has no zero-argument default here.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.homothety_map"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.incenter-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"incenter(t::EGTriangle)\n\nCenter of the circle inscribed in t.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.incenter"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.incircle-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"incircle(t::EGTriangle)\n\nThe inscribed circle of t.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.incircle"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.inradius-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"inradius(t::EGTriangle)\n\nRadius of the inscribed circle of t.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.inradius"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.internal_similitude_center-Tuple{EGCircle2, EGCircle2}","page":"API Reference","text":"internal_similitude_center(c1::EGCircle2, c2::EGCircle2; atol=1e-9)\n\nThe internal center of similitude of c1 and c2: the point dividing the segment of centers internally in ratio c1.r : c2.r. Throws an ArgumentError if both radii are (near) zero.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.internal_similitude_center"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.internal_tangent_lines-Tuple{EGCircle2, EGCircle2}","page":"API Reference","text":"internal_tangent_lines(c1::EGCircle2, c2::EGCircle2; atol=1e-9)\n\nThe common internal tangent lines of c1 and c2 (the ones that cross the segment between the centers). Returns 0, 1 or 2 lines; empty when the circles overlap (no internal tangents exist).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.internal_tangent_lines"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.intersection-Tuple{EGLine, EGEllipse2}","page":"API Reference","text":"intersection(l::EGLine, e::EGEllipse2; atol=1e-9)\n\nIntersection points of l and e. Returns a Vector{EGPoint{2,Float64}} with 0, 1 or 2 points.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.intersection"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.intersection-Tuple{EGLine, EGHyperbola2}","page":"API Reference","text":"intersection(l::EGLine, h::EGHyperbola2; atol=1e-9)\n\nIntersection points of l and h. Returns a Vector{EGPoint{2,Float64}} with 0, 1 or 2 points.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.intersection"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.intersection-Tuple{EGLine, EGLine}","page":"API Reference","text":"intersection(a, b; atol=1e-9)\n\nIntersection points of two EGLines, an EGLine and an EGCircle2, two EGSegments, or two EGCircle2s. Returns a Vector{EGPoint{2,Float64}} with 0, 1 or 2 points.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.intersection"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.intersection-Tuple{EGLine, EGParabola2}","page":"API Reference","text":"intersection(l::EGLine, par::EGParabola2; atol=1e-9)\n\nIntersection points of l and par. Returns a Vector{EGPoint{2,Float64}} with 0, 1 or 2 points.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.intersection"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.intersection-Tuple{EGSegment, EGSegment}","page":"API Reference","text":"intersection(s1::EGSegment, s2::EGSegment; atol=1e-9)\n\nIntersection point of two finite segments (empty if they don't actually cross, even if the lines they lie on would). Returns a Vector{EGPoint{2,Float64}} with 0 or 1 points; overlapping collinear segments are treated as having no (single-point) intersection.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.intersection"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.interstices-Tuple{EGCircle2, EGCircle2, EGCircle2}","page":"API Reference","text":"interstices(c1::EGCircle2, c2::EGCircle2, c3::EGCircle2; atol=1e-9)\n\nThe curvilinear-triangle gap(s) of three mutually tangent circles, as a Vector{EGInterstice2} — length 1 when the three are an externally tangent chain (none contains another), or 2 when one contains the other two (each internally tangent to it, and externally tangent to each other). Independent of the order c1, c2, c3 are given in.\n\nBuilt by reusing tangent_circles (the CCC Apollonius problem): every genuine interstice has an inscribed circle tangent to all three (never enclosing any of them), and each such solution's center pins down exactly one interstice. Throws an ArgumentError if the three circles aren't all pairwise tangent.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.interstices"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.inversion-Tuple{EGPoint, EGCircle2}","page":"API Reference","text":"inversion(p::EGPoint, c::EGCircle2)\n\nThe image of p under inversion with respect to circle c (center O, radius r): the point p' on ray O -> p such that |Op| * |Op'| = r^2.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.inversion"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.inversion_neg-Tuple{EGPoint, EGCircle2}","page":"API Reference","text":"inversion_neg(p::EGPoint, c::EGCircle2)\n\nThe image of p under inversion of negative ratio with respect to c: the ordinary (positive) inversion, point-reflected through c.center — i.e. the point on ray p -> O (not O -> p) at distance r^2/|Op| from O.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.inversion_neg"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.invert-Tuple{EGCircle2, EGPoint}","page":"API Reference","text":"invert(c::EGCircle2, center::EGPoint; k::Real=1.0, atol=1e-9)\n\nThe image of c under inversion with respect to the circle centered at center with radius k. A circle through center inverts to an EGLine (the mirror image of invert(::EGLine, ::EGPoint)); any other circle inverts to another EGCircle2 — so this returns a Union{EGCircle2,EGLine}.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.invert"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.invert-Tuple{EGLine, EGPoint}","page":"API Reference","text":"invert(l::EGLine, center::EGPoint; k::Real=1.0, atol=1e-9)\n\nThe image of l under inversion with respect to the circle centered at center with radius k. A line not through the inversion center always inverts to a circle through it. Throws an ArgumentError if l passes through center (its image would be l itself, not a circle).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.invert"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.invert-Tuple{EGSegment, EGPoint}","page":"API Reference","text":"invert(s::EGSegment, center::EGPoint; k::Real=1.0, atol=1e-9)\n\nThe image of s under inversion with respect to the circle centered at center with radius k. A segment on a line through center inverts to another EGSegment (on the same line); any other segment inverts to an EGCircularArc2 of invert(::EGLine, ::EGPoint)'s image circle — specifically the arc that does not pass through center, since that point is the image of the line's own point at infinity, which the segment (being finite) never reaches. So this returns a Union{EGSegment,EGCircularArc2}.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.invert"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.invert-Tuple{EGTriangle, EGPoint}","page":"API Reference","text":"invert(t::EGTriangle, center::EGPoint; k::Real=1.0, atol=1e-9)\ninvert(pg::EGStraightNgon, center::EGPoint; k::Real=1.0, atol=1e-9)\n\nThe image of t/pg under inversion with respect to the circle centered at center with radius k: each side inverts independently (see invert(::EGSegment, ::EGPoint)) to a straight EGSegment or an EGCircularArc2, and the results are collected into an EGCurvilinearNgon2. Throws an ArgumentError if any vertex coincides with center (that vertex has no image under inversion).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.invert"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.invert_neg-Tuple{EGLine, EGPoint}","page":"API Reference","text":"invert_neg(l::EGLine, center::EGPoint; k::Real=1.0, atol=1e-9)\ninvert_neg(c::EGCircle2, center::EGPoint; k::Real=1.0, atol=1e-9)\n\nThe image of l/c under inversion of negative ratio with respect to the circle centered at center with radius k: the ordinary invert image, point-reflected through center.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.invert_neg"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.is_collinear-Tuple{EGPoint, EGPoint, EGPoint}","page":"API Reference","text":"is_collinear(a::EGPoint, b::EGPoint, c::EGPoint; atol=1e-9)\n\nWhether points a, b and c lie on a common line.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.is_collinear"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.is_concyclic-NTuple{4, EGPoint}","page":"API Reference","text":"is_concyclic(a::EGPoint, b::EGPoint, c::EGPoint, d::EGPoint; atol=1e-9)\n\nWhether the four points lie on a common circle (or are collinear, treated as a degenerate \"circle\" of infinite radius). a, b, c must not be collinear unless d is collinear with them too.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.is_concyclic"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.is_convex-Tuple{EGPolygon}","page":"API Reference","text":"is_convex(p::EGPolygon; atol=1e-9)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.is_convex"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.is_cyclic-Tuple{EGQuadrilateral}","page":"API Reference","text":"is_cyclic(q::EGQuadrilateral; atol=1e-9)\n\nWhether the four vertices of q lie on a common circle. Also deferred from Phase 3 (needed is_concyclic, itself needing circumcenter/circumradius(::EGTriangle), not ported until egtriangle.jl) — referenced here only inside a function body, so the include order (this file comes well before egtriangle.jl) doesn't matter.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.is_cyclic"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.is_degenerate-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"is_degenerate(t::EGTriangle; atol=1e-9)\n\nWhether the three vertices of t are collinear (zero area).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.is_degenerate"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.is_direct-Tuple{EGAngle2}","page":"API Reference","text":"is_direct(ang::EGAngle2)\n\nWhether ang is oriented counterclockwise (its signed measure is positive).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.is_direct"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.is_on_ellipse-Tuple{EGPoint, EGEllipse2}","page":"API Reference","text":"is_on_ellipse(p::EGPoint, e::EGEllipse2; atol=1e-9)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.is_on_ellipse"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.is_on_hyperbola-Tuple{EGPoint, EGHyperbola2}","page":"API Reference","text":"is_on_hyperbola(p::EGPoint, h::EGHyperbola2; atol=1e-9)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.is_on_hyperbola"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.is_on_parabola-Tuple{EGPoint, EGParabola2}","page":"API Reference","text":"is_on_parabola(p::EGPoint, par::EGParabola2; atol=1e-9)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.is_on_parabola"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.is_parallel-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"is_parallel(l1, l2; atol=1e-9)\n\nWhether two EGLines (or EGRays / EGSegments) have the same direction.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.is_parallel"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.is_perpendicular-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"is_perpendicular(l1, l2; atol=1e-9)\n\nWhether two EGLines (or EGRays / EGSegments) are orthogonal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.is_perpendicular"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.isodynamic_points-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"isodynamic_points(t::EGTriangle)\n\nThe two isodynamic points of t, as a 2-tuple: the common points of its three_apollonius_circles (for each vertex, the locus of points whose distances to the other two vertices are in the same ratio as the two sides meeting at that vertex). Throws an ArgumentError for an equilateral triangle (the Apollonius circles degenerate to lines).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.isodynamic_points"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.isogonal_conjugate-Tuple{EGTriangle, EGPoint}","page":"API Reference","text":"isogonal_conjugate(t::EGTriangle, p::EGPoint)\n\nThe isogonal conjugate of p with respect to t: reflect each cevian vertex -> p across the internal angle bisector at that vertex; the three reflected lines concur at the isogonal conjugate. An involution (isogonal_conjugate(t, isogonal_conjugate(t, p)) == p) that sends orthocenter ↔ circumcenter, centroid ↔ symmedian point, and fixes the incenter.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.isogonal_conjugate"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.isosceles_right_triangle_on_segment-Tuple{EGPoint, EGPoint}","page":"API Reference","text":"isosceles_right_triangle_on_segment(a::EGPoint, b::EGPoint; ccw::Bool=true)\n\nThe isosceles right triangle with hypotenuse [a, b], built via Thales' theorem (the right-angle vertex lies on the circle with diameter [a, b]), counterclockwise from a to b (ccw=false builds it on the other side).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.isosceles_right_triangle_on_segment"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.isosceles_triangle_on_segment-Tuple{EGPoint, EGPoint, Real}","page":"API Reference","text":"isosceles_triangle_on_segment(a::EGPoint, b::EGPoint, leg::Real; ccw::Bool=true)\n\nThe isosceles triangle with base [a, b] and the given equal leg length (distance(a, c) == distance(b, c) == leg), built counterclockwise from a to b (ccw=false builds it on the other side). Throws an ArgumentError if leg is too short to reach across the base.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.isosceles_triangle_on_segment"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.isotomic_conjugate-Tuple{EGTriangle, EGPoint}","page":"API Reference","text":"isotomic_conjugate(t::EGTriangle, p::EGPoint)\n\nThe isotomic conjugate of p with respect to t: reflect, for each side, the point where cevian vertex -> p meets that side across the side's midpoint; the three cevians through the reflected points concur at the isotomic conjugate. An involution that fixes the centroid.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.isotomic_conjugate"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.kenmotu_circle-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"kenmotu_circle(t::EGTriangle)\n\nThe Kenmotu circle of t: centered at the kenmotu_point, with radius √2·a·b·c / (4·Area + a²+b²+c²). Passes through the 6 points where the three equal inscribed squares of the Kenmotu configuration meet the sides of t.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.kenmotu_circle"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.kenmotu_point-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"kenmotu_point(t::EGTriangle)\n\nThe Kenmotu point of t (Kimberling center X(371)): the common vertex of the three congruent squares inscribed in t, one per vertex, each with a diagonal's endpoints on the two sides meeting at that vertex. Given here by its trilinear coordinates cos(A - π/4) : cos(B - π/4) : cos(C - π/4) (equivalently cos A + sin A etc.).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.kenmotu_point"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.kiepert_hyperbola-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"kiepert_hyperbola(t::EGTriangle)\n\nThe Kiepert hyperbola of t: the unique rectangular hyperbola through t's three vertices, its centroid and its orthocenter (centered at Kimberling center X(115)). Built via conic_through_points on those 5 points, rather than reproducing tkz-elements' own longer axis-construction algorithm — both give the same curve, since a conic is uniquely determined by 5 points and this one is already known to pass through all 5.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.kiepert_hyperbola"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.kiepert_parabola-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"kiepert_parabola(t::EGTriangle)\n\nThe Kiepert parabola of t: focus at Kimberling center X(110) (trilinear(a/(b²-c²), b/(c²-a²), c/(a²-b²))), directrix the euler_line. Throws an ArgumentError if t is isosceles (X(110) is undefined then).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.kiepert_parabola"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.lemoine_axis-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"lemoine_axis(t::EGTriangle)\n\nThe Lemoine axis of t: the polar line of the symmedian point with respect to the circumcircle.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.lemoine_axis"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.lemoine_inellipse-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"lemoine_inellipse(t::EGTriangle)\n\nThe Lemoine inellipse of t: the bifocal ellipse with foci at the centroid and symmedian_point, passing through the t[1]-side vertex of the cevian triangle of Kimberling center X(598).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.lemoine_inellipse"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.line_circle_position-Tuple{EGLine, EGCircle2}","page":"API Reference","text":"line_circle_position(l::EGLine, c::EGCircle2; atol=1e-9)\n\nHow l and c relate: :disjoint (no intersection), :tangent (touch at exactly one point), or :secant (cross at two points).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.line_circle_position"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.macbeath_inellipse-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"macbeath_inellipse(t::EGTriangle)\n\nThe MacBeath inellipse of t: the bifocal ellipse with foci at the circumcenter and orthocenter, passing through the t[1]-side vertex of the cevian triangle of the macbeath_point. Only defined (as a real ellipse) when t has no obtuse angle.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.macbeath_inellipse"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.macbeath_point-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"macbeath_point(t::EGTriangle)\n\nThe MacBeath point of t (Kimberling center X(264)): the isotomic conjugate of the circumcenter. It is the Brianchon point of the MacBeath inconic (the inconic with foci at the circumcenter and the orthocenter).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.macbeath_point"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.mandart_inellipse-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"mandart_inellipse(t::EGTriangle)\n\nThe Mandart inellipse of t: centered at the mittenpunkt, tangent to the three sides at the extouch_triangle's vertices. Built via conic_through_points on those 3 vertices together with 2 of their point-reflections through the mittenpunkt (also on the ellipse, since it's centrally symmetric about its own center).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.mandart_inellipse"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.measure-Tuple{EGAngle2}","page":"API Reference","text":"measure(ang::EGAngle2)\n\nThe signed measure of ang (radians, in (-π, π], counterclockwise from ray vertex -> a to ray vertex -> b) — see angle_between.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.measure"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.measure-Tuple{EGCircularArc2}","page":"API Reference","text":"measure(arc::EGCircularArc2)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.measure"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.measure-Tuple{EGEllipticArc2}","page":"API Reference","text":"measure(arc::EGEllipticArc2)\n\nThe swept parameter range from p1 to p2, counterclockwise, in [0, 2π) (the ellipse's own angular parameter, not true arc angle).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.measure"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.medial_triangle-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"medial_triangle(t::EGTriangle)\n\nThe medial triangle of t: its vertices are the midpoints of the sides of t.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.medial_triangle"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.median-Tuple{EGTriangle, Integer}","page":"API Reference","text":"median(t::EGTriangle, i)\n\nThe median line from vertex t[i] to the midpoint of the opposite side.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.median"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.mediator-Tuple{EGTriangle, Integer}","page":"API Reference","text":"mediator(t::EGTriangle, i)\n\nThe perpendicular bisector of the side opposite vertex t[i] (passes through the circumcenter).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.mediator"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.midpoint-Tuple{EGPoint, EGPoint}","page":"API Reference","text":"midpoint(p1::EGPoint, p2::EGPoint)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.midpoint"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.mittenpunkt-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"mittenpunkt(t::EGTriangle)\n\nThe mittenpunkt of t (barycentric coordinates a(s-a) : b(s-b) : c(s-c)), the point of concurrence of the lines from each excenter to the midpoint of the corresponding side.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.mittenpunkt"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.mixtilinear_incircle-Tuple{EGTriangle, Integer}","page":"API Reference","text":"mixtilinear_incircle(t::EGTriangle, i::Integer; atol=1e-9)\n\nThe mixtilinear incircle at vertex t[i] (i in 1:3): the circle tangent to the two sides of t through that vertex, and internally tangent to the circumcircle of t. Built by reusing tangent_circles (the CLL Apollonius case) and picking, among its up to 4 solutions, the one nestled inside the vertex's angle with internal circumcircle tangency.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.mixtilinear_incircle"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.morley_triangle-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"morley_triangle(t::EGTriangle)\n\nThe Morley triangle of t: formed by the intersections of adjacent interior-angle trisectors. Always equilateral (Morley's trisector theorem).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.morley_triangle"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.nagel_point-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"nagel_point(t::EGTriangle)\n\nThe Nagel point of t (barycentric coordinates (s-a):(s-b):(s-c)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.nagel_point"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.napoleon_point-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"napoleon_point(t::EGTriangle; outward::Bool=true)\n\nThe center of the (outer, by default) Napoleon triangle of t — which, by Napoleon's theorem, coincides with the centroid of t itself.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.napoleon_point"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.napoleon_triangle-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"napoleon_triangle(t::EGTriangle; outward::Bool=true)\n\nThe (outer, by default) Napoleon triangle of t: the triangle formed by the centroids of the equilateral triangles erected on each side of t (outward by default; inward if outward=false). Always equilateral (Napoleon's theorem).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.napoleon_triangle"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.nine_point_center-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"nine_point_center(t::EGTriangle)\n\nCenter of the nine-point (Euler) circle of t: the midpoint of the segment joining the circumcenter and the orthocenter.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.nine_point_center"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.nine_point_circle-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"nine_point_circle(t::EGTriangle)\n\nThe nine-point (Euler) circle of t, passing through the midpoints of the three sides, the feet of the three altitudes, and the midpoints of the segments from the orthocenter to each vertex. Its radius is half the circumradius.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.nine_point_circle"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.normalized_measure-Tuple{EGAngle2}","page":"API Reference","text":"normalized_measure(ang::EGAngle2)\n\nThe measure of ang, in [0, 2π) instead of (-π, π].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.normalized_measure"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.offset_line-Tuple{EGLine, Real}","page":"API Reference","text":"offset_line(l::EGLine, d::Real)\n\nThe line parallel to l, shifted by signed distance d along its normal (positive d shifts towards the left of l.p1 -> l.p2).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.offset_line"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.on_line-Tuple{EGPoint, EGLine}","page":"API Reference","text":"on_line(p::EGPoint, l::EGLine; atol=1e-9)\n\nWhether point p lies on the infinite line l.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.on_line"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.on_segment-Tuple{EGPoint, EGSegment}","page":"API Reference","text":"on_segment(p::EGPoint, s::EGSegment; atol=1e-9)\n\nWhether point p lies on the finite segment s (endpoints included).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.on_segment"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.orthic_axis-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"orthic_axis(t::EGTriangle)\n\nThe orthic axis of t: the radical axis of its circumcircle and nine_point_circle. Always perpendicular to the euler_line.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.orthic_axis"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.orthic_inellipse-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"orthic_inellipse(t::EGTriangle)\n\nThe orthic inellipse of t: centered at the symmedian_point, tangent to the three sides at the orthic_triangle's vertices (the altitude feet). Built the same way as mandart_inellipse, via conic_through_points. Only defined (as a real ellipse) when t has no obtuse angle.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.orthic_inellipse"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.orthic_triangle-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"orthic_triangle(t::EGTriangle)\n\nThe orthic triangle of t: its vertices are the feet of the altitudes of t.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.orthic_triangle"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.orthocenter-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"orthocenter(t::EGTriangle)\n\nIntersection point of the three altitudes of t, obtained from the Euler line relation H = 3G - 2O.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.orthocenter"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.orthogonal-Tuple{EGVector{2}}","page":"API Reference","text":"orthogonal(v::EGVector)\n\nv rotated by +90 degrees (counterclockwise). 2D only.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.orthogonal"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.orthopole-Tuple{EGLine, EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"orthopole(l::EGLine, t::EGTriangle)\n\nThe orthopole of l with respect to t: project each vertex of t onto l, then draw through each projection the perpendicular to the side opposite that vertex; the three perpendiculars concur at the orthopole.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.orthopole"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.orthoptic-Tuple{EGEllipse2}","page":"API Reference","text":"orthoptic(e::EGEllipse2)\n\nThe orthoptic (director) circle of e: the locus of points from which the two tangent lines to e are perpendicular. Always a real circle, of radius sqrt(a^2 + b^2) centered at e.center.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.orthoptic"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.orthoptic-Tuple{EGHyperbola2}","page":"API Reference","text":"orthoptic(h::EGHyperbola2)\n\nThe orthoptic (director) circle of h: the locus of points from which the two tangent lines to h are perpendicular. Only real when a > b (radius sqrt(a^2 - b^2), centered at h.center); throws an ArgumentError otherwise, since then no real such circle exists.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.orthoptic"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.orthoptic-Tuple{EGParabola2}","page":"API Reference","text":"orthoptic(par::EGParabola2)\n\nThe orthoptic curve of par: the locus of points from which the two tangent lines to par are perpendicular. For a parabola this is, somewhat surprisingly, exactly its own directrix.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.orthoptic"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.parallel_through-Tuple{EGLine, EGPoint}","page":"API Reference","text":"parallel_through(l::EGLine, p::EGPoint)\n\nThe line through p parallel to l.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.parallel_through"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.parallelogram-Tuple{EGPoint, EGPoint, EGPoint}","page":"API Reference","text":"parallelogram(a::EGPoint, b::EGPoint, c::EGPoint)\n\nThe parallelogram with consecutive vertices a, b, c and fourth vertex d = a + (c - b) (so [a,b] ∥ [d,c] and [b,c] ∥ [a,d]).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.parallelogram"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.path","page":"API Reference","text":"path(obj; action=:path, kwargs...)\n\nExperimental, not yet stable — names and behavior may still change.\n\nAdd obj (an EGPoint, EGVector, EGSegment, EGLine, EGRay, EGCircle2, EGBoundingBox, EGEllipse2, EGParabola2, EGHyperbola2, EGAngle2, EGHalfPlane2, EGStrip2, any conic arc (EGCircularArc2, EGEllipticArc2, EGParabolicArc2, EGHyperbolicArc2), or any EGPolygon — EGTriangle, EGQuadrilateral, EGStraightNgon, EGCircularSector2, EGCircularSegment2, EGAnnularSector2, EGInterstice2, EGCurvilinearTriangle2, EGCurvilinearQuadrilateral2, EGCurvilinearNgon2) to the current path of an active Luxor Drawing, using Luxor.jl's own primitives. This is a package extension: EuclideanGeometry doesn't depend on Luxor, but methods for path become available as soon as both packages are loaded (using EuclideanGeometry, Luxor).\n\nDeliberately thin: path never touches color, fill, or labels — that's already what Luxor's own sethue, setopacity, label, etc. do well. Every method accepts action (:path by default, meaning \"add to the current path and do nothing else\" — pass :stroke, :fill, :fillstroke or :clip to render/use it immediately, exactly as Luxor's own shape functions do) plus whatever shape-specific keywords the curve needs (extend for the unbounded EGLine/EGRay, srange/n for EGParabola2, trange/n/branch for EGHyperbola2, as/radius for EGAngle2, n for EGEllipticArc2/EGParabolicArc2/EGHyperbolicArc2 and for any EGPolygon with such a side). EGSegment/EGLine/EGRay also take as=:arrow to draw as an arrow instead of a plain line — the one exception to the \"just adds to the path\" rule, since it draws immediately (action is ignored in that case). See Drawing with Luxor.jl for the full per-type reference.\n\nNote: EGBoundingBox is ambiguous when both packages are loaded with using (Luxor has its own BoundingBox type) — write EuclideanGeometry.EGBoundingBox explicitly when you mean this package's.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.path"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.pedal_triangle-Tuple{EGTriangle, EGPoint}","page":"API Reference","text":"pedal_triangle(t::EGTriangle, p::EGPoint)\n\nThe pedal triangle of p with respect to t: the feet of the perpendiculars from p to the three side-lines of t. Generalizes orthic_triangle (pedal triangle of the orthocenter) and contact_triangle (pedal triangle of the incenter).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.pedal_triangle"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.perimeter-Tuple{EGPolygon}","page":"API Reference","text":"perimeter(p::EGPolygon)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.perimeter"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.perpendicular_bisector-Tuple{EGPoint, EGPoint}","page":"API Reference","text":"perpendicular_bisector(a::EGPoint, b::EGPoint)\nperpendicular_bisector(s::EGSegment)\n\nThe perpendicular bisector (mediatrix) of segment [a, b].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.perpendicular_bisector"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.perpendicular_through-Tuple{EGLine, EGPoint}","page":"API Reference","text":"perpendicular_through(l::EGLine, p::EGPoint)\n\nThe line through p perpendicular to l.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.perpendicular_through"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.point_in_polygon-Tuple{EGPoint, EGPolygon}","page":"API Reference","text":"point_in_polygon(p::EGPoint, pg::EGPolygon)\n\nWhether p lies inside pg, via ray-casting (even-odd rule). Points exactly on the boundary may return either true or false.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.point_in_polygon"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.point_on_arc-Tuple{EGCircularArc2, Real}","page":"API Reference","text":"point_on_arc(arc, t::Real)\n\nThe point on arc at parameter t (t = 0 gives arc.p1, t = 1 gives arc.p2) — defined for EGCircularArc2, EGEllipticArc2, EGParabolicArc2 and EGHyperbolicArc2.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.point_on_arc"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.point_on_ellipse-Tuple{EGEllipse2, Real}","page":"API Reference","text":"point_on_ellipse(e::EGEllipse2, t::Real)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.point_on_ellipse"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.point_on_hyperbola-Tuple{EGHyperbola2, Real}","page":"API Reference","text":"point_on_hyperbola(h::EGHyperbola2, t::Real; branch::Int=1)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.point_on_hyperbola"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.point_on_parabola-Tuple{EGParabola2, Real}","page":"API Reference","text":"point_on_parabola(par::EGParabola2, s::Real)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.point_on_parabola"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.polar_line-Tuple{EGCircle2, EGPoint}","page":"API Reference","text":"polar_line(c::EGCircle2, p::EGPoint; atol=1e-9)\n\nThe polar line of p with respect to c: the line through inversion(p, c) perpendicular to the line from c.center to p. When p is outside c, this is the chord of contact of the two tangent lines from p (see tangent_points); when p is on c, it's the tangent line at p. nothing when p is c's center (matching the EGEllipse2/EGHyperbola2/ EGParabola2 methods, once ported), rather than throwing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.polar_line"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.polar_line-Tuple{EGEllipse2, EGPoint}","page":"API Reference","text":"polar_line(e::EGEllipse2, p::EGPoint; atol=1e-9)\n\nThe polar line of p with respect to e. When p is outside e, this is the chord of contact of the two tangent lines from p; when p is on e, it's the tangent line at p. nothing when p is e's center (its polar is the line at infinity).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.polar_line"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.polar_line-Tuple{EGHyperbola2, EGPoint}","page":"API Reference","text":"polar_line(h::EGHyperbola2, p::EGPoint; atol=1e-9)\n\nThe polar line of p with respect to h (see polar_line(::EGEllipse2, ::EGPoint)). nothing when p is h's center.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.polar_line"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.polar_line-Tuple{EGParabola2, EGPoint}","page":"API Reference","text":"polar_line(par::EGParabola2, p::EGPoint; atol=1e-9)\n\nThe polar line of p with respect to par (see polar_line(::EGEllipse2, ::EGPoint)). nothing when par's focus is on its directrix (degenerate parabola).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.polar_line"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.polar_point-Tuple{Real, Real, EGPoint{2}}","page":"API Reference","text":"polar_point(r, angle, center::EGPoint)\n\nThe point at distance r from center, at angle radians counterclockwise from the positive x-axis: center + (r*cos(angle), r*sin(angle)).\n\nNo zero-argument default for center here (unlike the Point2-based polar_point, which already claims the (r::Real, angle::Real) default-elided fallback — see rotation_map for why an EG-typed default would collide with it): pass center explicitly.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.polar_point"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.polar_point_deg-Tuple{Real, Real, EGPoint{2}}","page":"API Reference","text":"polar_point_deg(r, angle, center::EGPoint)\n\nLike polar_point, but angle is given in degrees.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.polar_point_deg"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.pole-Tuple{EGCircle2, EGLine}","page":"API Reference","text":"pole(c::EGCircle2, l::EGLine)\n\nThe pole of l with respect to c: the point whose polar line (see polar_line) is l. Throws an ArgumentError if l passes through c.center (its pole would be the point at infinity).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.pole"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.poncelet_point-Tuple{EGTriangle, EGPoint}","page":"API Reference","text":"poncelet_point(t::EGTriangle, p::EGPoint; atol=1e-9)\n\nThe Poncelet point of t and a 4th point p: for any 4 points in general position, the nine-point circles of the 4 triangles formed by leaving out one of them always share a common point.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.poncelet_point"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.power_of_point-Tuple{EGPoint, EGCircle2}","page":"API Reference","text":"power_of_point(p::EGPoint, c::EGCircle2)\n\nThe power of p with respect to c: distance(p, c.center)^2 - c.r^2. Negative inside c, zero on c, positive outside.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.power_of_point"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.projection-Tuple{EGPoint, EGLine}","page":"API Reference","text":"projection(p::EGPoint, l::EGLine)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.projection"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.radical_axis-Tuple{EGCircle2, EGCircle2}","page":"API Reference","text":"radical_axis(c1::EGCircle2, c2::EGCircle2; atol=1e-9)\n\nThe radical axis of c1 and c2: the line of points with equal power with respect to both circles, perpendicular to the line joining their centers. Defined even when the circles don't intersect (it coincides with intersection(c1, c2) when they do, and with perpendicular_bisector of the centers when c1.r == c2.r).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.radical_axis"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.radical_center-Tuple{EGCircle2, EGCircle2, EGCircle2}","page":"API Reference","text":"radical_center(c1::EGCircle2, c2::EGCircle2, c3::EGCircle2)\n\nThe radical center of c1, c2 and c3: the common point of their three pairwise radical axes (they always concur, unless the three centers are collinear).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.radical_center"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.radical_circle-Tuple{EGCircle2, EGCircle2, EGCircle2}","page":"API Reference","text":"radical_circle(c1::EGCircle2, c2::EGCircle2, c3::EGCircle2)\n\nThe radical circle of c1, c2 and c3: centered at their radical_center, orthogonal to all three (its radius squared equals their common power with respect to the radical center). Throws an ArgumentError if that power is negative (the radical center lies inside the circles, so no real orthogonal circle exists).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.radical_circle"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.rectangle_on_segment-Tuple{EGPoint, EGPoint, Real}","page":"API Reference","text":"rectangle_on_segment(a::EGPoint, b::EGPoint, height::Real; ccw=true)\n\nThe rectangle with side [a, b] and the given height, built counterclockwise from a to b (ccw=false builds it on the other side).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.rectangle_on_segment"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.reflection-Tuple{EGAngle2, EGPoint}","page":"API Reference","text":"reflection(ang::EGAngle2, about::EGPoint)\nreflection(ang::EGAngle2, about::EGLine)\n\nReflect ang. Since EGAngle2 is an EGSet — the infinite wedge swept counterclockwise from a to b, not just a bare signed value — reflecting about an EGLine (a true mirror) swaps a/b so the result is a genuine mirror image of the wedge (exactly the same swap convention EGCircularArc2/EGEllipticArc2 use to stay a true mirror image rather than the complementary region); reflecting about an EGPoint (a point reflection) needs no swap. Consequently measure's sign is preserved by both — a point reflection and a mirror are both \"apply the same swap-or-not rule uniformly across the EG hierarchy\", rather than the sign-flip-under-mirror convention a bare rotation instruction would suggest.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.reflection"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.reflection-Tuple{EGHalfPlane2, EGPoint}","page":"API Reference","text":"reflection(hp::EGHalfPlane2, about::EGPoint)\nreflection(hp::EGHalfPlane2, about::EGLine)\n\nReflect hp's boundary. Reflecting about an EGPoint (a point reflection) is orientation-preserving, so side is unchanged. Reflecting about an EGLine (a true mirror) reverses orientation, so side flips — otherwise the reflected half-plane would represent the wrong side of its own (also reflected) boundary.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.reflection"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.reflection-Tuple{EGPoint, EGLine}","page":"API Reference","text":"reflection(p::EGPoint, l::EGLine)\n\nReflect p across the line l (axial symmetry).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.reflection"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.reflection-Tuple{EGPoint, EGPoint}","page":"API Reference","text":"reflection(p::EGPoint, about::EGPoint)\n\nReflect p through the point about (point symmetry).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.reflection"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.reflection-Tuple{EGVector, EGPoint}","page":"API Reference","text":"reflection(v::EGVector, about::EGPoint)\n\nPoint-reflect the direction v — simply -v, since a free vector has no position for about to act on.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.reflection"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.reflection-Tuple{EGVector{2}, EGLine}","page":"API Reference","text":"reflection(v::EGVector{2}, about::EGLine)\n\nReflect the direction v across about's own direction (axial symmetry); about's position is irrelevant, only its direction matters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.reflection"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.reflection_map-Tuple{EGLine{2}}","page":"API Reference","text":"reflection_map(l::EGLine)\n\nThe affine map reflecting across l. Equivalent to p -> reflection(p, l), but composable.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.reflection_map"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.reflection_triangle-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"reflection_triangle(t::EGTriangle)\n\nThe reflection triangle of t: each vertex reflected across its opposite side line (distinct from orthic_triangle, which projects onto the opposite side instead of reflecting across it).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.reflection_triangle"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.regular_polygon-Tuple{EGPoint, EGPoint, Integer}","page":"API Reference","text":"regular_polygon(center::EGPoint, vertex::EGPoint, n::Integer)\n\nThe regular n-gon centered at center with vertex as one of its vertices.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.regular_polygon"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.rotate","page":"API Reference","text":"rotate(s::EGStrip2, angle, center=EGPoint(0.0, 0.0))\nhomothety(s::EGStrip2, k, center=EGPoint(0.0, 0.0))\nreflection(s::EGStrip2, about)\n\nTransform both boundary lines pointwise. Since \"the band between two lines\" doesn't depend on either line's own orientation, no side-flipping bookkeeping is needed here (unlike EGHalfPlane2) — membership is recomputed fresh from the transformed lines every time.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.rotate"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.rotate-2","page":"API Reference","text":"rotate(p::EGPoint, angle, center=EGPoint(0.0, 0.0))\n\nRotate p by angle radians (counterclockwise) around center.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.rotate"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.rotate-3","page":"API Reference","text":"rotate(ang::EGAngle2, angle, center=EGPoint(0.0, 0.0))\nhomothety(ang::EGAngle2, k, center=EGPoint(0.0, 0.0))\n\nTransform ang pointwise (vertex, a and b). measure is preserved by both (a rotation or a homothety of any ratio, positive or negative, is orientation-preserving in 2D and never changes an angle's own signed measure).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.rotate"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.rotate-4","page":"API Reference","text":"rotate(hp::EGHalfPlane2, angle, center=EGPoint(0.0, 0.0))\nhomothety(hp::EGHalfPlane2, k, center=EGPoint(0.0, 0.0))\n\nTransform hp's boundary pointwise. side is unchanged by both: a rotation or a homothety of any ratio (positive or negative) is orientation-preserving in 2D, so \"left of the transformed boundary\" still corresponds to the same physical half-plane.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.rotate"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.rotate-Tuple{EGVector{2}, Real}","page":"API Reference","text":"rotate(v::EGVector{2}, angle::Real)\n\nRotate the direction v by angle radians (counterclockwise).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.rotate"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.rotation_map-Tuple{Real, EGPoint{2}}","page":"API Reference","text":"rotation_map(angle::Real, center::EGPoint)\n\nThe affine map rotating by angle radians (counterclockwise) around center. Equivalent to p -> rotate(p, angle, center), but composable.\n\nNo zero-argument default for center here (unlike rotate): a rotation_map(angle::Real) single-argument fallback already exists for the old Point2-based AffineMap family, and since center's type never appears in that auto-generated method's signature, an EG-typed default would collide with it — pass center explicitly instead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.rotation_map"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.second_brocard_point-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"second_brocard_point(t::EGTriangle)\n\nThe second Brocard point of t: the point Ω' such that ∠Ω'BA = ∠Ω'CB = ∠Ω'AC (the same Brocard angle as first_brocard_point). Constructed analogously, tangent at the other endpoint of each pair.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.second_brocard_point"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.second_fermat_point-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"second_fermat_point(t::EGTriangle)\n\nThe second Fermat point of t (Kimberling center X(14), also called the second isogonic center): constructed like fermat_point but with the three equilateral triangles erected inward instead of outward. Equal to the isogonal conjugate of one of the two isodynamic_points — and, like them, degenerate for an equilateral triangle.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.second_fermat_point"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.second_lemoine_circle-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"second_lemoine_circle(t::EGTriangle)\n\nThe second Lemoine (cosine) circle of t: centered at the symmedian point, with radius a*b*c / (a^2+b^2+c^2).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.second_lemoine_circle"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.side_of_line-Tuple{EGPoint, EGLine}","page":"API Reference","text":"side_of_line(p::EGPoint, l::EGLine)\n\n+1 if p is to the left of l (oriented from l.p1 to l.p2), -1 if to the right, 0 if p is on l.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.side_of_line"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.sides-Tuple{EGPolygon}","page":"API Reference","text":"sides(p::EGPolygon)\n\nThe sides of p, in order. Default: straight EGSegments between consecutive vertices; concrete curved-region subtypes (from Phase 5 on) override this directly instead of implementing vertices.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.sides"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.sides-Tuple{EGQuadrilateral}","page":"API Reference","text":"sides(q::EGQuadrilateral)\n\nThe four side segments [a,b], [b,c], [c,d], [d,a].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.sides"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.simson_line-Tuple{EGTriangle, EGPoint}","page":"API Reference","text":"simson_line(t::EGTriangle, p::EGPoint)\n\nThe Simson line of p with respect to t: the line through the feet of the perpendiculars from p to the three side-lines of t. These feet are only guaranteed to be collinear when p lies on the circumcircle of t.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.simson_line"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.slope_angle-Tuple{Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"slope_angle(obj)\n\nThe angle (radians, from the positive x-axis) of obj's direction — any EGLine/EGRay/EGSegment.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.slope_angle"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.soddy_circles-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"soddy_circles(t::EGTriangle; atol=1e-9)\n\nThe two Soddy circles of t, as (inner=..., outer=...): build the three mutually tangent circles centered at the vertices with radii s-a, s-b, s-c (s the semiperimeter, see three_tangent_circles), then find their common tangent circles (the classical Apollonius CCC problem, tangent_circles) other than the three vertex circles themselves — the smaller one nestled between them is inner, the larger one enclosing them is outer.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.soddy_circles"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.soddy_line-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"soddy_line(t::EGTriangle; atol=1e-9)\n\nThe Soddy line of t: the line through the centers of its two soddy_circles (inner and outer).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.soddy_line"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.spieker_center-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"spieker_center(t::EGTriangle)\n\nThe Spieker center of t (the incenter of its medial triangle; barycentric coordinates (b+c) : (c+a) : (a+b)). Equal to midpoint(incenter(t), nagel_point(t)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.spieker_center"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.spieker_circle-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"spieker_circle(t::EGTriangle)\n\nThe Spieker circle of t: the incircle of its medial triangle.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.spieker_circle"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.square_inscribed-Tuple{EGTriangle, Integer}","page":"API Reference","text":"square_inscribed(t::EGTriangle, i::Integer)\n\nThe square inscribed in t with one side on [t[j], t[k]] (the side opposite t[i]) and its other two vertices on the sides through t[i]. Returns an EGQuadrilateral, its 4 vertices in order starting from t[j]. There are 3 such squares (one per side, hence the index i ∈ 1:3) — their common side length is a*h / (a+h), a the base length and h the corresponding height, independent of how oblique the triangle is.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.square_inscribed"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.square_on_segment-Tuple{EGPoint, EGPoint}","page":"API Reference","text":"square_on_segment(a::EGPoint, b::EGPoint; ccw=true)\n\nThe square with side [a, b], built counterclockwise from a to b (ccw=false builds it on the other side).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.square_on_segment"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.steiner_circumellipse-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"steiner_circumellipse(t::EGTriangle)\n\nThe Steiner circumellipse of t: the unique ellipse centered at centroid that passes through the three vertices of t (it is the ellipse of minimum area circumscribing t). Constructed via conic_through_points using the three vertices together with the reflections of two of them across the centroid (also on the ellipse, since it's centrally symmetric about its center).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.steiner_circumellipse"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.steiner_inellipse-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"steiner_inellipse(t::EGTriangle)\n\nThe Steiner inellipse of t: the unique ellipse centered at centroid that is tangent to the three sides of t at their midpoints (it is the ellipse of maximum area inscribed in t). Its foci are found via Marden's theorem: representing the vertices of t as complex numbers z1, z2, z3, the foci are the two roots of the derivative of (z-z1)*(z-z2)*(z-z3).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.steiner_inellipse"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.steiner_line-Tuple{EGTriangle, EGPoint}","page":"API Reference","text":"steiner_line(t::EGTriangle, p::EGPoint)\n\nThe Steiner line of p with respect to t: the line through the reflections of p across the three side-lines of t. These reflections are only guaranteed to be collinear (and the line only guaranteed to pass through the orthocenter) when p lies on the circumcircle of t — compare simson_line, whose feet of perpendiculars are the midpoints of p and each of these reflections.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.steiner_line"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.strip_width-Tuple{EGStrip2}","page":"API Reference","text":"strip_width(s::EGStrip2)\n\nThe perpendicular distance between s's two boundary lines.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.strip_width"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.symmedian_point-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"symmedian_point(t::EGTriangle)\n\nThe symmedian (Lemoine) point of t (barycentric coordinates a² : b² : c²).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.symmedian_point"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.tangent_circles-Tuple{EGCircle2, EGCircle2, EGCircle2}","page":"API Reference","text":"tangent_circles(c1::EGCircle2, c2::EGCircle2, c3::EGCircle2; atol=1e-9)\n\nCircle(s) tangent to c1, c2 and c3 — the classical Apollonius problem. Returns a Vector{EGCircle2{Float64}} with up to 8 solutions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.tangent_circles"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.tangent_circles-Tuple{EGCircle2, EGCircle2, EGLine}","page":"API Reference","text":"tangent_circles(c1::EGCircle2, c2::EGCircle2, l::EGLine; atol=1e-9)\n\nCircle(s) tangent to c1, c2 and l (the CCL Apollonius case). Returns a Vector{EGCircle2{Float64}} with up to 8 solutions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.tangent_circles"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.tangent_circles-Tuple{EGLine, EGLine, EGCircle2}","page":"API Reference","text":"tangent_circles(l1::EGLine, l2::EGLine, c::EGCircle2; atol=1e-9)\n\nCircle(s) tangent to l1, l2 and c (the CLL Apollonius case). l1 and l2 must not be parallel. Returns a Vector{EGCircle2{Float64}} with up to 4 solutions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.tangent_circles"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.tangent_circles_through_point-Tuple{EGCircle2, EGCircle2, EGPoint}","page":"API Reference","text":"tangent_circles_through_point(c1::EGCircle2, c2::EGCircle2, p::EGPoint; atol=1e-9)\ntangent_circles_through_point(l::EGLine, c::EGCircle2, p::EGPoint; atol=1e-9)\n\nCircle(s) tangent to c1/c2 (or to l/c), passing through p — the CCP/CLP Apollonius cases, solved by inverting about p. p must not lie on c1/c2 (or l/c). Returns a Vector{EGCircle2{Float64}} with up to 4 solutions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.tangent_circles_through_point"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.tangent_circles_through_point-Tuple{EGLine, EGLine, EGPoint}","page":"API Reference","text":"tangent_circles_through_point(l1::EGLine, l2::EGLine, p::EGPoint; atol=1e-9)\n\nCircle(s) tangent to both l1 and l2, passing through p. Returns a Vector{EGCircle2{Float64}} with up to 4 solutions (2 per bisector).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.tangent_circles_through_point"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.tangent_circles_through_points-Tuple{EGPoint, EGPoint, EGCircle2}","page":"API Reference","text":"tangent_circles_through_points(a::EGPoint, b::EGPoint, c::EGCircle2; atol=1e-9)\n\nCircle(s) passing through a and b and tangent (internally or externally) to circle c. Returns a Vector{EGCircle2{Float64}} with 0, 1 or 2 solutions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.tangent_circles_through_points"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.tangent_circles_through_points-Tuple{EGPoint, EGPoint, EGLine}","page":"API Reference","text":"tangent_circles_through_points(a::EGPoint, b::EGPoint, l::EGLine; atol=1e-9)\n\nCircle(s) passing through a and b and tangent to line l. Returns a Vector{EGCircle2{Float64}} with 0, 1 or 2 solutions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.tangent_circles_through_points"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.tangent_circles_with_radius-Tuple{EGLine, EGLine, Real}","page":"API Reference","text":"tangent_circles_with_radius(l1::EGLine, l2::EGLine, r::Real; atol=1e-9)\ntangent_circles_with_radius(l::EGLine, c::EGCircle2, r::Real; atol=1e-9)\ntangent_circles_with_radius(c1::EGCircle2, c2::EGCircle2, r::Real; atol=1e-9)\n\nThe circles of given radius r tangent to both l1/l2 (or l/c, or c1/c2). Returns a Vector{EGCircle2{Float64}} with up to 4 solutions (duplicates removed). If l1 and l2 are parallel, this always returns an empty vector: the only radius with solutions is r = distance(l1,l2)/2, for which there is a whole line of valid centers rather than finitely many circles, and that degenerate case is not handled.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.tangent_circles_with_radius"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.tangent_length-Tuple{EGCircle2, EGPoint}","page":"API Reference","text":"tangent_length(c::EGCircle2, p::EGPoint)\n\nLength of the tangent segment from p to c (0 if p is inside c).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.tangent_length"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.tangent_lines-Tuple{EGCircle2, EGPoint}","page":"API Reference","text":"tangent_lines(c::EGCircle2, p::EGPoint; atol=1e-9)\n\nThe line(s) through p tangent to c (0, 1 or 2 of them, see tangent_points). When p is on c, this is the single tangent line at p (perpendicular to the radius there) rather than the degenerate EGLine(p, p).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.tangent_lines"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.tangent_lines-Tuple{EGEllipse2, EGPoint}","page":"API Reference","text":"tangent_lines(e::EGEllipse2, p::EGPoint; atol=1e-9)\n\nThe line(s) through p tangent to e (see tangent_points). When p is on e, this is the single tangent line at p (its polar line) rather than the degenerate EGLine(p, p).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.tangent_lines"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.tangent_lines-Tuple{EGHyperbola2, EGPoint}","page":"API Reference","text":"tangent_lines(h::EGHyperbola2, p::EGPoint; atol=1e-9)\n\nThe line(s) through p tangent to h (see tangent_points). When p is on h, this is the single tangent line at p (its polar line) rather than the degenerate EGLine(p, p).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.tangent_lines"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.tangent_lines-Tuple{EGParabola2, EGPoint}","page":"API Reference","text":"tangent_lines(par::EGParabola2, p::EGPoint; atol=1e-9)\n\nThe line(s) through p tangent to par (see tangent_points). When p is on par, this is the single tangent line at p (its polar line) rather than the degenerate EGLine(p, p).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.tangent_lines"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.tangent_parallel-Tuple{EGCircle2, EGLine}","page":"API Reference","text":"tangent_parallel(c::EGCircle2, l::EGLine)\n\nThe two tangent lines to c that are parallel to l: the tangents at the two points where the diameter perpendicular to l meets c.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.tangent_parallel"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.tangent_points-Tuple{EGCircle2, EGPoint}","page":"API Reference","text":"tangent_points(c::EGCircle2, p::EGPoint; atol=1e-9)\n\nPoints of tangency on c of the lines from p tangent to c. Returns a Vector{EGPoint{2,Float64}} with 0 points (p strictly inside c), 1 point (p on c) or 2 points (p outside c).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.tangent_points"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.tangent_points-Tuple{EGEllipse2, EGPoint}","page":"API Reference","text":"tangent_points(e::EGEllipse2, p::EGPoint; atol=1e-9)\n\nPoints of tangency on e of the lines from p tangent to e, found by intersecting e with the polar line of p. Returns a Vector{EGPoint{2,Float64}} with 0 or 2 points (1 if p is on e; empty if p is e's center).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.tangent_points"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.tangent_points-Tuple{EGHyperbola2, EGPoint}","page":"API Reference","text":"tangent_points(h::EGHyperbola2, p::EGPoint; atol=1e-9)\n\nPoints of tangency on h of the lines from p tangent to h, found via p's polar line. Returns a Vector{EGPoint{2,Float64}} with 0 or 2 points (1 if p is on h).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.tangent_points"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.tangent_points-Tuple{EGParabola2, EGPoint}","page":"API Reference","text":"tangent_points(par::EGParabola2, p::EGPoint; atol=1e-9)\n\nPoints of tangency on par of the lines from p tangent to par, found via p's polar line. Returns a Vector{EGPoint{2,Float64}} with 0 or 2 points (1 if p is on par).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.tangent_points"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.tangential_triangle-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"tangential_triangle(t::EGTriangle)\n\nThe tangential triangle of t: its vertices are the pairwise intersections of the lines tangent to the circumcircle of t at each vertex.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.tangential_triangle"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.taylor_circle-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"taylor_circle(t::EGTriangle)\n\nThe Taylor circle of t: the common circle through the 6 taylor_points (any 3 of them already determine it).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.taylor_circle"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.taylor_points-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"taylor_points(t::EGTriangle)\n\nThe 6 points of the Taylor configuration, as a 6-tuple: project each vertex of the orthic_triangle onto each of the two sides of t not used to define it. All 6 are always concyclic, on the taylor_circle.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.taylor_points"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.thebault_circles-Tuple{EGTriangle, EGPoint}","page":"API Reference","text":"thebault_circles(t::EGTriangle, p::EGPoint; atol=1e-9)\n\nThe two Thébault circles of t for a point p on side [t[2], t[3]] (the side opposite t[1]): each is tangent to the cevian t[1] -> p, to the side [t[2], t[3]], and internally tangent to the circumcircle of t — one nestled against t[2], the other against t[3]. Returned as a named tuple (near_b=..., near_c=...). By the Sawayama–Thébault theorem, the incenter of t always lies on the segment joining their two centers. Built the same way as mixtilinear_incircle, by reusing tangent_circles (the CLL Apollonius case).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.thebault_circles"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.three_apollonius_circles-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"three_apollonius_circles(t::EGTriangle)\n\nThe three (vertex) Apollonius circles of t, as a 3-tuple (circ_a, circ_b, circ_c): circ_a is the locus of points whose distances to B and C are in the ratio AB:AC (so it passes through A), and likewise for circ_b (through B) and circ_c (through C). All three pass through the two isodynamic_points of t. Throws an ArgumentError for an equilateral triangle (each Apollonius circle then degenerates to a line).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.three_apollonius_circles"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.three_tangent_circles-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"three_tangent_circles(t::EGTriangle)\n\nThe three circles centered at the vertices of t, each with radius equal to the tangent length from that vertex to the incircle (s - a at the vertex opposite side a, etc., s the semiperimeter). Every pair is externally tangent — e.g. distance(A, B) == (s-a) + (s-b) == c — meeting exactly at the point where the incircle touches the side between them. These are the starting configuration soddy_circles is built from.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.three_tangent_circles"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.translate-Tuple{EGPoint, EGVector}","page":"API Reference","text":"translate(p::EGPoint, v::EGVector)\n\nTranslate p by v. The fourth member of the rotate/homothety/ reflection/translate quartet implemented across the whole package; unlike the other three, it takes no center (a translation has none).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.translate"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.translation_map-Tuple{EGPoint{2}}","page":"API Reference","text":"translation_map(v::EGPoint)\n\nThe affine map p -> p + v.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.translation_map"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.triangle_30_60_90_on_segment-Tuple{EGPoint, EGPoint}","page":"API Reference","text":"triangle_30_60_90_on_segment(a::EGPoint, b::EGPoint; ccw::Bool=true)\n\nThe right triangle with hypotenuse [a, b], angle 30° at a and 60° at b, built counterclockwise from a to b (ccw=false builds it on the other side).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.triangle_30_60_90_on_segment"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.trilinear_coordinates-Tuple{EGTriangle, EGPoint}","page":"API Reference","text":"trilinear_coordinates(t::EGTriangle, p::EGPoint)\n\nThe trilinear coordinates of p relative to t: the actual signed perpendicular distances (x, y, z) from p to sides a = [t[2],t[3]], b = [t[3],t[1]], c = [t[1],t[2]] (positive on the same side as the opposite vertex). For p inside t, all three are positive; for p on a side, the corresponding coordinate is 0.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.trilinear_coordinates"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.trilinear_point-Tuple{EGTriangle, Real, Real, Real}","page":"API Reference","text":"trilinear_point(t::EGTriangle, x, y, z)\n\nThe point with trilinear coordinates (x:y:z) relative to t (the values need not be normalized — trilinear coordinates are only meaningful up to a common scale factor, unlike the actual distances trilinear_coordinates returns). Converts to barycentric via (a*x : b*y : c*z), a/b/c being the side lengths opposite t[1]/t[2]/t[3].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.trilinear_point"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.trisector-Tuple{EGTriangle, Integer}","page":"API Reference","text":"trisector(t::EGTriangle, i)\n\nThe two rays from vertex t[i] trisecting the interior angle there into three equal parts (see angle_trisectors; this is the construction behind the morley_triangle).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.trisector"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.van_lamoen_circle-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"van_lamoen_circle(t::EGTriangle)\n\nThe Van Lamoen circle of t: the common circle through the 6 van_lamoen_points.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.van_lamoen_circle"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.van_lamoen_points-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"van_lamoen_points(t::EGTriangle)\n\nThe 6 circumcenters of the sub-triangles that the three medians of t cut it into (each an area formed by one vertex, one non-adjacent side midpoint, and the centroid), as a 6-tuple. A classical theorem states these 6 points are always concyclic — see van_lamoen_circle.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.van_lamoen_points"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.vertex-Tuple{EGParabola2}","page":"API Reference","text":"vertex(par::EGParabola2)\n\nThe vertex of par: the midpoint between its focus and the foot of the perpendicular from the focus to the directrix.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.vertex"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.vertices-Tuple{EGTriangle}","page":"API Reference","text":"vertices(p)\n\nThe vertices of a straight-sided EGPolygon (EGTriangle, EGQuadrilateral, EGStraightNgon), in order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.vertices"},{"category":"macro","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.@affinemap!-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"@affinemap! m begin ... end\n\nThe mutating counterpart of @affinemap — see @translate! for what \"mutating\" means for immutable shapes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.@affinemap!"},{"category":"macro","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.@affinemap-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"@affinemap m begin ... end\n@affinemap m c                # a single shape/expression also works\n\nApply the EGAffineMap m to every shape named in the block — see @translate for the full rundown of how the block is read and what it returns.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.@affinemap"},{"category":"macro","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.@boundingbox-Tuple{Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"@boundingbox begin\n    c = EGCircle2(...)\n    s = EGSegment(...)\n    t                     # a shape already defined earlier\nend\n@boundingbox c             # a single shape/expression also works\n\nThe bbox_union of every shape named in the block — via EGBoundingBox applied to each, then reduced with bbox_union — as a single expression. Each top-level line in the block is either:\n\nan assignment name = expr: expr is evaluated and bound to name exactly as if the @boundingbox weren't there (so name stays usable on later lines, or after the macro, exactly like ordinary code), and its value is folded into the union; or\na bare expression (most often just the name of a shape defined earlier, outside the block or on an earlier line inside it): its value is folded into the union, nothing is assigned.\n\nLines run in order, top to bottom, exactly as written — this is not a new scope (no let): a bare name refers to whatever name already means at that point, and an assignment defines name in the enclosing scope, so mixing \"build a new shape here\" and \"also include this shape from earlier\" freely on different lines works as expected.\n\nA single expression instead of a begin ... end block (@boundingbox c, or even @boundingbox c = EGCircle2(...)) works the same way, treated as a one-line block — EGBoundingBox(c) directly is simpler for that case, but this stays consistent rather than requiring begin/end only sometimes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.@boundingbox"},{"category":"macro","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.@homothety!-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"@homothety! k begin ... end\n@homothety! k center begin ... end\n@homothety! k c\n\nThe mutating counterpart of @homothety — see @translate! for what \"mutating\" means for immutable shapes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.@homothety!"},{"category":"macro","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.@homothety-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"@homothety k begin ... end\n@homothety k center begin ... end\n@homothety k c              # a single shape/expression also works\n\nhomothety every shape named in the block by ratio k (about center, defaulting to the origin exactly like homothety itself), returning them as a tuple — see @translate for the full rundown of how the block is read and what it returns.\n\n@homothety! k begin ... end\n@homothety! k center begin ... end\n\nThe mutating form — see @translate!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.@homothety"},{"category":"macro","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.@invert!-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"@invert! center begin ... end\n@invert! center k begin ... end\n\nThe mutating counterpart of @invert — see @translate! for what \"mutating\" means for immutable shapes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.@invert!"},{"category":"macro","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.@invert-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"@invert center begin ... end\n@invert center k begin ... end\n@invert center c              # a single shape/expression also works\n\ninvert every shape named in the block with respect to the circle centered at center (radius k, defaulting to 1.0 exactly like invert itself) — see @translate for the full rundown of how the block is read and what it returns. invert can change a shape's own type (an EGLine inverts to an EGCircle2 and vice versa) — no different here.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.@invert"},{"category":"macro","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.@invert_neg!-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"@invert_neg! center begin ... end\n@invert_neg! center k begin ... end\n\nThe mutating counterpart of @invert_neg — see @translate! for what \"mutating\" means for immutable shapes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.@invert_neg!"},{"category":"macro","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.@invert_neg-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"@invert_neg center begin ... end\n@invert_neg center k begin ... end\n@invert_neg center c\n\ninvert_neg every shape named in the block — the negative-ratio counterpart of @invert; see @translate for the full rundown of how the block is read and what it returns.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.@invert_neg"},{"category":"macro","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.@reflection!-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"@reflection! about begin ... end\n@reflection! about c\n\nThe mutating counterpart of @reflection — see @translate! for what \"mutating\" means for immutable shapes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.@reflection!"},{"category":"macro","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.@reflection-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"@reflection about begin ... end\n@reflection about c         # a single shape/expression also works\n\nreflection every shape named in the block about a point or a line, returning them as a tuple — see @translate for the full rundown of how the block is read and what it returns.\n\n@reflection! about begin ... end\n\nThe mutating form — see @translate!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.@reflection"},{"category":"macro","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.@rotate!-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"@rotate! angle begin ... end\n@rotate! angle center begin ... end\n@rotate! angle c\n\nThe mutating counterpart of @rotate — see @translate! for what \"mutating\" means for immutable shapes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.@rotate!"},{"category":"macro","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.@rotate-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"@rotate angle begin ... end\n@rotate angle center begin ... end\n@rotate angle c             # a single shape/expression also works\n\nrotate every shape named in the block by angle (about center, defaulting to the origin exactly like rotate itself), returning them as a tuple — see @translate for the full rundown of how the block is read (assignments vs. bare references) and what it returns.\n\n@rotate! angle begin ... end\n@rotate! angle center begin ... end\n\nThe mutating form — see @translate!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.@rotate"},{"category":"macro","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.@translate!-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"@translate! v begin ... end\n@translate! v c\n\nThe mutating counterpart of @translate — see its docstring for the full rundown (what \"mutating\" means for immutable shapes, and the ArgumentError on a bare unnamed expression).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.@translate!"},{"category":"macro","location":"api/#EuclideanGeometry.@translate-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"@translate v begin\n    c = EGCircle2(...)\n    s = EGSegment(...)\n    t                     # a shape already defined earlier\nend\n@translate v c             # a single shape/expression also works\n\ntranslate every shape named in the block by v, returning them as a tuple in order (C, S, T = @translate v begin ... end) — c/s/t themselves are untouched, exactly like calling translate by hand and keeping the result under a new name. Each top-level line is either an assignment name = expr (runs as ordinary code, and its value is translated into the result tuple) or a bare expression (most often the name of a shape defined earlier); see @boundingbox for the full rundown of that part, which works identically here.\n\n@translate! v begin ... end\n\nThe mutating form: instead of leaving c/s/t alone and returning copies, it rebinds each named one (an assignment or a bare existing variable) to its own translated value. EGCircle2/EGSegment/... are all immutable structs, so nothing is changed in place — the object itself never mutates, only the variable is repointed at a new one (the same trick Setfield.jl's @set! uses for immutable structs generally). A bare unnamed expression (nothing to rebind) is an ArgumentError in this form.\n\nWhen embedding a call to @translate/@rotate/@homothety/@reflection directly inside another expression — as an argument to a function, say — wrap it in its own parentheses: f((@rotate angle p), other_arg), not f(@rotate angle p, other_arg). Without them, Julia's bare @macro arg1 arg2 ... call syntax swallows the surrounding comma-separated arguments into an unwanted tuple; this is a general Julia parsing rule for any multi-argument macro call, not specific to these. A bare statement (x = @rotate angle p) never has this problem.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"EuclideanGeometry.@translate"},{"category":"section","location":"polygons/#Polygons-and-Bounding-Boxes","page":"Polygons & Bounding Boxes","text":"EGStraightNgon is an ordered list of vertices (no repeated closing point), assumed to describe a simple polygon (edges don't cross themselves). It's one member of the EGPolygon family — the same one EGTriangle and EGQuadrilateral belong to — so area, perimeter, centroid, is_convex and point_in_polygon are all defined once, generically, on EGPolygon itself, rather than reimplemented per type. This page also covers a separate, lighter-weight EGBoundingBox type for quick axis-aligned containment/overlap tests.","title":"Polygons & Bounding Boxes"},{"category":"section","location":"polygons/#Basic-measurements","page":"Polygons & Bounding Boxes","text":"using EuclideanGeometry\n\npg = EGStraightNgon([EGPoint(0.0, 0.0), EGPoint(4.0, 0.0), EGPoint(4.0, 3.0), EGPoint(1.0, 3.0)])\nvertices(pg)\n4-element Vector{EGPoint{2, Float64}}:\n [0.0, 0.0]\n [4.0, 0.0]\n [4.0, 3.0]\n [1.0, 3.0]\n\narea(pg)       # 10.5, shoelace formula\nperimeter(pg)  # sum of edge lengths\ncentroid(pg)   # area-weighted centroid — not the plain vertex average\n[2.238095238095238, 1.4285714285714286]\n\ncentroid weights by area rather than just averaging the vertices (a polygon with vertices clustered on one side but a large flat area on the other still gets the geometrically correct center of mass). For a (near-)zero-area degenerate polygon, where that weighting breaks down, it falls back to the plain vertex average instead.\n\nrotate, reflection and homothety all work on an EGStraightNgon too, transforming every vertex:\n\nrotate(pg, pi / 4, centroid(pg))\nhomothety(pg, 2.0)\nEGStraightNgon(EGPoint{2, Float64}[[0.0, 0.0], [8.0, 0.0], [8.0, 6.0], [2.0, 6.0]])","title":"Basic measurements"},{"category":"section","location":"polygons/#Convexity-and-containment","page":"Polygons & Bounding Boxes","text":"is_convex(pg)                                # true\npoint_in_polygon(EGPoint(2.0, 1.0), pg)        # true: strictly inside\npoint_in_polygon(EGPoint(5.0, 1.0), pg)        # false: strictly outside\nfalse\n\npoint_in_polygon uses the standard ray-casting (even-odd) rule, and works the same way for every EGPolygon — EGTriangle, EGQuadrilateral, or any curved region from Circles. Its one sharp edge: a point that lies exactly on the boundary can come back as either true or false, depending on which edge and in which direction — this is a well-known property of ray casting itself, not a bug, so don't rely on an exact boundary point going either way.","title":"Convexity and containment"},{"category":"section","location":"polygons/#Convex-hull","page":"Polygons & Bounding Boxes","text":"pts = [EGPoint(0.0, 0.0), EGPoint(4.0, 0.0), EGPoint(4.0, 3.0), EGPoint(1.0, 3.0), EGPoint(2.0, 1.0)]\nhull = convex_hull(pts)\nvertices(hull)\n4-element Vector{EGPoint{2, Float64}}:\n [0.0, 0.0]\n [4.0, 0.0]\n [4.0, 3.0]\n [1.0, 3.0]\n\nconvex_hull is Andrew's monotone chain algorithm (O(n log n), sorts the points then builds the lower and upper chains in one pass each), returning an EGStraightNgon. Points strictly inside the hull — like (2, 1) above — are simply dropped; duplicate input points are also removed before the sweep.","title":"Convex hull"},{"category":"section","location":"polygons/#Bounding-boxes","page":"Polygons & Bounding Boxes","text":"EGBoundingBox is a separate, minimal type — just two points, .min and .max — for when a full polygon is more machinery than you need (a quick overlap test, a viewport, a spatial-indexing key). It has constructors from a vector of points or directly from an EGSegment, EGTriangle, EGPolygon or EGCircle2:\n\nbb = EGBoundingBox(pg)          # same as EGBoundingBox(vertices(pg))\nbb.min, bb.max\n([0.0, 0.0], [4.0, 3.0])\n\nbbox_width(bb), bbox_height(bb)   # 4.0, 3.0\nbbox_center(bb)                   # (2.0, 1.5)\nbbox_diagonal(bb)                 # 5.0\nbbox_aspect_ratio(bb)             # width / height\n1.3333333333333333\n\nTranslating (+/- an EGPoint) and scaling (* a real number) an EGBoundingBox both return a new, still-valid EGBoundingBox — scaling by a negative factor still produces min <= max correctly, by re-sorting the two scaled corners rather than assuming the sign of the factor.\n\nUnlike everything in the EGPolygon family, EGBoundingBox deliberately sits outside the EGObject hierarchy's region branch and has no rotate/reflection methods: an arbitrary rotation or reflection wouldn't generally produce another axis-aligned box, only a rotated rectangle, which isn't what this type represents. Translation and uniform scaling about the origin are the only transforms that always preserve that invariant, so those are what's supported (+/-/* above, rather than rotate/reflection/homothety).\n\nEGPoint(1.0, 1.0) in bb   # containment, boundary included\nbb2 = EGBoundingBox(EGPoint(2.0, 1.0), EGPoint(6.0, 5.0))\nbboxes_intersect(bb, bb2)     # true: they overlap\nbbox_intersection(bb, bb2)    # the overlapping region itself\nEGBoundingBox([2.0, 1.0] .. [4.0, 3.0])\n\nbboxes_intersect treats touching (sharing just an edge or corner) as intersecting. bbox_intersection returns nothing instead of a degenerate box when they don't overlap at all.","title":"Bounding boxes"},{"category":"section","location":"polygons/#Named-polygon-constructors","page":"Polygons & Bounding Boxes","text":"A handful of constructors build common quadrilaterals and regular polygons directly, so they interoperate with everything above (area, is_convex, EGBoundingBox, ...) for free.\n\nFunction Builds Returns\nparallelogram the parallelogram through 3 consecutive vertices a, b, c (the 4th, d, is completed automatically) EGQuadrilateral\nsquare_on_segment the square with [a,b] as one side EGQuadrilateral\nrectangle_on_segment the rectangle with [a,b] as one side and a given height EGQuadrilateral\nregular_polygon the regular n-gon with a given center and one vertex EGStraightNgon\n\na, b, c = EGPoint(0.0, 0.0), EGPoint(4.0, 0.0), EGPoint(5.0, 2.0)\n\nparallelogram(a, b, c)          # 4th vertex completed as a + (c - b)\nsquare_on_segment(a, b)         # built counterclockwise from a to b by default\nrectangle_on_segment(a, b, 2.0) # same, with an explicit height instead of |a-b|\nregular_polygon(EGPoint(0.0, 0.0), EGPoint(1.0, 0.0), 6)  # a regular hexagon\nEGStraightNgon(EGPoint{2, Float64}[[1.0, 0.0], [0.5000000000000001, 0.8660254037844386], [-0.4999999999999998, 0.8660254037844387], [-1.0, 1.2246467991473532e-16], [-0.5000000000000004, -0.8660254037844385], [0.5000000000000001, -0.8660254037844386]])\n\nsquare_on_segment and rectangle_on_segment both take a ccw keyword (default true) to build on the other side of [a,b] instead — useful when the side you're extending from is itself part of a larger polygon and you need to stay outside (or inside) it consistently.","title":"Named polygon constructors"},{"category":"section","location":"polygons/#Quadrilaterals","page":"Polygons & Bounding Boxes","text":"EGQuadrilateral is a dedicated 4-vertex type (fields a, b, c, d, taken in order around the shape), distinct from a general EGStraightNgon — it exists because a quadrilateral has its own well-known named features (a pair of diagonals, a well-defined \"is it cyclic\" question) that don't generalize cleanly to arbitrary polygons.\n\nq = EGQuadrilateral(EGPoint(0.0, 0.0), EGPoint(4.0, 0.0), EGPoint(5.0, 3.0), EGPoint(1.0, 3.0))\nvertices(q)\n([0.0, 0.0], [4.0, 0.0], [5.0, 3.0], [1.0, 3.0])\n\narea, perimeter, is_convex and EGBoundingBox all work on an EGQuadrilateral exactly as they do on any other EGPolygon (internally, it's treated as the 4-vertex polygon [a,b,c,d] for these):\n\narea(q), perimeter(q), is_convex(q)\n(12.0, 14.32455532033676, true)\n\nsides(q)        # the 4 sides, as EGSegments a-b, b-c, c-d, d-a\ndiagonals(q)     # the 2 diagonals, as EGSegments a-c and b-d\ndiagonal_intersection(q)  # where the diagonals cross (nothing if they're parallel)\n[2.5, 1.5]\n\nis_cyclic(q)                     # false: no circle passes through all 4 vertices\npoint_in_polygon(EGPoint(2.0, 1.0), q)  # true: strictly inside\ntrue\n\nThere's no separate point_in_quadrilateral function — since EGQuadrilateral is a genuine EGPolygon rather than an unrelated struct, the one generic point_in_polygon/Base.in already covers it, along with every other member of the family.\n\nOne thing to watch: centroid of an EGQuadrilateral is the plain average of the 4 vertices (a+b+c+d)/4, not area-weighted like the generic centroid(::EGPolygon) — for a quadrilateral this plain average is itself a classical, well-defined point (sometimes called the \"vertex centroid\"), so it's kept simple rather than silently reproducing the polygon's weighted version under the same name.\n\nrotate, reflection and homothety transform a, b, c and d pointwise, same as for any other polygon:\n\nrotate(q, pi / 6)\nEGQuadrilateral([0.0, 0.0], [3.464101615137755, 1.9999999999999998], [2.8301270189221936, 5.098076211353316], [-0.6339745962155611, 3.098076211353316])","title":"Quadrilaterals"},{"category":"section","location":"tangency/#Tangency-and-Apollonius-Problems","page":"Tangency & Apollonius Problems","text":"The classical problem of Apollonius asks: given three objects, each either a point, a line or a circle, find the circle(s) tangent to all three (a point counts as a \"circle\" of radius zero to be tangent to, i.e. passing through it). There are ten combinations up to symmetry (PPP, LPP, LLP, CPP, CLP, CCP, LLL, CLL, CCL, CCC); this package covers the ones that involve at least one circle or line (PPP is just circumcircle of an EGTriangle, and LLL is incenter/excenters).\n\nThe functions are named by what role the points among the three objects play:\n\ntangent_circles_through_points — two of the three objects are points the circle must pass through; the third is a line or a circle it must be tangent to.\ntangent_circles_through_point — one point to pass through, and two lines/circles to be tangent to.\ntangent_circles — no points at all: two or three lines/circles, all to be tangent to.\n\nEvery one of these can return multiple solutions (up to 8, for three circles), so they all return a Vector{EGCircle2} — never a single EGCircle2. The given circles themselves are always excluded from the result, even when the configuration is symmetric enough that one of them would otherwise satisfy the tangency equations too (a circle is degenerately \"tangent\" to itself in that sense).","title":"Tangency & Apollonius Problems"},{"category":"section","location":"tangency/#Through-two-points,-tangent-to-a-line","page":"Tangency & Apollonius Problems","text":"In general there are up to two such circles — but whenever a and b are placed symmetrically about the perpendicular from their midpoint to l (as here), one of the two degenerates to infinite radius (a straight line), leaving exactly one genuine circle:\n\nusing EuclideanGeometry\n\na, b = EGPoint(-3.0, 0.0), EGPoint(3.0, 0.0)\nl = EGLine(EGPoint(-5.0, -4.0), EGPoint(5.0, -4.0))\n\nsols = tangent_circles_through_points(a, b, l)\nlength(sols)   # exactly 1, in this symmetric case\n1\n\nThe circle's center lies on the perpendicular bisector of [a,b] and it touches the line from above. A less symmetric choice of a, b and l generally gives two such circles — often of very different sizes, since one tends to hug the line closely while the other bulges around to reach it from the far side.","title":"Through two points, tangent to a line"},{"category":"section","location":"tangency/#Tangent-to-three-circles","page":"Tangency & Apollonius Problems","text":"With three circles and no points at all, this is Apollonius' original problem — up to 8 solutions, since each of the three tangencies can independently be internal or external.\n\nR = 3.0\ncenters = [EGPoint(R * cos(pi / 2 + 2pi * k / 3), R * sin(pi / 2 + 2pi * k / 3)) for k in 0:2]\ngiven = [EGCircle2(centers[k+1], 1.0) for k in 0:2]\n\nsols3 = tangent_circles(given[1], given[2], given[3])\nlength(sols3)   # 8 solutions\n8\n\nTwo of those eight happen to be concentric with the given configuration by symmetry here — a small one nested between the three circles, and a large one enclosing all of them:\n\nsmall = sols3[argmin(s.r for s in sols3)]\nbig = sols3[argmax(s.r for s in sols3)]\nEGCircle2([0.0, 0.0], 4.0)","title":"Tangent to three circles"},{"category":"section","location":"tangency/#Interstices:-the-gap-between-three-tangent-circles","page":"Tangency & Apollonius Problems","text":"Three mutually tangent circles (each pair, not just each with a third common circle) leave a curvilinear-triangle-shaped gap between them — called an interstice in this context (the same term used for the gaps of an Apollonian gasket). interstices returns it (or them — see below) as a Vector{EGInterstice2}, each one bounded by three EGCircularArc2s, one per circle:\n\nc1 = EGCircle2(EGPoint(0.0, 0.0), 40.0)\nc2 = EGCircle2(EGPoint(90.0, 0.0), 50.0)   # tangent to c1: distance 90 == 40 + 50\nc3_center = intersection(EGCircle2(c1.center, c1.r + 35.0), EGCircle2(c2.center, c2.r + 35.0))[1]\nc3 = EGCircle2(c3_center, 35.0)            # tangent to both c1 and c2\n\ngaps = interstices(c1, c2, c3)\nlength(gaps)   # 1: an externally tangent \"chain\" of 3 has exactly one gap\n1\n\nIt's built by reusing tangent_circles(c1, c2, c3) above: every genuine interstice has an inscribed circle tangent to all three (one of the CCC solutions) that never encloses any of them — unlike the \"big\" solution of a chain like this one, which contains all three and isn't a curvilinear triangle of these three circles at all (see soddy_circles for that pairing, inner and outer, given a name).\n\nA second kind of configuration is possible: if one circle contains the other two (each internally tangent to it, and externally tangent to each other), the two inner circles' own tangency point splits the gap into two separate interstices instead of one:\n\nR = 100.0\nbig = EGCircle2(EGPoint(0.0, 0.0), R)\nA = EGCircle2(polar_point_deg(R - 25.0, 100.0, big.center), 25.0) # inside big, tangent to it\nB_center = intersection(EGCircle2(big.center, R - 45.0), EGCircle2(A.center, A.r + 45.0))[1]\nB = EGCircle2(B_center, 45.0)                                       # inside big, tangent to it and to A\n\nlength(interstices(big, A, B))   # 2\n2\n\ninterstices figures out which of these two cases applies (and in the second case, correctly splits the gap in two) on its own — the order c1, c2, c3 are given in never matters. It throws an ArgumentError if the three circles aren't all pairwise tangent to begin with.\n\nAn EGInterstice2's area is computed via a Green's-theorem line integral around its three arcs rather than a \"straight triangle plus or minus circular segments\" formula — the latter needs an extra inside/outside decision per arc that breaks down for very unequal arc sizes (as in the nested case above), while the line integral doesn't:\n\narea(gaps[1])\nperimeter(gaps[1])   # the three arcs' arc_length, summed\n128.55948082738038\n\nrotate, reflection and homothety all work on an EGInterstice2 too — each of the three arcs transforms on its own, and (since each does so correctly regardless of orientation, see reflection(::EGCircularArc2, _) above) they still connect end to end afterward into the transformed gap.","title":"Interstices: the gap between three tangent circles"},{"category":"section","location":"tangency/#Through-a-point,-tangent-to-two-objects","page":"Tangency & Apollonius Problems","text":"tangent_circles_through_point covers the remaining \"one point + two circles/lines\" cases (LLP, CCP, CLP). For example, tangent to two circles and passing through a chosen point between them:\n\nc1 = EGCircle2(EGPoint(-4.0, 0.0), 1.5)\nc2 = EGCircle2(EGPoint(4.0, 0.0), 1.5)\np = EGPoint(0.0, 1.0)\n\nsols_p = tangent_circles_through_point(c1, c2, p)\nlength(sols_p)\n4","title":"Through a point, tangent to two objects"},{"category":"section","location":"tangency/#Related-helpers","page":"Tangency & Apollonius Problems","text":"offset_line shifts a line by a signed distance — the tool these constructions use internally to turn \"tangent to a line at distance r\" into \"passes through\" the offset line, and occasionally useful directly.\ntangent_circles_with_radius fixes the radius of the solution in advance and asks for circles of exactly that radius tangent to two given lines or a line and a circle.\nexternal_similitude_center / internal_similitude_center and the common tangent lines from Circles are the two-circle building blocks these constructions are built from.\nThe triangle-specific tangent-circle configurations — mixtilinear_incircle, soddy_circles, three_tangent_circles and thebault_circles — are covered in Triangles & Triangle Centers, since they are defined in terms of a triangle's sides and angles rather than arbitrary circles.","title":"Related helpers"},{"category":"section","location":"triangles/#Triangles-and-Triangle-Centers","page":"Triangles & Triangle Centers","text":"EGTriangle is three points, indexed t[1], t[2], t[3] (its own type, <: EGPolygon — see EuclideanGeometry.jl for the full hierarchy). This page is organized the same way triangle geometry usually is: the basic measurements, the four classical centers and how the Euler line ties three of them together, the excircles, barycentric coordinates, and then the (long) catalogue of further named centers and derived triangles that classical triangle geometry has accumulated.\n\nThroughout, the running example is the scalene triangle:\n\nusing EuclideanGeometry\n\nA, B, C = EGPoint(0.0, 0.0), EGPoint(8.0, 0.0), EGPoint(3.0, 6.0)\nt = EGTriangle(A, B, C)\n\narea(t), perimeter(t), is_degenerate(t)\n(24.0, 22.518453608406023, false)","title":"Triangles & Triangle Centers"},{"category":"section","location":"triangles/#The-four-classical-centers,-and-the-Euler-line","page":"Triangles & Triangle Centers","text":"Function Center Defined as\ncentroid G average of the three vertices\ncircumcenter O equidistant from the three vertices (circumcircle passes through them)\nincenter I equidistant from the three sides (incircle touches them)\northocenter H intersection of the three altitudes\n\nG, O and H are always collinear — that line is the euler_line — and G divides OH in a fixed 1:2 ratio (H = O + 3(G - O)). I is not on the Euler line in general (it only coincides with the others for an equilateral triangle).\n\nG, O, I, H = centroid(t), circumcenter(t), incenter(t), orthocenter(t)\nel = euler_line(t)\nnpc = nine_point_circle(t)\nEGCircle2([3.5, 2.125], 2.1830311495716224)\n\nnine_point_circle passes through the three edge midpoints, the three altitude feet, and the three midpoints of segment [vertex, H] (nine points in total, though only the circle itself — not the nine points — is what the function returns). Its center, nine_point_center, is the midpoint of O and H, and it always sits on the Euler line too — its radius is exactly half the circumradius. euler_points returns that last group of three (the midpoints of [H, vertex]) directly, for when you need those specific points rather than just the circle.\n\neuler_points(t)\n([1.5, 1.25], [5.5, 1.25], [3.0, 4.25])\n\nTwo more lines are naturally associated with this configuration: orthic_axis, the radical axis of the circumcircle and the nine-point circle (always perpendicular to the Euler line), and brocard_axis, the line through O and the symmedian point — whose polar line with respect to the circumcircle is, in turn, the lemoine_axis.\n\northic_axis(t), brocard_axis(t), lemoine_axis(t)\n(EGLine([-5.399999999999997, 8.799999999999997] -> [-5.774999999999997, 8.299999999999997]), EGLine([4.0, 1.75] -> [3.2470588235294118, 2.2588235294117647]), EGLine([-13.380191693290733, 13.495207667731629] -> [-13.889015222702497, 12.742266491261041]))","title":"The four classical centers, and the Euler line"},{"category":"section","location":"triangles/#Excenters-and-excircles","page":"Triangles & Triangle Centers","text":"Where the incircle is tangent to all three sides from inside the triangle, each excircle is tangent to one side and to the extensions of the other two, from outside. There are three of them, one opposite each vertex — excenters and exradii return all three as a named tuple (A=..., B=..., C=...), and excircles the actual circles.\n\nex = excenters(t)\nexc = excircles(t)\n(A = EGCircle2([11.259226804203012, 6.958584852041318], 6.958584852041318), B = EGCircle2([-3.259226804203012, 5.273539746245172], 5.273539746245172), C = EGCircle2([4.551022871703643, -7.363709689994646], 7.363709689994646))","title":"Excenters and excircles"},{"category":"section","location":"triangles/#Barycentric-coordinates","page":"Triangles & Triangle Centers","text":"Every point of the plane can be written as a weighted average of the three vertices, p = αA + βB + γC with α+β+γ = 1 — these weights (α,β,γ) are its barycentric coordinates with respect to t. barycentric_point goes from coordinates to a point (weights don't need to be normalized — they're rescaled internally); barycentric_coordinates goes the other way.\n\nbarycentric_point(t, 1.0, 1.0, 1.0)     # (1:1:1) is always the centroid\nbarycentric_coordinates(t, centroid(t)) # (1/3, 1/3, 1/3)\n(0.3333333333333334, 0.3333333333333333, 0.3333333333333333)\n\nMost of the named centers below have simple, well-known barycentric coordinates (the incenter is (a:b:c) in the side lengths, for instance) — barycentric_point is the easiest way to construct a center directly from a formula found in a reference like the Encyclopedia of Triangle Centers, without rederiving it in Cartesian coordinates.","title":"Barycentric coordinates"},{"category":"section","location":"triangles/#Trilinear-coordinates","page":"Triangles & Triangle Centers","text":"Trilinear coordinates (x:y:z) are the other classical coordinate system for a triangle: unlike barycentric weights, they're proportional to the actual signed perpendicular distances from the point to the three sides. trilinear_coordinates returns those distances directly (not just up to a common scale); trilinear_point goes the other way, from a trilinear ratio to a point (internally converting to barycentric via (a·x : b·y : c·z)).\n\nx, y, z = trilinear_coordinates(t, incenter(t))\nx, y, z, inradius(t)   # x == y == z == inradius(t): (1:1:1) is always the incenter\n(2.1315850917081556, 2.1315850917081556, 2.1315850917081556, 2.1315850917081556)\n\ntrilinear_point(t, 1.0, 1.0, 1.0) ≈ incenter(t)\ntrue\n\nMany named centers found in a reference like the Encyclopedia of Triangle Centers are given as trilinears rather than barycentrics — kenmotu_point below is one example — so having both conversions on hand avoids rederiving one from the other by hand.","title":"Trilinear coordinates"},{"category":"section","location":"triangles/#Vertex-indexed-lines","page":"Triangles & Triangle Centers","text":"Four of the classical triangle lines — the altitude, median, and internal and external angle bisectors — plus the perpendicular bisector of the opposite side and the angle trisectors, all come in threes, one per vertex. Rather than one function per vertex, each is a single function taking the triangle and a vertex index i ∈ {1,2,3}:\n\nFunction The line through t[i]...\naltitude(t, i) ...perpendicular to the opposite side (concurs at orthocenter)\nmedian(t, i) ...through the opposite side's midpoint (concurs at centroid)\nbisector(t, i) ...the internal angle bisector (concurs at incenter)\nbisector_ext(t, i) ...the external angle bisector (perpendicular to bisector(t, i))\nmediator(t, i) the perpendicular bisector of the side opposite t[i] (concurs at circumcenter)\ntrisector(t, i) the 2 rays from t[i] trisecting the interior angle there\n\naltitude(t, 1), median(t, 1)\n(EGLine([0.0, 0.0] -> [-6.0, -5.0]), EGLine([0.0, 0.0] -> [5.5, 3.0]))\n\non_line(orthocenter(t), altitude(t, 1)), on_line(centroid(t), median(t, 1))\n(true, true)\n\nThe free function angle_trisectors(vertex, p1, p2) (see Angles on the previous page) is what trisector(t, i) is built from; use it directly when the two rays don't come from a triangle's own vertices.","title":"Vertex-indexed lines"},{"category":"section","location":"triangles/#Further-named-centers","page":"Triangles & Triangle Centers","text":"Beyond the classical four, this package implements a large catalogue of triangle centers and associated circles from classical triangle geometry. Two, as a sample:\n\nNa = nagel_point(t)     # point where the excircle-tangency cevians meet\nGe = gergonne_point(t)  # point where the incircle-tangency cevians meet\n[3.280260799020241, 2.254676044407567]\n\nThe rest, grouped by what they're built from:\n\nGroup Functions\nCevian-intersection points nagel_point, gergonne_point, symmedian_point, fermat_point, second_fermat_point\nConjugates of a point isogonal_conjugate, isotomic_conjugate\nComplement/anticomplement of a point complement, anticomplement\nBuilt from the incenter/excenters mittenpunkt, spieker_center, spieker_circle, bevan_point, de_longchamps_point\nOn the circumcircle or Euler line feuerbach_point, feuerbach_points, isodynamic_points\nBrocard configuration first_brocard_point, second_brocard_point, brocard_angle, brocard_circle, brocard_midpoint\nCongruent-squares / conjugate-of-classical-center points kenmotu_point/kenmotu_circle, macbeath_point\nA 4th point's common nine-point-circle point poncelet_point\nPedal-type constructions simson_line, steiner_line, orthopole, pedal_triangle\nNamed auxiliary circles conway_points/conway_circle, taylor_points/taylor_circle, first_lemoine_points/first_lemoine_circle/second_lemoine_circle, van_lamoen_points/van_lamoen_circle\nMutually tangent circle systems mixtilinear_incircle, three_tangent_circles/soddy_circles/soddy_line, thebault_circles\nApollonius circles of a triangle three_apollonius_circles (one per vertex; their common points are the isodynamic_points)\nAxis-like lines fermat_axis (through fermat_point and second_fermat_point); see also orthic_axis/brocard_axis/lemoine_axis above and soddy_line\n\nAll of these take the EGTriangle as their (only, or first) argument and return an EGPoint, an EGCircle2, an EGLine, or — where there's naturally more than one, like soddy_circles or three_apollonius_circles — a tuple or named tuple. See the API Reference for exact signatures.\n\nsteiner_line(t, p) is closely related to simson_line(t, p): reflecting (rather than projecting) p across the three side-lines gives three points that are collinear exactly when p is on the circumcircle — and that Steiner line always passes through the orthocenter, parallel to the Simson line of the same point.\n\nbrocard_midpoint(t)   # midpoint of the two Brocard points (Kimberling X(39))\nkenmotu_point(t)       # X(371): common vertex of 3 congruent inscribed squares\nmacbeath_point(t)      # X(264): isotomic conjugate of the circumcenter\n[3.367484209662866, 2.251661061438766]\n\nsecond_fermat_point is built exactly like fermat_point but with the three equilateral triangles erected inward instead of outward; fermat_axis is the line through both:\n\nfermat_axis(t)   # EGLine(fermat_point(t), second_fermat_point(t))\nEGLine([3.2897184800774584, 2.2544606099920776] -> [-0.6060124097899183, 2.652887632819417])\n\nponcelet_point(t, p) uses a striking fact about any 4 points in general position (here, t's 3 vertices plus a 4th, p): the nine-point circles of the 4 triangles obtained by leaving out one point each time always share a single common point.\n\nponcelet_point(t, EGPoint(5.0, -2.0))\n[3.947058823529412, -0.011764705882353454]\n\nthebault_circles builds the two circles of the classical Sawayama–Thébault configuration: given a point p on side [t[2], t[3]], each is tangent to the cevian t[1] -> p, to that side, and internally tangent to the circumcircle — one nestled against t[2], the other against t[3].\n\nD = B + 0.4 * (C - B)   # a point on side [B, C]\nth = thebault_circles(t, D)\n(near_b = EGCircle2([5.371112263108153, -0.0859769259815697], 2.0746085878974125), near_c = EGCircle2([2.4345060682541315, 3.3019775631070742], 2.1616563458939564))\n\nRemarkably, no matter where p sits on the side, the incenter always lies exactly on the segment joining the two circles' centers (the theorem the construction is named for):\n\non_line(incenter(t), EGLine(th.near_b.center, th.near_c.center))\ntrue\n\nthree_tangent_circles is the simpler configuration soddy_circles is itself built from: one circle per vertex, radius equal to the tangent length from that vertex to the incircle, each pair meeting exactly where the incircle touches the side between them.\n\nbase = three_tangent_circles(t)\ndistance(base[1].center, base[2].center) ≈ base[1].r + base[2].r   # pairwise externally tangent\ntrue\n\ntaylor_points is the full 6-point construction behind taylor_circle: project each vertex of the orthic_triangle onto each of the two sides not used to define it — all 6 results are concyclic.\n\nlength(taylor_points(t))   # 6\n6\n\nvan_lamoen_points is another 6-concyclic-points theorem: the circumcenters of the 6 small triangles the 3 medians cut t into (each formed by one vertex, one non-adjacent side midpoint, and the centroid) always lie on a common circle, van_lamoen_circle.\n\nlength(van_lamoen_points(t))   # 6\n6\n\ncomplement(t, p) and anticomplement(t, p) are the homotheties of ratio -1/2 and -2 about the centroid — inverses of each other. Applied to t's own vertices they give the medial_triangle and the anticomplementary triangle (see Derived triangles) respectively:\n\ncomplement(t, A) ≈ midpoint(B, C)\ntrue\n\nkenmotu_circle is centered at the kenmotu_point, with radius √2·a·b·c / (4·Area + a²+b²+c²) — it passes through the 6 points where the three congruent inscribed squares of the Kenmotu configuration meet the sides.\n\nkenmotu_circle(t)\nEGCircle2([3.5187969924812026, 2.0751879699248117], 2.2284070439510018)\n\nfeuerbach_points is the excircle analogue of feuerbach_point: the nine-point circle is not just internally tangent to the incircle, it's also externally tangent to each of the three excircles, at these three points.\n\nfeuerbach_points(t)\n(A = [5.352914605817791, 3.2792670676871545], B = [1.5211275575918342, 3.0467856891260245], C = [3.740334969430836, -0.04476130080446383])\n\nkiepert_hyperbola is the unique rectangular hyperbola through t's 3 vertices, its centroid and its orthocenter (built directly via conic_through_points on those 5 points); kiepert_parabola has focus Kimberling center X(110) and directrix the euler_line (undefined for an isosceles triangle, where X(110) doesn't exist):\n\nkiepert_hyperbola(t) isa EGHyperbola2\nkiepert_parabola(t) isa EGParabola2\ntrue","title":"Further named centers"},{"category":"section","location":"triangles/#Steiner-ellipses","page":"Triangles & Triangle Centers","text":"Two more ellipses are naturally associated with a triangle, both centered at the centroid: the Steiner inellipse, the (unique, maximum-area) ellipse inscribed in the triangle and tangent to the sides at their midpoints, and the Steiner circumellipse, the (unique, minimum-area) ellipse through the three vertices. steiner_inellipse and steiner_circumellipse build them.\n\ninell = steiner_inellipse(t)\ncircumell = steiner_circumellipse(t)\ninell.center ≈ circumell.center ≈ centroid(t)\ntrue\n\nThe circumellipse is always exactly the image of the inellipse under a homothety of ratio -2 about the centroid — the same ratio that relates a triangle to its own medial_triangle:\n\ncircumell.a ≈ 2 * inell.a, circumell.b ≈ 2 * inell.b\n(true, true)\n\nsteiner_inellipse finds its foci via Marden's theorem: representing the vertices as complex numbers z1, z2, z3, the foci are the two roots of the derivative of (z-z1)(z-z2)(z-z3). steiner_circumellipse is built via conic_through_points, using the three vertices together with the reflections of two of them across the centroid (also on the ellipse, since any conic centered at a point is symmetric about it).","title":"Steiner ellipses"},{"category":"section","location":"triangles/#More-named-inellipses","page":"Triangles & Triangle Centers","text":"Beyond Steiner's, five more classical inscribed ellipses:\n\nFunction Center Foci\nlemoine_inellipse midpoint of centroid & symmedian point centroid, symmedian point\nbrocard_inellipse midpoint of the two Brocard points second/first Brocard point\nmacbeath_inellipse midpoint of circumcenter & orthocenter circumcenter, orthocenter (acute triangles only)\nmandart_inellipse mittenpunkt — (fit through 5 points instead, see below)\northic_inellipse symmedian_point — (acute triangles only)\n\nThe first three are bifocal ellipses (see Conics: Ellipse, Parabola & Hyperbola): each is pinned down by requiring it pass through one specific extra point (a vertex of a particular cevian triangle). mandart_inellipse and orthic_inellipse instead fit a conic through 5 points directly via conic_through_points: the 3 vertices of the extouch_triangle/orthic_triangle it's tangent to, plus the point-reflections of 2 of them through the known center (also on the ellipse, since it's centrally symmetric about its own center).\n\nmandart_inellipse(t).center ≈ mittenpunkt(t)\ntrue","title":"More named inellipses"},{"category":"section","location":"triangles/#Derived-triangles","page":"Triangles & Triangle Centers","text":"These take an EGTriangle and return another EGTriangle built from it:\n\nFunction Vertices are...\nmedial_triangle the three edge midpoints\nanticomplementary_triangle B+C-A, C+A-B, A+B-C — the inverse of medial_triangle (t is its medial triangle)\northic_triangle the three altitude feet\nreflection_triangle each vertex reflected across its opposite side line (unlike orthic_triangle, which projects instead)\ncontact_triangle the three points where the incircle touches the sides\nextouch_triangle the three points where the excircles touch the sides\nexcentral_triangle the three excenters\ntangential_triangle tangents to the circumcircle at each vertex, pairwise intersected\nnapoleon_triangle centers of equilateral triangles erected on each side (napoleon_point is its centroid — by Napoleon's theorem, always t's own centroid too)\nmorley_triangle intersections of adjacent angle trisectors\npedal_triangle projections of a chosen point onto the three sides\ncevian_triangle / circumcevian_triangle feet of cevians through a chosen point (extended to the circumcircle, for the latter)\n\nmt = medial_triangle(t)\nEGTriangle([5.5, 3.0], [1.5, 3.0], [4.0, 0.0])\n\nThe medial triangle is always similar to t at half scale, sharing its centroid — and its own circumcircle is exactly t's nine-point circle.","title":"Derived triangles"},{"category":"section","location":"triangles/#Inscribed-squares","page":"Triangles & Triangle Centers","text":"square_inscribed(t, i) builds the square with one side on the side opposite t[i] and its other two vertices exactly on the two sides through t[i] — returned as an EGQuadrilateral. There are 3 such squares (one per side, hence the vertex index):\n\nsq = square_inscribed(t, 1)\ndistance(sq[1], sq[2]), distance(sq[2], sq[3])   # equal: it really is a square\n(3.4393760040689854, 3.4393760040689854)\n\nIts side length is a*h / (a+h) (a the base length, h the corresponding height) regardless of how oblique the triangle is — a classical fact that falls out of simple similar-triangles reasoning once you set up coordinates with the base on an axis.","title":"Inscribed squares"},{"category":"section","location":"triangles/#Triangles-built-on-a-segment","page":"Triangles & Triangle Centers","text":"A different family of constructors, analogous to square_on_segment (see Polygons & Bounding Boxes): each takes a base segment [a, b] and returns the classical named triangle with that segment as one side (or, for the two \"hypotenuse\" ones, as the hypotenuse), plus a ccw::Bool=true keyword picking which side of [a, b] the third vertex falls on.\n\nFunction Triangle\nequilateral_triangle_on_segment equilateral, side [a, b]\nisosceles_triangle_on_segment isosceles, base [a, b], given leg length\ntriangle_30_60_90_on_segment right triangle, hypotenuse [a, b], angles 30°/60° at a/b\nisosceles_right_triangle_on_segment isosceles right triangle, hypotenuse [a, b] (via Thales' theorem)\ngolden_triangle_on_segment isosceles 72°-72°-36°, base [a, b]\ngolden_gnomon_on_segment isosceles 36°-36°-108° (the golden gnomon), base [a, b]\negyptian_triangle_on_segment 3-4-5 right triangle, [a, b] the \"4\" side, right angle at b\n\np1, p2 = EGPoint(0.0, 0.0), EGPoint(6.0, 0.0)\ngolden_triangle_on_segment(p1, p2)   # apex angle 36°, both base angles 72°\nEGTriangle([0.0, 0.0], [6.0, 0.0], [2.9999999999999996, 9.233050611525757])\n\negyptian_triangle_on_segment(p1, p2)   # legs 4:3 (here 6:4.5), hypotenuse 5 (here 7.5)\nEGTriangle([0.0, 0.0], [6.0, 0.0], [6.0, 4.5])","title":"Triangles built on a segment"},{"category":"section","location":"points_lines/#Points,-Lines-and-Rays","page":"Points, Lines & Rays","text":"These are the building blocks everything else in the package is made of.\n\nA point is EGPoint, and a vector (a free direction, with no fixed location) is the separate type EGVector. Both wrap the same (x, y) coordinate pair, but keeping them distinct means EGVector can carry its own meaning (a direction, e.g. what direction returns) without being confused for a location. Arithmetic between them stays permissive on purpose: EGPoint - EGPoint still gives back an EGPoint (not a vector), so familiar formulas like 2*about - p keep working exactly as before — see the table below.\nA segment is a finite piece of straight line between two points: EGSegment.\nA line (EGLine) is the infinite straight line through two points, and a ray (EGRay) is the half-line starting at an origin and passing through a second point. Both are small immutable structs holding the two defining points.\n\nAll four are part of the package's own EG-prefixed type hierarchy (see EuclideanGeometry.jl for the full tree): EGPoint/EGVector sit directly under EGObject, while EGSegment/EGLine/EGRay are all EGCurves.\n\nType Represents Fields\nEGPoint a point in the plane p[1], p[2] (x, y)\nEGVector a free direction/displacement v[1], v[2] (x, y)\nEGSegment the segment [p1, p2] s[1], s[2]\nEGLine the infinite line through p1, p2 .p1, .p2\nEGRay the half-line from origin through through .origin, .through\n\nNone of EGSegment/EGLine/EGRay carry a direction magnitude — an EGLine through p1 and p2 is the same object as the line through p2 and p1 — but EGSegment and EGRay do remember which of their two points comes first, since that is what makes them finite/one-sided in the first place.","title":"Points, Lines & Rays"},{"category":"section","location":"points_lines/#Creating-them","page":"Points, Lines & Rays","text":"using EuclideanGeometry\n\nA = EGPoint(3.0, 4.0)\nO = EGPoint(0.0, 0.0)\n\ns = EGSegment(O, A)     # the segment from O to A\nl = EGLine(O, A)        # the infinite line through O and A\nr = EGRay(O, A)         # the half-line starting at O, through A\n\ndistance(O, A)\n5.0\n\ndistance(O, A) is 5.0: with A = (3, 4), this is just the classic 3-4-5 triangle. EGSegment, EGLine and EGRay all wrap the same two points here — what differs is only how far they extend and what other functions are willing to do with them (e.g. on_segment only makes sense for an EGSegment, slope_angle treats EGLine and EGRay as pointing one way and an EGSegment as pointing from its first point to its second).","title":"Creating them"},{"category":"section","location":"points_lines/#Points-vs.-vectors","page":"Points, Lines & Rays","text":"A point minus a point is still a point, not a vector — A - O above stays an EGPoint. This is a deliberate choice: it keeps existing point-arithmetic formulas (like a reflection written as 2*about - p) working exactly as they read, without forcing every subtraction through a vector type first. Where a genuine direction is called for — the result of direction below, for instance — the package hands back an actual EGVector instead:\n\nd = direction(l)    # an EGVector, not an EGPoint\ntypeof(d)\nEGVector{2, Float64}\n\nBoth types support the same arithmetic and the standard linear-algebra functions norm, dot and normalize — re-exported from LinearAlgebra purely so using EuclideanGeometry alone is enough to get the full vector toolkit, without a separate using LinearAlgebra:\n\nnorm(d)        # 5.0\nnormalize(d)   # (0.6, 0.8): unit vector, same direction\ndot(d, EGVector(1.0, 0.0))   # 3.0\n3.0","title":"Points vs. vectors"},{"category":"section","location":"points_lines/#Polar-coordinates","page":"Points, Lines & Rays","text":"polar_point builds a point from a distance and an angle (radians, counterclockwise from the positive x-axis) instead of x/y — and polar_point_deg is the same with the angle in degrees. Both require an explicit center (there's no zero-argument default here, to avoid ambiguity with Real-only single-argument calls elsewhere in the package):\n\nBpolar = polar_point_deg(4.0, 40.0, O)   # 4 units out from O, at 40°\ndistance(O, Bpolar)                       # 4.0, regardless of the angle\n3.9999999999999996\n\nThis is exactly the point/angle/modulus relationship slope_angle and distance give you the other way around: polar_point(distance(O, p), slope_angle(EGLine(O, p)), O) recovers p (for p != O).","title":"Polar coordinates"},{"category":"section","location":"points_lines/#Direction,-slope-and-predicates","page":"Points, Lines & Rays","text":"direction(l)                    # (3.0, 4.0) — l.p2 - l.p1, as an EGVector\nrad2deg(slope_angle(l))         # ≈ 53.13°\nis_collinear(O, A, EGPoint(6.0, 8.0))\non_line(EGPoint(6.0, 8.0), l)\non_segment(EGPoint(6.0, 8.0), s) # false: beyond A\nfalse\n\nFunction Returns Meaning\ndirection EGVector non-normalized direction of an EGLine/EGRay/EGSegment\nslope_angle angle atan(dy, dx) of that direction, in radians\nis_collinear Bool do three points lie on a common line?\nis_parallel / is_perpendicular Bool relation between two lines\non_line / on_segment Bool is a point on this line / this finite segment?\nside_of_line -1, 0 or 1 which side of a line a point falls on\n\nl_horiz = EGLine(EGPoint(0.0, 0.0), EGPoint(4.0, 0.0))\nside_of_line(EGPoint(2.0, 1.0), l_horiz)   #  1 : \"above\"\nside_of_line(EGPoint(2.0, -1.0), l_horiz)  # -1 : \"below\"\n-1","title":"Direction, slope and predicates"},{"category":"section","location":"points_lines/#Projection,-reflection-and-the-perpendicular-foot","page":"Points, Lines & Rays","text":"projection drops a point onto a line at a right angle; reflection mirrors a point through another point (its use for mirroring across a line is: project first, then reflect through the foot).\n\nP, Q = EGPoint(1.0, 1.0), EGPoint(6.0, 3.0)\nl = EGLine(P, Q)\nC = EGPoint(2.0, 6.0)\n\nfoot = projection(C, l)      # the perpendicular foot of C on l\nCref = reflection(C, foot)   # C mirrored through that foot — i.e. across l\n[5.172413793103448, -1.931034482758621]","title":"Projection, reflection and the perpendicular foot"},{"category":"section","location":"points_lines/#Rotation-and-homothety","page":"Points, Lines & Rays","text":"rotate turns a point about a center by an angle (radians, counter-clockwise); homothety scales it by a factor k about a center (k = -1 is a point reflection, 0 < k < 1 shrinks towards the center). Both default to the origin when no center is given.\n\nrotate(C, pi / 2, P)     # C rotated 90° about P\nhomothety(C, 2.0, P)     # C scaled by 2 about P\nbarycenter([P, Q, C], [1.0, 1.0, 2.0])   # weighted average of the three\n[2.75, 4.0]","title":"Rotation and homothety"},{"category":"section","location":"points_lines/#Parallels,-perpendiculars-and-bisectors","page":"Points, Lines & Rays","text":"parallel_through(l, C)             # line through C, parallel to l\nperpendicular_through(l, C)        # line through C, perpendicular to l\npb = perpendicular_bisector(P, Q)  # perpendicular to [P,Q] through its midpoint\nEGLine([3.5, 2.0] -> [1.5, 7.0])\n\nangle_bisectors is the analogous construction for two intersecting lines: it returns both bisectors (they are always perpendicular to each other), as a 2-element vector.\n\nxaxis = EGLine(EGPoint(0.0, 0.0), EGPoint(4.0, 0.0))\nyaxis = EGLine(EGPoint(0.0, 0.0), EGPoint(0.0, 4.0))\nangle_bisectors(xaxis, yaxis)   # the two diagonals y = x and y = -x\n2-element Vector{EGLine{2, Float64}}:\n EGLine([0.0, 0.0] -> [1.0, 1.0])\n EGLine([0.0, 0.0] -> [1.0, -1.0])","title":"Parallels, perpendiculars and bisectors"},{"category":"section","location":"points_lines/#Angles","page":"Points, Lines & Rays","text":"Under the hood, angles between two vectors come from two free functions: angle_between(u, v) (signed, counterclockwise from u to v, in (-π, π]) and angle_at(vertex, p1, p2) (unsigned, in [0, π]) — use these directly when you just need a number and not something to pass around.\n\nangle_between(EGVector(4.0, 0.0), EGVector(0.0, 4.0))   # π/2\n1.5707963267948966\n\nEGAngle2 wraps up the \"angle at a vertex between two rays\" idea into a small struct (vertex, a, b) so it can be passed around and measured without repeating the three points everywhere — measure/abs on an EGAngle2 are exactly angle_between/angle_at on its two defining vectors. Unlike a bare number, it's also a genuine region of the plane (it's part of the EGSet family): p in ang tests whether p falls in the infinite wedge swept counterclockwise from a to b. It's oriented: EGAngle2(O, P1, P2) and EGAngle2(O, P2, P1) have the same abs but opposite sign.\n\nO = EGPoint(0.0, 0.0)\nP1, P2 = EGPoint(4.0, 0.0), EGPoint(0.0, 4.0)\nang = EGAngle2(O, P1, P2)\n\nrad2deg(measure(ang))    # 90.0 — signed, counterclockwise from a to b\nabs(ang)                 # 1.5707... — the unsigned angle, in [0, π]\nis_direct(ang)           # true: measure(ang) > 0\n(O + EGPoint(1.0, 1.0)) in ang   # true: inside the wedge\ntrue\n\nnormalized_measure is measure shifted into [0, 2π) instead of (-π, π], for when you'd rather not deal with negative angles. Swapping a and b flips the sign of measure (and is_direct) but leaves abs unchanged:\n\nang2 = EGAngle2(O, P2, P1)\nmeasure(ang2), is_direct(ang2)   # (-π/2, false) — same magnitude, opposite orientation\n(-1.5707963267948966, false)\n\nangle_trisectors is the free-function analogue of angle_bisectors: given a vertex and two points, it returns the two rays that cut ∠(p1, vertex, p2) into three equal parts.\n\nrays = angle_trisectors(O, P1, P2)   # 2 rays, each 30° apart (a 90° angle, /3)\n2-element Vector{EGRay{2, Float64}}:\n EGRay([0.0, 0.0] -> [3.464101615137755, 1.9999999999999998])\n EGRay([0.0, 0.0] -> [2.0000000000000004, 3.4641016151377544])\n\nrotate, reflection and homothety transform vertex, a and b pointwise, so abs(ang) is always preserved, and so is the sign of measure — for every one of them, including reflection. That's a deliberate consequence of EGAngle2 being a real region rather than a bare signed value: reflecting about an EGLine (a true mirror) swaps a and b internally so the wedge comes back as a genuine mirror image of the original (not its complement), and that swap is exactly what keeps measure's sign unchanged too — the same convention EGCircularArc2/EGEllipticArc2 use for the same reason.\n\nmeasure(rotate(ang, pi / 3)) ≈ measure(ang)                        # sign preserved\nmeasure(reflection(ang, EGPoint(1.0, 1.0))) ≈ measure(ang)         # point reflection\nmeasure(reflection(ang, EGLine(O, EGPoint(1.0, 1.0)))) ≈ measure(ang)  # line reflection too\ntrue","title":"Angles"},{"category":"section","location":"points_lines/#Harmonic-conjugate-and-the-golden-ratio-point","page":"Points, Lines & Rays","text":"Given A, B on a line and a third point P on that same line, harmonic_conjugate returns the point P' for which (A, B; P, P') is a harmonic range — i.e. P and P' divide [A,B] internally and externally in the same ratio. golden_ratio_point is the special point that divides [A,B] in the golden ratio, A + (B-A)/φ.\n\nA, B = EGPoint(0.0, 0.0), EGPoint(8.0, 0.0)\nP = EGPoint(2.0, 0.0)\n\nPgold = golden_ratio_point(A, B)     # ≈ (4.944, 0)\nPconj = harmonic_conjugate(A, B, P)  # (-4.0, 0) — P's harmonic conjugate\n[-4.0, 0.0]\n\nP' falls outside [A,B], on the opposite side from B: that is exactly what makes the division \"external\" for one of the two points and \"internal\" for the other, which is the defining property of a harmonic range.\n\nSee the API Reference for the complete, exhaustive list of functions, including a few not walked through here (like apollonius_circle, which needs a circle to make sense of, or is_concyclic).","title":"Harmonic conjugate and the golden ratio point"},{"category":"section","location":"affine_maps/#Affine-Maps","page":"Affine Maps","text":"EGAffineMap (<: EGTransform, deliberately outside the EGObject tree — see its own docstring) is a general 2D affine transformation, p -> A*p + t for a 2×2 matrix A (stored as four scalars, a11 a12 a21 a22) and a translation t = (tx, ty). It generalizes rotate, homothety, reflection and translate into a single reusable, composable object, rather than a one-off function call: build one once, apply it to as many points/shapes as you like, and combine several into one with plain function composition (∘).\n\nUnlike those four (each always orientation-preserving, or a straightforward orientation flip for reflection), an EGAffineMap can be a genuine shear or non-uniform scale — so it's also the place where a few subtleties specific to general affine transformations show up: circles becoming ellipses, conic arcs sometimes needing their endpoints swapped, and circular-arc regions becoming curvilinear. Each is covered below.","title":"Affine Maps"},{"category":"section","location":"affine_maps/#Creating-one","page":"Affine Maps","text":"The general constructor, affine_map, builds the unique affine map sending three given (non-collinear) source points to three chosen destination points — the 2D affine analogue of \"3 points determine a transformation\":\n\nusing EuclideanGeometry\n\nsrc = (EGPoint(0.0, 0.0), EGPoint(1.0, 0.0), EGPoint(0.0, 1.0))\ndst = (EGPoint(2.0, 3.0), EGPoint(5.0, 3.0), EGPoint(2.0, 7.0))\nm = affine_map(src, dst)\nEGAffineMap([3.0 0.0; 0.0 4.0], t=(2.0, 3.0))\n\nIt throws an ArgumentError if the three source points are (or are too close to) collinear, since then no unique affine map is determined (there are either none or infinitely many, depending on dst).\n\nMore often, though, one of the specific named constructors below is more direct than describing the map via three point correspondences:\n\nFunction Builds the affine map for...\ntranslation_map p -> p + v\nrotation_map p -> rotate(p, angle, center)\nhomothety_map p -> homothety(p, k, center)\nreflection_map p -> reflection(p, l)\n\nrotation_map and homothety_map both require center explicitly — there is no zero-argument default here, unlike rotate/homothety themselves (a default would collide with the single-argument form these two functions would otherwise auto-generate).\n\ntm = translation_map(EGPoint(3.0, -2.0))\nrm = rotation_map(pi / 2, EGPoint(1.0, 1.0))\nhm = homothety_map(2.0, EGPoint(1.0, 1.0))\nrefm = reflection_map(EGLine(EGPoint(0.0, 0.0), EGPoint(1.0, 0.0)))\nEGAffineMap([1.0 0.0; 0.0 -1.0], t=(0.0, 0.0))\n\nEach of these agrees exactly with its point-based counterpart — rotation_map(angle, center)(p) == rotate(p, angle, center), and likewise for the other three — the map versions exist purely so the transformation itself can be stored, composed and reused, rather than re-specifying angle/center (or k/center, or l) on every call.","title":"Creating one"},{"category":"section","location":"affine_maps/#Applying-and-composing","page":"Affine Maps","text":"An EGAffineMap is directly callable, and works pointwise on every other straight-edged type in the package:\n\nm(EGPoint(1.0, 0.0))                 # a single point\nt = EGTriangle(EGPoint(0.0, 0.0), EGPoint(1.0, 0.0), EGPoint(0.0, 1.0))\nm(t)                                 # a whole EGTriangle, vertex by vertex\nEGTriangle([2.0, 3.0], [5.0, 3.0], [2.0, 7.0])\n\nThe same works for EGSegment, EGLine, EGRay, EGStraightNgon, EGQuadrilateral, EGAngle2, EGHalfPlane2 and EGStrip2 — all pointwise, vertex by vertex (or endpoint by endpoint). It's also directly callable on an EGVector, applying the linear part only (no translation, since a vector has no position):\n\nm(EGVector(1.0, 0.0))\n<3.0, 0.0>\n\nEGHalfPlane2 needs one extra piece of bookkeeping: unlike rotate/ homothety (always orientation-preserving in 2D), a general affine map can reverse orientation, which would flip which side of the transformed boundary is \"inside\" — so its side field is recomputed fresh from an interior point, rather than carried over unchanged.","title":"Applying and composing"},{"category":"section","location":"affine_maps/#Conics:-type-is-preserved,-but-never-a-circle","page":"Affine Maps","text":"A circle, ellipse, hyperbola or parabola all stay their own conic type under any invertible affine map — this is a basic invariant (by Sylvester's law of inertia, the map acts on a conic's defining quadratic form as a congruence, which can't change how many of its eigenvalues are positive/negative/zero). Concretely:\n\nm(c::EGCircle2) always returns an EGEllipse2, never another EGCircle2 — only a similarity (rotation/homothety/reflection/ translation, or a combination) sends a circle to a circle, and this covers every affine map, so the return type doesn't special-case that.\nm(e::EGEllipse2) returns another EGEllipse2, m(h::EGHyperbola2) another EGHyperbola2, m(par::EGParabola2) another EGParabola2 — each computed exactly (no sampling or fitting): the conic's own quadratic form transforms as a congruence, which is diagonalized to recover the new semi-axes/angle directly (for the parabola, whose focus/directrix are metric rather than affine-invariant quantities, the new vertex/focal-parameter are instead recovered from its parametrization rewritten in the orthonormal frame aligned with the transformed axis direction).\n\nc = EGCircle2(EGPoint(1.0, 2.0), 5.0)\nskew = EGAffineMap(2.0, 0.5, -0.3, 1.4, 3.0, -1.0)\nskew(c)          # a genuine EGEllipse2: the linear part isn't a similarity\nrm(c)            # rm is a pure rotation, so this EGEllipse2 has a == b\nEGEllipse2(center=[0.0, 1.0000000000000002], a=5.0, b=5.0, angle=0.0)","title":"Conics: type is preserved, but never a circle"},{"category":"section","location":"affine_maps/#Conic-arcs:-orientation-reversing-maps-need-an-endpoint-swap","page":"Affine Maps","text":"m(arc::EGCircularArc2) returns an EGEllipticArc2 (same reasoning as the circle case above); EGEllipticArc2, EGHyperbolicArc2 and EGParabolicArc2 each map to the same arc type, on the image of their underlying conic.\n\nFor the two closed-conic arcs (circular, elliptic), \"the arc from p1 to p2\" only picks out one of two complementary arcs because it's understood to sweep counterclockwise. An orientation-reversing map (det(m) < 0, like a reflection) turns that sweep clockwise, so p1/p2 are swapped when reconstructing the transformed arc — otherwise it would silently become the complementary arc instead. EGHyperbolicArc2/ EGParabolicArc2 need no such swap: a single hyperbola branch or a parabola is open, so two points on it always determine one unambiguous arc regardless of orientation.","title":"Conic arcs: orientation-reversing maps need an endpoint swap"},{"category":"section","location":"affine_maps/#Circular-arc-regions-become-curvilinear","page":"Affine Maps","text":"EGCircularSector2, EGCircularSegment2, EGAnnularSector2 and EGInterstice2 each hold a concrete EGCircularArc2 (or two) as a field — but under a general affine map, that arc generically becomes elliptic, which no longer fits. So m applied to one of these returns the more general EGCurvilinearTriangle2/EGCurvilinearQuadrilateral2/ EGCurvilinearNgon2 instead, built from the same sides, each individually mapped through m:\n\nc = EGCircle2(EGPoint(0.0, 0.0), 4.0)\narc = EGCircularArc2(c, EGPoint(4.0, 0.0), EGPoint(0.0, 4.0))\nsec = EGCircularSector2(arc)\nskew(sec)   # an EGCurvilinearTriangle2: 2 straight sides + 1 elliptic arc\nEGCurvilinearTriangle2(EGSegment([3.0, -1.0] -> [11.0, -2.2]), EGEllipticArc2{Float64}(EGEllipse2(center=[3.0, -1.0], a=8.250610742696544, b=5.72078861456184, angle=0.04532994360037254), [11.0, -2.2], [5.0, 4.6]), EGSegment([5.0, 4.6] -> [3.0, -1.0]))\n\nEGCurvilinearTriangle2/EGCurvilinearQuadrilateral2/EGCurvilinearNgon2 themselves are already general enough to be closed under this: m just maps each of their sides through itself, whatever mix of segments and conic arcs they are.\n\nMaps compose with ordinary function composition, ∘, right-to-left just like any other Julia functions — (m2 ∘ m1)(p) == m2(m1(p)):\n\ncomposed = rm ∘ tm     # first translate, then rotate\ncomposed(EGPoint(0.0, 0.0))\n[4.0, 3.0]\n\nThis builds a single new EGAffineMap (not a closure), so applying composed to many points is exactly as cheap as applying any other single map — the composition happens once, up front, not on every call.","title":"Circular-arc regions become curvilinear"},{"category":"section","location":"affine_maps/#Applying-one-map-to-several-shapes-at-once","page":"Affine Maps","text":"@affinemap applies m to a whole begin ... end block of shapes in one expression, returning the results as a tuple:\n\nT1, C1 = @affinemap skew begin\n    tri = EGTriangle(EGPoint(0.0, 0.0), EGPoint(1.0, 0.0), EGPoint(0.0, 1.0))\n    circ = EGCircle2(EGPoint(1.0, 2.0), 5.0)\nend\nT1, C1\n(EGTriangle([3.0, -1.0], [5.0, -1.3], [3.5, 0.3999999999999999]), EGEllipse2(center=[6.0, 1.5], a=10.31326342837068, b=7.1509857682023, angle=0.04532994360037254))\n\ntri/circ themselves are untouched — @affinemap! is the mutating counterpart, rebinding each named shape to its own image under m instead of returning copies (immutable structs can't be changed in place, so this works by repointing the variable, not the object). Both accept a single expression too (@affinemap m circ), and both are part of a larger family — @translate, @rotate, @homothety, @reflection, @invert, @invert_neg — that apply their own transform to a block the same way.","title":"Applying one map to several shapes at once"},{"category":"section","location":"circles/#Circles","page":"Circles","text":"EGCircle2 is a center and a radius, c.center and c.r. Some of what follows is a method on a circle (area, rotate, ...); the rest — tangency, power of a point, radical axes, inversion — is a function of one or more circles.","title":"Circles"},{"category":"section","location":"circles/#Power-of-a-point-and-tangent-lines","page":"Circles","text":"The power_of_point of p with respect to a circle c is distance(p, c.center)^2 - c.r^2: negative inside the circle, zero on it, positive outside. Its square root, when p is outside, is exactly the length of a tangent segment from p to the circle — that's tangent_length. tangent_points and tangent_lines give the two actual points/lines of tangency.\n\nusing EuclideanGeometry\n\nc = EGCircle2(EGPoint(0.0, 0.0), 5.0)\np = EGPoint(13.0, 0.0)\n\npower_of_point(p, c)     # 144.0 = 13² - 5²\ntangent_length(c, p)     # 12.0  = sqrt(144)\npts = tangent_points(c, p)\n2-element Vector{EGPoint{2, Float64}}:\n [1.9230769230769234, 4.615384615384615]\n [1.9230769230769234, -4.615384615384615]\n\nThis is the classic tangent from an external point construction: pts[1] and pts[2] are the two points where a line through p just touches the circle, and power_of_point(p, c) is nothing but the square of that tangent length either way.","title":"Power of a point and tangent lines"},{"category":"section","location":"circles/#Radical-axis,-radical-center-and-radical-circle","page":"Circles","text":"Two circles that don't share a center have a radical axis: the locus of points whose power is equal with respect to both. radical_axis always exists — even for circles that don't meet — and is perpendicular to the line joining their centers. \"Don't share a center\" is checked with a scale-aware tolerance (atol, like elsewhere in the package), not bare equality — two circles can be mathematically concentric yet have centers that only agree up to floating-point roundoff (this comes up for, e.g., orthic_axis of an equilateral triangle, whose circumcircle and nine-point circle are concentric), and a bare == would miss that and return a wildly wrong axis instead of throwing. Three circles have three pairwise radical axes, and those three lines always meet at a single point, the radical_center; radical_circle is centered there, orthogonal to all three (only defined when that common power is non-negative).\n\nc1 = EGCircle2(EGPoint(0.0, 0.0), 3.0)\nc2 = EGCircle2(EGPoint(8.0, 0.0), 2.0)\nc3 = EGCircle2(EGPoint(3.0, 6.0), 4.0)\n\nra = radical_axis(c1, c2)\nrc = radical_center(c1, c2, c3)\nrcirc = radical_circle(c1, c2, c3)\nEGCircle2([4.3125, 1.010416666666666], 3.258619046510005)","title":"Radical axis, radical center and radical circle"},{"category":"section","location":"circles/#Inversion,-polar-lines-and-poles","page":"Circles","text":"Inversion in a circle c sends a point p to the point on ray c.center → p at distance k²/distance(p, c.center) from the center (k = c.r by default): points outside c go inside and vice versa, and points on c are fixed. inversion does this for a point; invert has methods for an EGLine (which usually inverts to an EGCircle2 through the center), an EGCircle2 (which inverts to another EGCircle2, or an EGLine if it passes through the inversion center), an EGSegment (see below), an EGTriangle, and an EGStraightNgon.\n\ninversion(EGPoint(10.0, 0.0), c)   # (2.5, 0.0): 5²/10 = 2.5\n[2.5, 0.0]\n\ninversion_neg and invert_neg give the negative-ratio inversion instead: the same image, point-reflected through the inversion circle's own center (i.e. on ray p -> O rather than O -> p):\n\ninversion_neg(EGPoint(10.0, 0.0), c)   # (-2.5, 0.0): the mirror image of the positive one\n[-2.5, 0.0]","title":"Inversion, polar lines and poles"},{"category":"section","location":"circles/#Inverting-a-segment,-triangle-or-polygon","page":"Circles","text":"A straight side doesn't generally stay straight under inversion: it only does when the line it lies on passes through the inversion center (then it inverts to another EGSegment, on that same line); otherwise it inverts to an arc of the circle its line inverts to — specifically the arc that does not pass through the center, since that point is the image of the line's own point at infinity, which a finite segment never reaches. invert(::EGSegment, ::EGPoint) returns whichever of the two applies, as a Union{EGSegment,EGCircularArc2}:\n\ninvert(EGSegment(EGPoint(5.0, 2.0), EGPoint(8.0, 6.0)), EGPoint(0.0, 0.0))   # an EGCircularArc2\ninvert(EGSegment(EGPoint(3.0, 0.0), EGPoint(8.0, 0.0)), EGPoint(0.0, 0.0))   # an EGSegment: this line passes through (0,0)\nEGSegment([0.3333333333333333, 0.0] -> [0.125, 0.0])\n\nSince an EGTriangle's or EGStraightNgon's sides invert independently like this, their image is generally a mix of straight and curved sides — not representable as another EGTriangle/EGStraightNgon. invert(::EGTriangle, ::EGPoint) and invert(::EGStraightNgon, ::EGPoint) return an EGCurvilinearNgon2 instead: a closed region bounded by any mix of EGSegment and EGCircularArc2 sides, each inverted independently and reconnected in order.\n\nt2 = EGTriangle(EGPoint(5.0, 2.0), EGPoint(9.0, 3.0), EGPoint(6.0, 8.0))\ncp = invert(t2, EGPoint(0.0, 0.0))\narea(cp), perimeter(cp)\n(0.003276299211514883, 0.264924463164291)\n\narea/perimeter are the same generic, sides-based EGPolygon formulas every closed shape in the package shares (a straight side's own contribution reduces to the standard shoelace-formula edge term); rotate, reflection and homothety all work on an EGCurvilinearNgon2 too, transforming each side independently.\n\npolar_line and pole are the projective dual of this: the polar of p with respect to c is the line through the two tangent points from p (when p is outside c) — and pole is its inverse, recovering p from that line.\n\npl = polar_line(c, p)   # the line through pts[1] and pts[2] above\npole(c, pl)              # back to p = (13.0, 0.0)\n[13.0, 0.0]","title":"Inverting a segment, triangle or polygon"},{"category":"section","location":"circles/#Similitude-centers-and-common-tangents","page":"Circles","text":"Two circles have two centers of similitude: the external_similitude_center (where their external common tangents meet) and the internal_similitude_center (where the internal ones — the ones that cross between the circles — meet). external_tangent_lines and internal_tangent_lines return those tangent lines directly.\n\nexternal_similitude_center(c1, c2)   # (24.0, 0.0)\ninternal_similitude_center(c1, c2)   # (4.8, 0.0)\next = external_tangent_lines(c1, c2)\nint = internal_tangent_lines(c1, c2)\n2-element Vector{EGLine{2, Float64}}:\n EGLine([4.8, 0.0] -> [1.875, 2.3418742493993996])\n EGLine([4.8, 0.0] -> [1.875, -2.3418742493993996])\n\nThe external center is far from both circles here because c1 and c2 have fairly close radii — the closer two radii are, the further out the external center sits, reaching infinity (parallel tangents) when the radii are exactly equal (in which case external_similitude_center throws, since there is no finite point to return).\n\nThese similitude centers are also exactly the points used by the Apollonius/tangent-circle constructions — see Tangency & Apollonius Problems — since a circle tangent to two given circles is related to them by a homothety centered at one of these two points.\n\ntangent_parallel gives the two tangent lines to a circle parallel to a given line — the tangents at the two ends of the diameter perpendicular to it:\n\nt1, t2 = tangent_parallel(c, EGLine(EGPoint(0.0, 0.0), EGPoint(1.0, 1.0)))\n(EGLine([3.5355339059327373, -3.5355339059327373] -> [7.071067811865475, 0.0]), EGLine([-3.5355339059327373, 3.5355339059327373] -> [-7.071067811865475, 0.0]))","title":"Similitude centers and common tangents"},{"category":"section","location":"circles/#How-two-circles-(or-a-line-and-a-circle)-relate","page":"Circles","text":"circles_position and line_circle_position classify the relationship between two circles, or a line and a circle, as a Symbol rather than a single boolean — useful when you need to distinguish, say, \"tangent\" from \"disjoint\" from \"one contains the other\" in one call instead of chaining several predicates:\n\ncircles_position(EGCircle2(EGPoint(0.0, 0.0), 5.0), EGCircle2(EGPoint(2.0, 0.0), 3.0))   # :tangent_int\nline_circle_position(EGLine(EGPoint(0.0, 5.0), EGPoint(1.0, 5.0)), EGCircle2(EGPoint(0.0, 0.0), 5.0))   # :tangent\n:tangent\n\ncircles_position returns one of :identical, :concentric, :disjoint_ext, :tangent_ext, :secant, :tangent_int or :disjoint_int; line_circle_position returns one of :disjoint, :tangent or :secant.","title":"How two circles (or a line and a circle) relate"},{"category":"section","location":"circles/#Circular-arcs","page":"Circles","text":"EGCircularArc2 is the arc of a circle between two of its points, traversed counterclockwise from the first to the second — fixing the direction like this is what makes the two points unambiguous, since otherwise there'd be two different arcs (the short way and the long way around) that a bare pair of points couldn't tell apart.\n\np1, p2 = EGPoint(5.0, 0.0), EGPoint(0.0, 5.0)\narc = EGCircularArc2(c, p1, p2)\n\nmeasure(arc)        # π/2: the swept angle, counterclockwise from p1 to p2\narc_length(arc)      # c.r * measure(arc)\n7.853981633974483\n\npoint_on_arc(arc, 0.0) ≈ p1   # t=0 is p1, t=1 is p2\nmidpoint(arc)                   # point_on_arc(arc, 0.5)\n[3.5355339059327378, 3.5355339059327373]\n\nSwapping p1 and p2 gives the complementary arc (the other 3/4 of this circle here), not the same arc traversed backwards — EGCircularArc2 only ever sweeps counterclockwise, so which points is p1 and which is p2 is exactly what picks one of the two candidate arcs. Ellipses have the exact same arc type, EGEllipticArc2, and the exact same convention — see Conics: Ellipse, Parabola & Hyperbola.\n\nEGCircularArc2 is also what interstices (see Tangency & Apollonius Problems) builds a curvilinear triangle's three sides out of.","title":"Circular arcs"},{"category":"section","location":"circles/#Circular-sectors,-segments-and-annuli","page":"Circles","text":"An arc alone is just a curve; closing it up gives a genuine region — a finite-area EGPolygon whose sides mix straight segments with the arc itself. EGCircularSector2, EGCircularSegment2 and EGAnnularSector2 are the three classical ways to do this (EGCircularSector2(circle, p1, p2) is shorthand for EGCircularSector2(EGCircularArc2(circle, p1, p2)), and likewise for the other two):\n\nEGCircularSector2 — the \"pie slice\": bounded by the two radii circle.center -> p1, circle.center -> p2, and the arc between them.\nEGCircularSegment2 — the \"cap\": bounded by the arc and the straight chord [p1, p2] instead of the two radii.\nEGAnnularSector2 — the \"ring slice\": the region between arc and the corresponding arc of a smaller, concentric circle (r_inner), closed off by the two radial segments between them — Luxor's own sector(center, innerradius, outerradius, ...) shape, represented here as a real EGPolygon.\n\nsec = EGCircularSector2(arc)\narea(sec)        # r² * measure(arc) / 2\nperimeter(sec)    # the two radii (2r) plus the arc length\n17.853981633974485\n\nseg = EGCircularSegment2(arc)\narea(seg)        # r² * (measure(arc) - sin(measure(arc))) / 2\nperimeter(seg)    # the arc length plus the chord [p1, p2]\n14.925049445839958\n\nasec = EGAnnularSector2(arc, 2.0)   # inner radius 2.0\narea(asec)        # outer sector area minus inner sector area\n16.493361431346415\n\nNone of these three formulas is hand-derived per type — every one comes for free from the generic, sides-based area/perimeter every EGPolygon shares (see EGPolygon's own docstring), and none needs to special-case whether arc sweeps less or more than half the circle (a \"minor\" vs \"major\" arc), since measure(arc) is already the specific directed angle for this arc.\n\nBoth EGCircularSector2 and EGCircularSegment2 have a Base.in matching their shape — inside the circle and on the correct side (angularly, for a sector; of the chord, for a segment):\n\nc.center in sec, c.center in seg   # the center is in the sector, but not this segment\n(true, false)","title":"Circular sectors, segments and annuli"},{"category":"section","location":"circles/#Transforming-arcs,-sectors-and-segments","page":"Circles","text":"rotate, reflection and homothety all work on an EGCircularArc2 (and, by delegating to it, on an EGCircularSector2/EGCircularSegment2/EGAnnularSector2 too). rotate and homothety never reverse orientation (even homothety with a negative ratio, a point reflection through the center, is really just a 180° rotation), so p1/p2 transform pointwise with no surprises:\n\nrotate(arc, pi / 3)\nhomothety(arc, -2.0)   # negative k: still no swap needed\nEGCircularArc2(EGCircle2([0.0, 0.0], 10.0), [-10.0, 0.0] -> [0.0, -10.0])\n\nReflecting about an EGPoint is likewise a point reflection — no swap. Reflecting about an EGLine, however, is a true mirror: it does reverse orientation, so reflection(::EGCircularArc2, ::EGLine) also swaps p1 and p2 internally, keeping the result's own p1 -> p2 sweep counterclockwise like every other EGCircularArc2:\n\nreflection(arc, EGPoint(1.0, 1.0))                     # point reflection: p1/p2 not swapped\nreflection(arc, EGLine(EGPoint(0.0, 0.0), EGPoint(1.0, 1.0)))  # line reflection: p1/p2 swapped\nEGCircularArc2(EGCircle2([0.0, 0.0], 5.0), [5.0, 0.0] -> [0.0, 5.0])\n\nWithout that swap, the reflected endpoints alone would trace out the arc's complementary 3/4-of-the-circle instead of its true mirror image.","title":"Transforming arcs, sectors and segments"},{"category":"section","location":"circles/#The-gap-between-three-mutually-tangent-circles","page":"Circles","text":"Three circles that are pairwise tangent (each coin-touching-coin, or one containing the other two) leave a curvilinear-triangle gap between them — interstices finds it (or, when one circle encloses the other two, both gaps, one on each side):\n\nu1 = EGCircle2(EGPoint(0.0, 0.0), 1.0)\nu2 = EGCircle2(EGPoint(2.0, 0.0), 1.0)\nu3 = EGCircle2(EGPoint(1.0, sqrt(3)), 1.0)\n\ngap = only(interstices(u1, u2, u3))\narea(gap), perimeter(gap)\n(0.16125448077398064, 3.1415926535897927)\n\nThe result is an EGInterstice2 — a specific 3-arc EGPolygon built by finding the circle(s) tangent to all three (the classical CCC Apollonius problem, see Tangency & Apollonius Problems) and picking out the facing arc on each of u1/u2/u3.","title":"The gap between three mutually tangent circles"},{"category":"section","location":"conics/#Conics:-Ellipse,-Parabola-and-Hyperbola","page":"Conics: Ellipse, Parabola & Hyperbola","text":"Three separate types — EGEllipse2, EGParabola2 and EGHyperbola2 — each aligned with a coordinate axis of its own (center/angle for the first two, focus/directrix for the parabola), plus a fifth constructor, conic_through_points, that fits an ellipse or hyperbola through five arbitrary points.\n\nAll three conic types share the same shape of API, since they share the same underlying pattern (a curve defined by an equation in its own local frame, plus a point/line duality): point_on_*, is_on_*, intersection with an EGLine, polar_line, tangent_points and tangent_lines all exist for each of the three, with the same meaning throughout. That shared behavior is described once, in Points, tangents and duality, rather than three times.","title":"Conics: Ellipse, Parabola & Hyperbola"},{"category":"section","location":"conics/#Ellipse","page":"Conics: Ellipse, Parabola & Hyperbola","text":"using EuclideanGeometry\n\ne = EGEllipse2(EGPoint(0.0, 0.0), 5.0, 3.0)  # center, semi-axis a, semi-axis b\nEGEllipse2(center=[0.0, 0.0], a=5.0, b=3.0, angle=0.0)\n\nEGEllipse2(center, a, b, angle=0.0) places semi-axis a along angle (radians from the x-axis) and semi-axis b perpendicular to it — a and b don't need to be ordered; either can be the longer one. There is also the classical bifocal constructor:\n\nEGEllipse2(EGPoint(-4.0, 0.0), EGPoint(4.0, 0.0), 5.0)   # foci + semi-major axis a\nEGEllipse2(center=[0.0, 0.0], a=5.0, b=3.0, angle=0.0)\n\nwhich throws an ArgumentError if a isn't greater than half the distance between the foci (otherwise no real ellipse has that focal distance and semi-major axis). A third form, EGEllipse2(f1, f2, p), builds the ellipse through a known point p instead of a known a — computing a from the bifocal sum (|pf1| + |pf2|)/2 first.\n\narea(e)        # π·a·b\nperimeter(e)   # Ramanujan's 2nd approximation (exact when a == b)\nfoci(e)        # the two focus points, as a 2-tuple\n([4.0, 0.0], [-4.0, 0.0])\n\nis_on_ellipse(EGPoint(5.0, 0.0), e)   # true: exactly at the end of the major axis\nis_on_ellipse(EGPoint(1.0, 1.0), e)   # false: strictly inside\nfalse\n\northoptic is the director circle: the locus of points from which the two tangent lines to e are perpendicular. For an ellipse it's always a real circle, of radius sqrt(a² + b²), centered at e.center.\n\northoptic(e)   # EGCircle2(center, sqrt(5^2+3^2)) ≈ EGCircle2(center, 5.83)\nEGCircle2([0.0, 0.0], 5.830951894845301)","title":"Ellipse"},{"category":"section","location":"conics/#Parabola","page":"Conics: Ellipse, Parabola & Hyperbola","text":"focus = EGPoint(0.0, 1.0)\ndirectrix = EGLine(EGPoint(-5.0, -1.0), EGPoint(5.0, -1.0))\npar = EGParabola2(focus, directrix)\nEGParabola2(focus=[0.0, 1.0], directrix=EGLine([-5.0, -1.0] -> [5.0, -1.0]))\n\nA parabola only has one natural constructor — the focus/directrix definition itself — since (unlike the ellipse/hyperbola) there's no second, equally standard center/axis parametrization to offer as an alternative.\n\nvertex(par)            # midpoint of focus and its foot on the directrix\nfocal_parameter(par)   # distance(focus, directrix), often called p\n2.0\n\npoint_on_parabola parametrizes by the signed distance s from the axis (so it's the local y-coordinate in the frame where the parabola reads y² = 2·p·x, with s = 0 at the vertex):\n\npoint_on_parabola(par, 2.0)\n[-2.0, 1.0]\n\nis_on_parabola(point_on_parabola(par, 2.0), par)   # true, by construction\ntrue\n\nThe parabola's own orthoptic (director curve) is, remarkably, exactly its directrix — a fact special to the parabola (the ellipse's and hyperbola's orthoptics are circles, not lines):\n\northoptic(par) == par.directrix\ntrue","title":"Parabola"},{"category":"section","location":"conics/#Hyperbola","page":"Conics: Ellipse, Parabola & Hyperbola","text":"h = EGHyperbola2(EGPoint(0.0, 0.0), 3.0, 4.0)  # center, transverse a, conjugate b\nEGHyperbola2(center=[0.0, 0.0], a=3.0, b=4.0, angle=0.0)\n\nEGHyperbola2(center, a, b, angle=0.0) reads (x/a)² - (y/b)² = 1 in the rotated local frame — a is the semi-transverse axis (along angle, the one that actually meets the curve) and b the semi-conjugate axis (which doesn't). The bifocal constructor mirrors the ellipse's:\n\nEGHyperbola2(EGPoint(-5.0, 0.0), EGPoint(5.0, 0.0), 3.0)   # foci + semi-transverse axis a\nEGHyperbola2(center=[0.0, 0.0], a=3.0, b=4.0, angle=0.0)\n\nthrowing instead when a isn't less than half the focal distance (the opposite inequality from the ellipse, since here c > a).\n\nfoci(h)         # the two foci, at distance sqrt(a²+b²) from the center\nasymptotes(h)   # the two asymptote lines, through the center\n(EGLine([0.0, 0.0] -> [3.0, 4.0]), EGLine([0.0, 0.0] -> [3.0, -4.0]))\n\northoptic (the director circle) exists for a hyperbola only when a > b, with radius sqrt(a² - b²) — otherwise there's no point in the plane from which both tangents can be perpendicular, and it throws an ArgumentError:\n\northoptic(EGHyperbola2(EGPoint(0.0, 0.0), 5.0, 3.0))   # a > b: EGCircle2 of radius sqrt(25-9) = 4\nEGCircle2([0.0, 0.0], 4.0)\n\npoint_on_hyperbola parametrizes one branch at a time (branch=1, the default, or branch=-1 for the other) using the hyperbolic functions: x = branch·a·cosh(t), y = b·sinh(t).\n\npoint_on_hyperbola(h, 0.5)          # on the branch nearer +x\npoint_on_hyperbola(h, 0.5; branch=-1)  # the mirrored point on the other branch\n[-3.382877895619142, 2.0843812219749895]\n\nis_on_hyperbola(point_on_hyperbola(h, 0.5), h)   # true, on either branch\ntrue\n\np in h (via Base.in) is the natural analogue of \"inside\" for a curve that doesn't bound a single finite region: it's true when p is on h itself or beyond either branch ((x/a)² - (y/b)² >= 1 in the local frame) — i.e. on the same side as the curve, rather than in the \"waist\" between the two branches.","title":"Hyperbola"},{"category":"section","location":"conics/#Points,-tangents-and-duality","page":"Conics: Ellipse, Parabola & Hyperbola","text":"The following table applies to all three types (write conic for whichever of EGEllipse2, EGParabola2 or EGHyperbola2 you're using):\n\nFunction Meaning\npoint_on_conic(conic, param) a point on the curve at the given parameter\nis_on_conic(p, conic) is p exactly on the curve?\nintersection(l, conic) 0, 1 or 2 points where an EGLine crosses the curve\npolar_line(conic, p) the polar line of p (see below)\ntangent_points(conic, p) the point(s) of tangency of the line(s) from p\ntangent_lines(conic, p) the tangent line(s) themselves\n\nThe polar line of a point p with respect to a conic is the projective-duality construction that makes all of tangent_points and tangent_lines work uniformly, for every conic (including EGCircle2, see Circles):\n\nWhen p is outside the curve, its polar is the chord joining the two points where the tangent lines from p touch the curve — so tangent_points is implemented as simply intersection(polar_line(conic, p), conic).\nWhen p is on the curve, its polar line degenerates to the tangent line at p itself — which is exactly why tangent_lines special-cases that situation, rather than returning the degenerate EGLine(p, p) a naive \"join p to its own tangent point\" would give.\npolar_line returns nothing at the one truly degenerate input: p being the ellipse/hyperbola's center (an ellipse's or hyperbola's polar of its own center would be the line at infinity), or the parabola's focus sitting on its own directrix (a degenerate parabola, not a real curve at all).\n\np = EGPoint(13.0, 0.0)\ntangent_points(e, p)\ntangent_lines(e, p)\n2-element Vector{EGLine{2, Float64}}:\n EGLine([13.0, 0.0] -> [1.923076923076923, 2.7692307692307687])\n EGLine([13.0, 0.0] -> [1.923076923076923, -2.7692307692307687])\n\nFor an EGHyperbola2 specifically, note that being far from the curve doesn't guarantee real tangents (or the lack of them) the way it does for an ellipse — it depends on which side of which branch p sits on; tangent_points/tangent_lines still return an empty vector rather than erroring when there are none.","title":"Points, tangents and duality"},{"category":"section","location":"conics/#Transforming-conics","page":"Conics: Ellipse, Parabola & Hyperbola","text":"rotate, reflection and homothety work on all three types. For EGEllipse2/EGHyperbola2, center transforms pointwise; rotate adds its angle onto angle too; homothety scales a/b by abs(k) (never negative, and never swaps which axis is which) while leaving angle alone, even for a negative k — a homothety, negative ratio included, never reverses orientation:\n\nrotate(e, pi / 6)\nhomothety(e, -2.0)   # a, b both scale by 2 (abs(-2.0)); angle unchanged\nEGEllipse2(center=[0.0, 0.0], a=10.0, b=6.0, angle=0.0)\n\nreflection needs two separate methods here, the same way the base reflection(::EGPoint, about) itself does:\n\nreflection(conic, about::EGPoint) is a point reflection (a 180° rotation) — orientation-preserving, so angle is unchanged.\nreflection(conic, about::EGLine) is a true mirror — orientation-reversing, so the new angle is 2φ - conic.angle, where φ is the line's own angle from the x-axis (not simply conic.angle unchanged, nor its negation — the formula accounts for the mirror line's own orientation).\n\nreflection(e, EGPoint(1.0, 1.0))                              # angle unchanged\nreflection(e, EGLine(EGPoint(0.0, 0.0), EGPoint(1.0, 1.0)))       # angle: 2φ - e.angle\nEGEllipse2(center=[0.0, 0.0], a=5.0, b=3.0, angle=1.5707963267948966)\n\nAn EGParabola2 is entirely determined by its focus and directrix, and both already transform correctly on their own (an EGPoint and an EGLine), so its rotate/reflection/homothety just transform each of the two and rebuild:\n\nrotate(par, pi / 4)\nEGParabola2(focus=[-0.7071067811865475, 0.7071067811865476], directrix=EGLine([-2.8284271247461903, -4.242640687119285] -> [4.242640687119286, 2.82842712474619]))","title":"Transforming conics"},{"category":"section","location":"conics/#Fitting-a-conic-through-five-points","page":"Conics: Ellipse, Parabola & Hyperbola","text":"conic_through_points takes five points in general position and returns the unique EGEllipse2 or EGHyperbola2 passing through all of them (five points determine a conic, the same way three determine a circle or two a line):\n\npts = [point_on_ellipse(EGEllipse2(EGPoint(1.0, 2.0), 6.0, 4.0, 0.3), t) for t in (0.1, 1.0, 2.0, 3.0, 4.5)]\nconic_through_points(pts...)\nEGEllipse2(center=[1.0000000000000007, 2.0000000000000004], a=5.999999999999999, b=4.000000000000001, angle=0.29999999999999954)\n\nIt throws an ArgumentError when the five points don't determine a unique conic (a degenerate configuration — e.g. four of them collinear), or when they do determine a conic but it's a parabola (discriminant ≈ 0): a parabola isn't representable by this function, since it isn't a (center, a, b, angle)-style object the way the other two are.\n\nInternally, the points are first centered on their own centroid and rescaled to unit average distance before the linear system is solved, and the fitted center/axes are transformed back afterwards — fitting directly in the original coordinates would badly ill-condition the underlying linear algebra for points far from the origin or spread far apart, since the quadratic terms of the conic's equation would then dwarf the linear and constant ones.","title":"Fitting a conic through five points"},{"category":"section","location":"drawing/#Drawing-with-Luxor.jl","page":"Drawing with Luxor.jl","text":"warning: Optional add-on, not core functionality\nEverything on this page comes from a package extension, not from EuclideanGeometry.jl itself. The package never depends on Luxor.jl — path has no methods at all until your own code also loads Luxor. This is also the newest, least settled corner of the package: keyword names and default behavior may still change. Everything described on the rest of this site (points, lines, circles, triangles, conics, tangency, affine maps...) is unaffected either way.","title":"Drawing with Luxor.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"drawing/#Activating-it","page":"Drawing with Luxor.jl","text":"Julia's package-extension mechanism ([weakdeps]/[extensions] in Project.toml, available since Julia 1.9) is what makes this possible: path is declared in EuclideanGeometry.jl but given no methods there. The moment your own session also has Luxor loaded, those methods materialize automatically — no configuration needed on your part beyond loading both packages:\n\nusing EuclideanGeometry, Luxor\n\nt = EGTriangle(EGPoint(-80.0, 60.0), EGPoint(80.0, 60.0), EGPoint(-20.0, -80.0))\n\nDrawing(400, 400, \"triangle.png\")\norigin()\nbackground(\"white\")\n\nsethue(\"steelblue\")\npath(t; action=:stroke)\npath(circumcircle(t); action=:stroke)\n\nsethue(\"orangered\")\npath(incenter(t); action=:fill)\n\nfinish()\n\nIf you only ever using EuclideanGeometry and never load Luxor, path simply doesn't exist as a callable function (methods(path) is empty) — there is no partial/broken state to worry about.","title":"Activating it"},{"category":"section","location":"drawing/#path-is-deliberately-thin","page":"Drawing with Luxor.jl","text":"path(obj; action=:path, kwargs...) only ever does one thing: it adds obj to Luxor's current path, using Luxor's own primitives underneath (Luxor.circle, Luxor.line, Luxor.poly, Luxor.arc2r, ...). It never calls sethue, never sets an opacity, and never draws a label — those are already exactly what Luxor's own sethue, setopacity and label do well, and giving path its own parallel vocabulary for them would just be a second thing to learn for no benefit. Every example on this page is path(...) interleaved with plain Luxor calls, the same way you'd combine any two of Luxor's own shape functions.\n\nThe one keyword every method shares is action, forwarded straight to Luxor exactly the way Luxor's own shape functions (circle, poly, ...) take it:\n\n:path (the default) — add to the current path and do nothing else. Use this when you're about to style it yourself (Luxor.strokepath(), Luxor.fillpath(), Luxor.clip(), or add more shapes to the same path first) or when you're going to reuse the same call with several actions.\n:stroke, :fill, :fillstroke — build the path and render it immediately, the one-call convenience:\n\nsethue(\"steelblue\")\npath(t; action=:stroke)       # outline only, one call\n\npath(t)                        # action=:path: just builds the path...\nLuxor.strokepath()              # ...you render it, same effect\n\nFor a translucent fill underneath a solid outline (a common combination that used to be its own fillcolor keyword on this page in an earlier version of this interface), just do what you'd do with any Luxor shape — build the path once, fill it, then stroke a fresh copy:\n\nsethue(\"steelblue\"); setopacity(0.15)\npath(t; action=:fill)\nsethue(\"steelblue\"); setopacity(1.0)\npath(t; action=:stroke)\n\nAnd for a label, Luxor.label (or plain Luxor.text) at whatever anchor point makes sense for that shape — which is almost always a point the package already gives you a name for, so there's nothing to look up:\n\nLuxor.label(\"I\", :N, Luxor.Point(incenter(t)...))\nLuxor.label(\"O\", :N, Luxor.Point(circumcenter(t)...))","title":"path is deliberately thin"},{"category":"section","location":"drawing/#What-each-type-builds","page":"Drawing with Luxor.jl","text":"path(obj; action=:path, kwargs...) dispatches on the type of obj. Besides action, every method also accepts whatever structural (not styling) keywords that curve needs — how far to extend an infinite line, how finely to sample a curve Luxor has no native primitive for, and so on:\n\nType Adds to the path as Type-specific keywords\nEGPoint a small circle radius=3\nEGSegment a straight line between its two points, or — pass as=:arrow — an arrow (see Arrows below) as=:plain (default)\nEGLine a long finite segment, since the line itself is infinite; extend=0.0 draws the exact finite segment between l.p1/l.p2 instead extend=1000.0: how far past each defining point; as=:plain/:arrow\nEGRay likewise, extended only past through (not past origin); extend=0.0 draws the exact finite segment from origin to through extend=1000.0; as=:plain/:arrow\nEGCircle2 Luxor's native circle —\nany EGPolygon every side, chained end to end into one closed path — a straight line for an EGSegment side, a true arc for an EGCircularArc2 side, an n-point sampled polyline for any other conic-arc side (see below); covers EGTriangle, EGQuadrilateral, EGStraightNgon, EGCircularSector2, EGCircularSegment2, EGAnnularSector2, EGInterstice2, EGCurvilinearTriangle2, EGCurvilinearQuadrilateral2 and EGCurvilinearNgon2 — one method for the whole family n=60 (only matters if some side needs sampling)\nEGBoundingBox an axis-aligned box —\nEGEllipse2 a smooth, Bézier-curve ellipse (Luxor's own axis-aligned ellipse(center, w, h), w = 2a, h = 2b) inside a rotated/translated frame matching e.center/e.angle, so the path stays a true curve at any zoom level —\nEGParabola2 Luxor has no native parabola primitive: sampled at n points via point_on_parabola over the parameter range srange, added as an open polyline srange=(-100.0, 100.0), n=60\nEGHyperbola2 likewise (no native primitive), sampled via point_on_hyperbola on one branch at a time trange=(-2.0, 2.0), n=60, branch=1 (pass branch=-1 and call again for the other branch)\nEGCircularArc2 a true circular arc from p1 to p2, via Luxor's own arc2r (Cairo's native arc primitive — not a polygonal approximation) —\nEGEllipticArc2, EGParabolicArc2, EGHyperbolicArc2 none of these has a native Cairo primitive either, so each is sampled at n points via point_on_arc over its own parameter range [0, 1] (arc.p1 to arc.p2), added as an open polyline n=60\nEGAngle2 see below — it has no single canonical path as=:arc (default), radius\nEGVector has no position of its own, so it's drawn as the segment from -> from + v from=EGPoint(0.0, 0.0), as=:plain/:arrow\nEGHalfPlane2 unbounded, so this draws its boundary line only (see EGLine above) extend=1000.0\nEGStrip2 likewise unbounded: both boundary lines, one call each extend=1000.0\n\nMost types default effectively to an outline when you pass action=:stroke (EGPoint would need action=:fill to actually show up, since an unfilled single-pixel-radius circle is invisible — but that choice is now yours to make, same as with any other type).\n\nThe single path(pg::EGPolygon) method is the direct payoff of building a real EGPolygon hierarchy: it walks sides(pg) via the same _polygon_walk area/perimeter already use, so one method — not seven, one per concrete type — covers every straight-sided and curved-region shape in the package. The one behavior difference from a hand-rolled per-vertex path: it always produces a closed path (no close=false open-polyline option), since a walked side sequence is inherently a loop.\n\nA subtlety worth knowing, inherited from Luxor itself rather than anything this package adds: with a non-:path action, most of Luxor's own shape-building functions (circle, poly, and so the types built on them here — EGCircle2, every EGPolygon, EGEllipse2) clear the current path first, so calling one always draws only that shape. A few of Luxor's own primitives don't — line (hence EGSegment/EGLine/EGRay here) and box (hence EGBoundingBox) add to whatever path is already there instead. In practice this rarely matters: a prior call with a real action (:stroke, :fill, ...) already emptied the path as a side effect of drawing it, regardless of which behavior the next call has. It only shows up if you deliberately chain several path(...; action=:path) calls to build one compound shape across multiple types and finish with a single action on the last call — in that case, a final EGSegment/EGLine/EGRay/EGBoundingBox call correctly includes everything built so far, while a final EGCircle2/EGPolygon/etc. call would silently discard it first.","title":"What each type builds"},{"category":"section","location":"drawing/#Arrows","page":"Drawing with Luxor.jl","text":"EGSegment/EGLine/EGRay take as=:arrow to draw as an arrow instead of a plain line, via Luxor's own arrow:\n\npath(EGSegment(EGPoint(-80.0, 0.0), EGPoint(80.0, 0.0)); as=:arrow)\n\nl = EGLine(EGPoint(0.0, -60.0), EGPoint(0.0, 60.0))\npath(l; extend=0.0, as=:arrow, arrowheadlength=15)   # extend=0.0: the exact finite segment\n\nThis is the one case where path doesn't just add to the current path: Luxor's arrow always strokes the shaft and fills the arrowhead immediately, with no deferred form, so action is ignored when as=:arrow. Keyword arguments other than as/extend (arrowheadlength, arrowheadangle, linewidth, ...) are forwarded straight to Luxor.arrow.","title":"Arrows"},{"category":"section","location":"drawing/#EGAngle2:-rays,-arc,-or-sector","page":"Drawing with Luxor.jl","text":"An EGAngle2 is genuinely just the space between two rays — but it's conventionally drawn as a small arc, or a filled wedge. as picks which:\n\nang = EGAngle2(t[2], t[1], t[3])   # the angle at vertex t[2]\n\npath(ang; as=:rays, action=:stroke)   # the literal two half-lines, a-vertex-b\npath(ang; as=:arc, action=:stroke)    # the conventional small arc (default)\npath(ang; as=:sector, action=:fill)   # closed pie-wedge, for shading\n\nradius defaults to 0.15 times the shorter of the distances from the vertex to ang.a and ang.b, so it looks reasonable at the figure's own scale without having to think about it — pass it explicitly to override. as=:arc and as=:sector are, in fact, nothing more than path(EGCircularArc2(...)) and path(EGCircularSector2(...)) under the hood (see below) — EGAngle2 just works out the right circle and endpoints first.","title":"EGAngle2: rays, arc, or sector"},{"category":"section","location":"drawing/#Circular-arcs,-sectors,-segments,-interstices-and-curvilinear-polygons","page":"Drawing with Luxor.jl","text":"EGCircularArc2, EGCircularSector2, EGCircularSegment2, EGAnnularSector2 and EGInterstice2 (see Circles, Tangency & Apollonius Problems) draw exactly like everything else — all through the same generic path(pg::EGPolygon) method described above:\n\ncirc = EGCircle2(EGPoint(0.0, 0.0), 30.0)\narc = EGCircularArc2(circ, EGPoint(30.0, 0.0), EGPoint(0.0, 30.0))\npath(arc; action=:stroke)\n\nsethue(\"steelblue\"); setopacity(0.4)\npath(EGCircularSector2(arc); action=:fill)   # the pie slice\npath(EGCircularSegment2(arc); action=:fill)  # the cap cut off by the chord\n\nc1 = EGCircle2(EGPoint(0.0, 0.0), 40.0)\nc2 = EGCircle2(EGPoint(90.0, 0.0), 50.0)   # tangent to c1: distance 90 == 40 + 50\nc3_center = intersection(EGCircle2(c1.center, c1.r + 35.0), EGCircle2(c2.center, c2.r + 35.0))[1]\nc3 = EGCircle2(c3_center, 35.0)            # tangent to both c1 and c2\n\nfor gap in interstices(c1, c2, c3)\n    sethue(\"red\"); setopacity(0.5)\n    path(gap; action=:fill)\nend\n\nsethue(\"purple\"); setopacity(0.5)\npath(invert(EGTriangle(EGPoint(50.0, 20.0), EGPoint(90.0, 30.0), EGPoint(60.0, 80.0)), EGPoint(0.0, 0.0)); action=:fill)\n\nEvery curved side here is built from Luxor's own arc2r/carc2r (Cairo's native circular-arc path primitive, driven by a center and the two endpoints) — never a sampled polyline standing in for the arc, the way EGParabola2/EGHyperbola2 above have to (Luxor has no native primitive for those curves, so sampling is the only option there).","title":"Circular arcs, sectors, segments, interstices and curvilinear polygons"},{"category":"section","location":"drawing/#Elliptic,-parabolic-and-hyperbolic-arcs","page":"Drawing with Luxor.jl","text":"EGEllipticArc2, EGParabolicArc2, EGHyperbolicArc2 (see Conics: Ellipse, Parabola & Hyperbola) draw the same way as EGCircularArc2, just sampled rather than a native Cairo primitive (like EGParabola2/EGHyperbola2 themselves):\n\ne = EGEllipse2(EGPoint(0.0, 0.0), 40.0, 20.0, pi / 6)\nearc = EGEllipticArc2(e, point_on_ellipse(e, 0.2), point_on_ellipse(e, 2.0))\npath(earc; action=:stroke)\n\nThey can also turn up as a side of a curvilinear region — not from building one directly (there's no EGEllipticSector2), but as the result of an EGAffineMap applied to a circular-arc region, since a non-conformal map turns a circular arc elliptic:\n\nsec = EGCircularSector2(EGCircularArc2(EGCircle2(EGPoint(0.0, 0.0), 30.0), EGPoint(30.0, 0.0), EGPoint(0.0, 30.0)))\nskew = EGAffineMap(1.3, 0.4, -0.2, 0.9, 0.0, 0.0)\npath(skew(sec); action=:stroke)   # an EGCurvilinearTriangle2 with one elliptic-arc side\n\npath(::EGPolygon) handles this transparently — a side is drawn with arc2r if it's an EGCircularArc2, or sampled at n points (the same n path itself takes) if it's any of the other three arc types.","title":"Elliptic, parabolic and hyperbolic arcs"},{"category":"section","location":"drawing/#Worked-example:-the-package-logo","page":"Drawing with Luxor.jl","text":"(Image: EuclideanGeometry.jl logo)\n\nThe logo is a Steiner chain: K1 and K2 are two circles internally tangent to a big circle K (and externally tangent to each other), and the chain is every further circle also tangent to both K and K2, each one also tangent to the previous. Rather than re-solving the 3-circle Apollonius problem (up to 8 candidate solutions) at every step of the chain, this inverts about the point where K and K2 touch: both become parallel lines, and in that inverted picture the whole chain collapses to a trivial row of equal circles translated along them — invert each one back and the real, naturally-shrinking chain falls out, one invert per new circle instead of a full Apollonius solve. delta_r then perturbs each circle's own already-inverted (real-space) radius by a constant amount before it's kept — a negative value shrinks every circle a bit further, opening up the gaps between them, which is what gives the logo its current look; it has to be applied there and not inside the inverted-space construction, since that construction only produces a valid chain when every one of those circles shares exactly the same radius. r_min stops the chain once a circle's own (already-perturbed) radius would no longer be a sensible circle to draw. reflection across the radial axis through K1 then gives the mirror-image chain trailing the other way, and three rotate copies (paired with Luxor.julia_green/julia_purple/julia_red, in that order) complete the 3-fold symmetric figure. A is placed at angle 270°, not 90°, because Luxor's y-axis points downward (screen coordinates), so 270° is the direction that reads as \"up\" once drawn:\n\nusing EuclideanGeometry\nusing Luxor: Drawing, origin, sethue, finish\nimport Luxor\n\ndelta_r = -2.0    # subtracted from each circle's own real-space radius\nr_min = 0.01      # stops the chain once that radius is no longer usable\nmax_circles = 100\n\nA = polar_point_deg(100.0, 270.0, EGPoint(0.0, 0.0))\nB = polar_point_deg(100.0, 30.0, EGPoint(0.0, 0.0))\ndiametro = distance(A, B)\nK1 = EGCircle2(A, diametro / 2)\nK2 = EGCircle2(B, diametro / 2)\nK = EGCircle2(EGPoint(0.0, 0.0), 100.0 + diametro / 2)\n\nT = K.center + K.r * (K2.center - K.center) / distance(K.center, K2.center)\nk_inv = diametro\nu = direction(invert(K, T; k=k_inv))\nu = u / norm(u)\n\nprev_inv = invert(K1, T; k=k_inv)\nstep = 2 * prev_inv.r\n\nV = [EGCircle2(K1.center, K1.r + delta_r)]\nfor _ in 1:max_circles\n    global prev_inv\n    prev_inv = EGCircle2(prev_inv.center + step * u, prev_inv.r)\n    real_circle = invert(prev_inv, T; k=k_inv)\n    r = real_circle.r + delta_r\n    r <= r_min && break\n    push!(V, EGCircle2(real_circle.center, r))\nend\n\nV = reflection.(V, Ref(EGLine(K.center, A)))\n\nDrawing(400, 400, \"logo.svg\")\norigin()\n\ncolors = (Luxor.julia_green, Luxor.julia_purple, Luxor.julia_red)\nfor (i, color) in enumerate(colors)\n    sethue(color)\n    path.(rotate.(V, deg2rad((i - 1) * 120)); action=:fill)\nend\n\nfinish()","title":"Worked example: the package logo"},{"category":"section","location":"drawing/#Name-collisions-with-Luxor","page":"Drawing with Luxor.jl","text":"Every EG-prefixed type in this package (EGPoint, EGCircle2, EGBoundingBox, ...) is safe to use unqualified alongside Luxor, precisely because the EG prefix keeps it out of Luxor's own namespace. What still collides is a handful of function names both packages independently export: midpoint, distance and rotate. Plain using EuclideanGeometry, Luxor still works (that's exactly what every example on this page does), but calling any of those three names unqualified is ambiguous and throws an UndefVarError pointing out the clash, rather than silently picking one:\n\njulia> using EuclideanGeometry, Luxor\n\njulia> midpoint(EGPoint(0.0, 0.0), EGPoint(4.0, 4.0))\nERROR: UndefVarError: `midpoint` not defined in `Main`\nHint: It looks like two or more modules export different bindings with this name, resulting in ambiguity. Try explicitly importing it from a particular module, or qualifying the name with the module it should come from.\nHint: a global variable of this name also exists in EuclideanGeometry.\nHint: a global variable of this name also exists in Luxor.\n\nTwo ways to resolve it, depending on which package's version you mean at that call site:\n\nQualify it explicitly: EuclideanGeometry.midpoint(...) or Luxor.rotate(...).\nIn your own scripts (not needed just to follow this page), prefer using EuclideanGeometry together with import Luxor instead of using Luxor — then only EuclideanGeometry's bindings are unqualified, and every Luxor call is written as Luxor.something, which sidesteps the ambiguity entirely rather than resolving it case by case. This is the convention this package's own test suite uses internally.\nOr the other way around: plain using EuclideanGeometry (every one of its names unqualified, colliding or not), plus using Luxor: f1, f2, ... naming only the handful of Luxor functions actually called, instead of blanket using Luxor — since distance/rotate are then never brought in from Luxor at all, there's no collision left to resolve. Add a plain import Luxor alongside so Luxor.something (e.g. Luxor.julia_green) still works for anything not explicitly named. This is what the logo example above does: using Luxor: Drawing, origin, sethue, finish for the few Luxor calls it makes, EuclideanGeometry dominant and unqualified everywhere else.","title":"Name collisions with Luxor"}]
}
